hadiths[600]{hadithnumber,text}:
"1","Lineage and name of Abu Bakr al-Siddiq (may Allah be pleased with him): Abu Bakr Abdullah ibn Uthman ibn Amir ibn Amr ibn Kab ibn Sad ibn Taym ibn Murra. Narrated Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Khalid al-Harrani from his father from Ibn Lahia from Abu al-Aswad from Urwa who said: Abu Bakr al-Siddiq's name is Abdullah ibn Uthman ibn Amir ibn Amr ibn Kab ibn Sad ibn Taym ibn Murra; he attended Badr with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). His mother was Umm al-Khayr Salma bint Sakhr ibn Amir ibn Amr ibn Kab ibn Sad ibn Taym ibn Murra ibn Kab ibn Luayy ibn Ghalib ibn Fihr ibn Malik; her mother was Dilaf, Umaima bint Ubayd ibn al-Naqid al-Khuzai; his grandmother, mother of Abu Quhafa, was Amina bint Abd al-Uzza ibn Harthan ibn Awf ibn Ubayd ibn Uwayj ibn Adi ibn Kab."
"2","Narrated Muhammad ibn Ali al-Madini al-Baghdadi from Dawud ibn Rushayd from al-Haytham ibn Adi who said: The mother of Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) was called Umm al-Khayr bint Sakhr ibn Amir. Abu Bakr died and both his parents inherited from him, and both had embraced Islam; his mother died before his father."
"3","Narrated Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr al-Muqaddami from Abdullah ibn Shabib al-Madani from Ibrahim ibn Yahya ibn Hani al-Shajari from his father from Khazim ibn al-Husayn from Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr from al-Zuhri from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah ibn Utba from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: The mothers of Abu Bakr, Uthman, Talha, al-Zubayr, Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, and Ammar ibn Yasir (may Allah be pleased with them) embraced Islam. It is said he was Atiq ibn Uthman, and he was called Atiq for the beauty of his face."
"4","Narrated Ahmad ibn al-Mualla al-Dimashqi from Hisham ibn Khalid from Damra ibn Rabia from al-Layth ibn Sad who said: Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) was called Atiq for the beauty of his face, and his name was Abdullah ibn Uthman."
"5","Narrated Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Sadaqa: I heard Abu Hafs Amr ibn Ali say: Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) was thin-faced, and he was called Atiq for the comeliness of his face. His name was Abdullah ibn Uthman. It was reported that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) named him Atiq, freed from the Fire."
"6","Narrated Abu al-Zinba Ruh ibn al-Faraj al-Misri from Yahya ibn Bukayr from Qays ibn Abi Qays al-Bukhari from Qutayba ibn Said from Ibn Lahia from Umara ibn Ghaziyya from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim from his father who said: I asked Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) about Abu Bakr's name, and she said: Abdullah. I said: They say Atiq. She said: Abu Quhafa had three sons, and he named one Atiq, one Muaytiq, and one Mutaaq."
"7","Narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari from Hamid ibn Yahya al-Balkhi from Sufyan ibn Uyayna from Ziyad ibn Sad from Amir ibn Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr from his father who said: Abu Bakr's name was Abdullah ibn Uthman, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) named him Atiq, freed from the Fire."
"8","Narrated al-Miqdam ibn Dawud al-Misri from Habib ibn Ruziq from Abdullah ibn Amir al-Aslami from Amr ibn Sulaym who said: I heard Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr say: The name of Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) was Abdullah ibn Uthman."
"9","Narrated Abdullah ibn Muhammad al-Umari the judge from Ismail ibn Abi Uways from Ishaq ibn Yahya ibn Talha from his uncle Ishaq ibn Talha who said: I entered upon Aisha, Mother of the Believers (may Allah be pleased with her), and she said: Abu Bakr entered upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said to him: You are freed from the Fire; from that day he was called Atiq."
"10","Narrated Muhammad ibn Ali al-Saigh al-Makki and Abu Zura Abd al-Rahman ibn Amr al-Dimashqi from Said ibn Mansur from Salih ibn Musa al-Talhi from Muawiya ibn Ishaq from Aisha bint Talha from Aisha, Mother of the Believers, that Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with them both) passed by the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: Whoever wishes to look at one freed from the Fire, let him look at this. She said: His family name for him was Abdullah ibn Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him)."
"11","Narrated Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shayba from Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim ibn Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr from Musa ibn Uqba who said: We know no four men with their sons who all met the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except these four: Abu Quhafa, Abu Bakr, Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakr, and Abu Atiq ibn Abd al-Rahman; Abu Atiq's name is Muhammad (may Allah be pleased with them)."
"12","Narrated Muttalib ibn Shuayb al-Azdi from Abdullah ibn Salih from al-Layth ibn Sad from Khalid ibn Yazid from Said ibn Abi Hilal from Rabia ibn Sayf from Shufayy al-Asbahi: I heard Abdullah ibn Amr say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: There will be twelve caliphs after me: Abu Bakr al-Siddiq will not remain after me but a little; and the owner of the millstone of the Arabs' abode will live praised and die martyred. It was asked: Who is he, Messenger of Allah? He said: Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him). Then he turned to Uthman and said: People will ask you to remove a shirt Allah has clothed you with; by Him in whose hand is my soul, if you remove it you will not enter Paradise until the camel passes through the eye of the needle."
"13","Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz from Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal, and al-Miqdam from Asad ibn Musa, from Hammad ibn Salama from Said ibn Jumhan from Safina, freedman of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: The caliphate is thirty years, then there will be kingship. Abu Bakr held two, Umar ten, Uthman twelve, Ali six (may Allah be pleased with them)."
"14","Narrated Muadh ibn al-Muthanna from Ali ibn al-Madini from Ishaq ibn Mansur al-Saluli from Muhammad ibn Sulayman al-Abdi from Harun ibn Sad from Imran ibn Zibyan from Abu Yahya Hakim ibn Sad who said: I heard Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) swear: Allah sent down Abu Bakr's name from heaven as al-Siddiq."
"15","Narrated Buhlul ibn Ishaq ibn Buhlul al-Anbari from his father from Abd al-Ala ibn Abi al-Musawir from Ikrima from Umm Hani who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, when he was taken by night: I wish to go out to Quraysh and tell them; so he told them and they denied him, while Abu Bakr affirmed him, and he was named al-Siddiq that day."
"16","Description of Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him). Narrated Tahir ibn Isa ibn Qiras al-Misri from Said ibn Abi Maryam from Ibn Lahia from al-Harith ibn Yazid from Ulayy ibn Rabah that Abdullah ibn Amr (may Allah be pleased with him) said: Three men of Quraysh with the brightest faces, finest character, and firmest modesty: if they tell you they do not lie to you, and if you tell them they do not call you liar: Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, Abu Ubayda ibn al-Jarrah, and Uthman ibn Affan (may Allah be pleased with them)."
"17","Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz from Arim Abu al-Numan from Hammad ibn Zayd from Thabit from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) that Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) dyed with henna and katam."
"18","Narrated Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari from Abd al-Razzaq from Mamar from Qatada and Thabit from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) that Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) dyed his beard with henna and katam, and Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) dyed with henna alone."
"19","Narrated Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal from al-Abbas ibn al-Walid al-Narsi from Yazid ibn Zuray from Said from Qatada from Anas, the same."
"20","Narrated Muhammad ibn Abdus ibn Kamil from Ali ibn al-Jad from Shuba from Humayd from Anas that Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) used to dye with henna and katam, and Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) used to dye with henna."
"21","Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz from al-Zubayr ibn Bakkar from Ibrahim ibn al-Mundhir from al-Waqidi from Shuayb ibn Talha ibn Abdullah ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakr al-Siddiq (may Allah be pleased with him) from his father from Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) that she saw a man passing while she was in her howdah and said: I never saw a man more like Abu Bakr than this. She was asked: Describe Abu Bakr to us. She said: He was a white, thin man with light sideburns, stooped, whose waist-wrapper would not stay up but slipped from his hips, emaciated in the face, deep-eyed, prominent-foreheaded, bare-knuckled; that is his description."
"22","Narrated Bishr ibn Musa from Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Muqri from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad ibn Anum from Umara ibn Ghurab from his uncle who said: I saw Abu Bakr al-Siddiq when he was the caliph of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with a red beard, deeply red."
"23","Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz from Abu Nuaym from Misar from Ziyad ibn Ilaqa who said: Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) used to dye with henna."
"24","Narrated Fudayl ibn Muhammad al-Malti from Abu Nuaym from Misar from Abi Awn from a man of Banu Asad who said: I saw Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) in the expedition of Dhat al-Salasil, his beard as if the flame of the arfaj shrub, upon a light white dusky she-camel of his."
"25","Narrated al-Qasim ibn Abbad al-Khattabi al-Basri from Muhammad ibn Sulayman Luwayn from Sufyan ibn Uyayna from Ismail from Qays who said: Muawiya (may Allah be pleased with him) said: I entered with my father upon Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him), and I saw Asma standing over his head, fair, and I saw Abu Bakr white and thin; then he mounted me and my father on two horses, presented us to him, and gave us gifts."
"26","The age, sermon, and death of Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him). Narrated Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari from Abd al-Razzaq from Ibn Jurayj from Ibn Shihab from Urwa from Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) died at the head of sixty-three. Ibn Shihab said: And Aisha said: And Abu Bakr died at the head of sixty-three."
"27","Narrated Muttalib ibn Shuayb al-Azdi from Abdullah ibn Salih from al-Layth ibn Sad from Uqayl from Ibn Shihab from Urwa from Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) died aged sixty-three."
"28","Narrated Yahya ibn Uthman ibn Salih from Said ibn Abi Maryam from Ibn Lahia from Abu al-Aswad from Urwa from Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) mentioned their births in my presence; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was older than Abu Bakr. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) died aged sixty-three, and Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) died aged sixty-three, after the two and a half years he lived after the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"29","Narrated Bishr ibn Musa from Khalaf ibn al-Walid from Israil from Abi Ishaq from Amir ibn Sad from Jarir ibn Abdullah al-Bajali who said: I was with Muawiya ibn Abi Sufyan (may Allah be pleased with them) and heard him say: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was taken aged sixty-three, Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) was taken aged sixty-three, and Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) was taken aged sixty-three. Abu Ishaq said: And Muawiya (may Allah be pleased with him) said: And mine is fifty-seven; then he lived about twenty years more."
"30","Narrated Abu Zura al-Dimashqi from Abu Nuaym from Yunus ibn Abi Ishaq from Abu al-Safar from al-Shaabi from Jarir from Muawiya, the same."
"31","Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz from Asim ibn Ali from Abu al-Ahwas from Abi Ishaq from Amir ibn Sad who said: Jarir narrated to me from Muawiya (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) died aged sixty-three."
"32","Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz from Muslim ibn Ibrahim from Shuba from Abi Ishaq from Amir ibn Sad from Muawiya (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: Abu Bakr was taken aged sixty-three."
"33","Narrated Muadh ibn al-Muthanna from Musaddad from Umayya ibn Khalid from Shuba from Abi Ishaq from Amir ibn Sad from Jarir from Muawiya (may Allah be pleased with him), the same."
"34","Narrated Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari from Abd al-Razzaq from Ismail ibn Abdullah from Dawud ibn Abi Hind from al-Shaabi who said: Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) died aged sixty-three."
"35","Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz from al-Zubayr ibn Bakkar from Abu Damra from Yahya ibn Said from Said ibn al-Musayyib who said: Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) died aged sixty-three; Abu Bakr ruled two years, was buried at night, and Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) prayed over him."
"36","Narrated Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari from Abd al-Razzaq from Ibn Jurayj from Abu al-Huwayrith from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) died aged sixty-five, and Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) at the same standing."
"37","Narrated Amr ibn Abi al-Tahir ibn al-Sarh al-Misri from Yusuf ibn Adi al-Kufi from Abu al-Ahwas from al-Agharr Abi Malik who said: When Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) wished to appoint Umar as successor, he sent for him and called him, and he came. He said: I call you to an affair tiring for whoever undertakes it; so fear Allah, Umar, through obeying Him, and obey Him through fearing Him, for the God-fearing is safe and guarded. The affair is laid open; none deserves it but one who acts by it. Whoever commands truth and acts falsehood, commands right and acts wrong, his hopes are like to be cut off and his deeds void. If you are put in charge of their affair, if you can keep your hand dry of their blood, your belly lean of their wealth, and your tongue dry of their honor, then do so; and there is no strength but through Allah."
"38","Narrated Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Hadrami from Harun ibn Musa al-Farwi from Musa ibn Abdullah ibn Hasan ibn Hasan ibn Ali ibn Abi Talib from his father from his grandfather al-Hasan ibn Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: When Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) was dying, he said: O Aisha, look at the milch-camel whose milk we drank, the bowl we ate breakfast from, and the cloak we wore, for we benefited from that while we were in charge of the Muslims' affair; when I die, return it to Umar. When Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) died, she sent it to Umar (may Allah be pleased with him), and Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: May Allah be pleased with you, Abu Bakr, you have tired whoever comes after you."
"39","Narrated Ahmad ibn Abd al-Wahhab ibn Najda al-Hawti from Abu al-Mughira from Hariz ibn Uthman from Nuaym ibn Namhama who said: In the sermon of Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) was: Do you not know that you go morning and evening toward a known term; whoever can have his term end while he is in Allah's work (mighty and majestic), let him do so; you will not attain that but through Allah (mighty and majestic). A people made their terms for others, and Allah forbade you to be like them: And be not like those who forgot Allah so He made them forget. Where are your brothers whom you know? They sent forth what they sent forth in their former days, and settled therein in misery or felicity. Where are the first tyrants who built cities and ringed them with walls; they have become beneath rocks and wells. This is Allah's Book (mighty and majestic), its wonders never end; so take counsel from it for a day of darkness, and enlighten yourselves by its light and exposition. Allah (mighty and majestic) praised Zakariya and his household, saying: They hastened in good deeds and called upon Us in hope and fear, and were humble to Us. No good in speech not sought thereby Allah's face, no good in wealth not spent in Allah's path, no good in one whose ignorance overcomes his forbearance, no good in one who in Allah's cause fears the blame of a blamer."
"40","Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz from Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal from Hammad ibn Salama from Hisham ibn Urwa from his father from Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) who said: Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) died on Tuesday night, and was buried at night."
"41","Narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari from Hisham ibn Ammar from al-Haytham ibn Imran who said: I heard my grandfather say: Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) died with a touch of consumption, and he ruled two and a half years."
"42","Narrated Abu al-Zinba Ruh ibn al-Faraj from Yahya ibn Bukayr who said: Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) was made caliph on the day the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) died, and Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) died in Jumada al-Akhira in the year thirteen; his age when he died, as Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) mentioned, was the age of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): sixty-three."
"43","And of what Abu Bakr al-Siddiq (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Narrated Abu al-Zinba Ruh ibn al-Faraj al-Misri from Said ibn Ufayr from Ulwan ibn Dawud al-Bajali from Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf from Salih ibn Kaysan from Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf from his father who said: I entered upon Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) visiting him in his illness in which he died; I greeted him and asked how he was. He sat up and I said: By Allah's praise you are recovered. He said: Yet I am in pain as you see, and you have given me work along with my pain. I made for you a covenant after me, and chose for you the best of you in my view, yet each of you swelled his nose at that hoping the affair would be his. I see the world has come, though not yet fully, yet it is coming; you will adorn your houses with silk curtains and brocade cushions, and scorn woolen Azeri bedding as if one of you were upon the thorns of sadan. By Allah, for one of you to be brought forward and have his neck struck, without a hadd, is better for him than to wander in the flood of the world. Then he said: Yet I grieve over nothing but three things I did I wish I had not done, three I did not do I wish I had done, and three I wish I had asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about. As for the three I wish I had not done: I wish I had not uncovered Fatima's house and had left it, though war were locked upon me; I wish on the day of the Saqifa of Banu Saida I had cast the affair upon the neck of one of the two men, Abu Ubayda or Umar, so he would be Commander of the Believers and I minister; I wish when I directed Khalid ibn al-Walid to the apostates I had stayed at Dhu al-Qassa: if the Muslims triumphed they triumphed, else I would be a rear or reinforcement. As for those I wish I had done: I wish when the Ashath was brought captive I had struck his neck, for it seems to me no evil comes but he flies to it; I wish when al-Fujaa al-Sulami was brought I had not burned him but killed him swiftly or freed him safely; I wish when I directed Khalid ibn al-Walid to Syria I had directed Umar to Iraq, so I would have stretched my right and left hands in Allah's path (mighty and majestic). As for the three I wish I had asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about: I wish I had asked him who holds this affair, so its people would not dispute it; I wish I had asked him whether the Ansar have a share in this affair; I wish I had asked him about the paternal aunt and the brother's daughter, for in my soul concerning them is a need."
"44","Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz from Abu Ghassan Malik ibn Ismail al-Nahdi from Abd al-Rahman ibn Humayd al-Rawasibi from Sulayman al-Amash from Ismail ibn Raja from Umayr, freedman of Ibn Abbas, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: Ali and al-Abbas disputed to Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with them) over the inheritance of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: I would not move it from the place where the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) placed it."
"45","Narrated al-Abbas ibn al-Fadl al-Asfati from Abd al-Jabbar ibn Said al-Masahqi from Yahya ibn Muhammad ibn Abbad ibn Hani al-Shajari from Hisham ibn Sad from Said ibn Abi Hilal from Abi Qabil from Abdullah ibn Amr ibn al-As (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) wrote to Amr ibn al-As that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said concerning the Ansar: Accept from their doers of good and overlook their wrongdoers."
"46","Narrated al-Abbas ibn al-Fadl al-Asfati from Abd al-Jabbar ibn Said al-Masahqi from Yahya ibn Muhammad al-Shajari from Hisham ibn Sad from Said ibn Abi Hilal from Abi Qabil from Abdullah ibn Amr (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) wrote to Amr ibn al-As that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) consulted concerning the matter of war, so hold to it."
"47","Narrated Abu Zayd al-Hawti from Abu al-Yaman from Attaf ibn Khalid from Talha ibn Abdullah ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakr from his father who said: I heard my father say: I heard Abu Bakr al-Siddiq (may Allah be pleased with him) say: I said: Messenger of Allah, do we act upon a matter already finished or upon a new matter? He said: Upon a matter already finished. I said: Then what are deeds for, Messenger of Allah? He said: Each is facilitated to what he was created for."
"48","The lineage of Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him). Narrated Abu al-Zinba Ruh ibn al-Faraj from Amr ibn Khalid al-Harrani from Yaqub ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Muhammad ibn Abdullah from Musa ibn Uqba from Ibn Shihab who said: Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz said to Abu Bakr ibn Sulayman ibn Abi Hathma: Who first wrote from Abdullah, Commander of the Believers? He said: Al-Shifa bint Abdullah, who was among the first emigrant women, told me that Labid ibn Rabia and Adi ibn Hatim came to Medina and went to the mosque and found Amr ibn al-As. They said: Ibn al-As, seek permission for us to see the Commander of the Believers. He said: By Allah, you have hit his name; he is the commander and we are the believers. Amr entered upon Umar and said: Peace upon you, Commander of the Believers. Umar said to him: What is this? He said: You are the commander and we are the believers; and writing ran from that day."
"49","Narrated Ahmad ibn Abd al-Rahim al-Barqi from Abd al-Malik ibn Hisham from Ziyad ibn Abdullah from Ibn Ishaq who said: Umar ibn al-Khattab ibn Nufayl ibn Abd al-Uzza ibn Rabah ibn Abdullah ibn Qurt ibn Razah ibn Adi ibn Kab ibn Luayy ibn Ghalib ibn Fihr ibn Malik, kunya Abu Hafs (may Allah be pleased with him); his mother was Hantama bint Hisham ibn al-Mughira ibn Abdullah ibn Amr ibn Makhzum, and Hantama's mother was al-Shifa bint Abd Qays ibn Adi ibn Said ibn Sahm ibn Amr ibn Husays ibn Kab ibn Luayy. And Abu Usama al-Halabi narrated it to us from al-Hajjaj ibn Abi Mani al-Rusafi from his grandfather Ubaydullah ibn Ziyad from al-Zuhri."
"50","Description of Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him). Reported Abu Yazid al-Qaratis i from Asad ibn Musa from Mubarak ibn Fadala from al-Hasan from al-Aswad ibn Sari who said: I used to recite to him, meaning the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and did not know his companions, until a man came, wide between the shoulders, bald. I was told: Be silent, be silent. I said: Alas, who is this for whom I must be silent before the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? I was told: Umar ibn al-Khattab."
"51","Narrated Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari from Abd al-Razzaq from Mamar from Asim ibn Abi al-Nujud from Zirr ibn Hubaysh who said: The people of Medina went out in a gathering of theirs, and I saw a bald man, left-handed, ambidextrous, towering over the people by a forearm, wearing a coarse waist-wrapper and a Qatari cloak, saying: O people, emigrate and do not be Bedouins; let none of you throw at the rabbit with his stick or stone and then eat it; let spears and arrows slaughter for you."
"52","Narrated Yahya ibn Ayyub al-Allaf al-Misri from Said ibn Abi Maryam from Yahya ibn Ayyub from Yahya ibn Said from Said ibn al-Musayyib who said: Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) was bald, severely bald."
"53","Narrated Abu Yazid al-Qaratis i from Asad ibn Musa from Israil from Abi Ishaq from Abi al-Ahwas and Abi Ubayda from Abdullah who said: Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) mounted a horse and galloped it, his thigh was uncovered, and the people of Najran saw a black mole on his thigh and said: This is the one we find in our book who will expel us from our land."
"54","Narrated Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal from his father from Ishaq ibn Isa al-Tabba who said: I saw Malik ibn Anas with a full mustache, and asked him about it. He said: Zayd ibn Aslam narrated to me from Amir ibn Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr that Umar ibn al-Khattab when angry would twist his mustache and blow."
"55","Narrated Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi from Asim ibn Ali from Abdullah ibn Umar from Zayd ibn Aslam from his father who said: Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) would take hold of his ear, meaning his own ear, then leap onto the horse."
"56","Narrated Musa ibn Isa ibn al-Mundhir al-Himsi from Haywa ibn Shurayh al-Himsi from Baqiyya ibn al-Walid from Bahir ibn Sad from Khalid ibn Madan from Abd al-Rahman ibn Amr al-Sulami that a freedwoman of Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) offered to dye his beard, and he said: Do you wish to extinguish my light as so-and-so extinguished his light."
"57","Narrated Bakr ibn Sahl from Abdullah ibn Yusuf from Muhammad ibn Himyar from Thabit ibn Ajlan who said: I heard Abu Amir Sulaym ibn Amir say: I saw Umar not changing anything of his beard."
"58","Narrated Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Irq al-Himsi from Muhammad ibn al-Musaffa from Suwayd ibn Abd al-Aziz from Thabit ibn Ajlan from Mujahid from Ibn Umar that Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) would not dye his gray hair. It was said: Commander of the Believers, will you not dye, though Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) used to dye? Umar said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever grows a gray hair in Islam, it will be light for him on the Day of Resurrection; and I shall not dye my gray."
"59","Narrated Uthman ibn Umar al-Dabbi from Amr ibn Marzuq from Shuba from Asim from Zirr who said: I was in Medina and saw a dark, left-handed, ambidextrous, bulky man towering over the people as if upon a mount, and he was Umar (may Allah be pleased with him)."
"60","Narrated Yusuf al-Qadi from Amr ibn Marzuq from Shuba from Simak ibn Harb from Abdullah ibn Hilal who said: I saw Umar (may Allah be pleased with him), a bulky man, as if among the men of Banu Sadus."
"61","The age and death of Umar, with disagreement on his age (may Allah be pleased with him). Narrated Ahmad ibn Dawud al-Makki from Habib, secretary of Malik, from the nephew of al-Zuhri from al-Zuhri from Said ibn al-Musayyib from Ubayy ibn Kab (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Jibril (peace be upon him) said to me: Let Islam weep over Umar's death (may Allah be pleased with him)."
"62","Narrated Muhammad ibn Uthman ibn Abi Shayba from my uncle Abu Bakr from Abdullah ibn Idris from Layth from Maruf ibn Abi Maruf al-Mawsili who said: When Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) was struck, I heard a voice: [truncated in source, transmitted literally as an incomplete utterance]."
"63","Narrated Muttalib ibn Shuayb al-Azdi from Abdullah ibn Salih from al-Layth from Ubaydullah ibn Umar from al-Zuhri from al-Miswar ibn Makhrama who said: Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) ruled ten years, then died."
"64","Narrated Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari from Abd al-Razzaq from Ibn Jurayj from Abu al-Huwayrith from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) that Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) died aged sixty-six."
"65","Narrated Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal from his father from Hushaym from Dawud from al-Shaabi that Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) died aged sixty-three."
"66","Narrated Abu Zura al-Dimashqi from Abu Nuaym from Yunus ibn Abi Ishaq from Abu al-Safar from al-Shaabi from Jarir from Muawiya (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was taken aged sixty-three, Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) was taken aged sixty-three, and Umar was taken aged sixty-three."
"67","Narrated Ubayd ibn Ghannam from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shayba from al-Hasan ibn Musa from Abu Hilal from Qatada who said: Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) was killed aged sixty-one."
"68","Narrated Ishaq al-Dabari from Abd al-Razzaq from Ibn Jurayj from Ibn Shihab who said: Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) died at the head of fifty-five."
"69","Narrated Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal from his father from Hushaym from Ali ibn Zayd from Salim ibn Abdullah that Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) was taken aged fifty-five."
"70","Narrated Muhammad ibn Nasr al-Saigh from Ibrahim ibn Hamza al-Zubayri from Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad from Ubaydullah ibn Umar from Nafi from Ibn Umar who said: Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) died aged fifty-five."
"71","Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz from Muslim ibn Ibrahim from Jarir ibn Hazim from Nafi from Ibn Umar who said: Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) died aged fifty-five. And he said: Gray came to me early from my maternal uncles, Banu al-Mughira."
"72","Narrated Muttalib ibn Shuayb al-Azdi from Abdullah ibn Salih from Rishdin ibn Sad from Abu Yusuf al-Harith ibn Yusuf al-Ansari of Banu al-Harith ibn al-Khazraj from Sahl ibn Sad al-Ansari who said: Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) was buried on Wednesday, with four nights left of Dhu al-Hijja, in the year twenty-three."
"73","Narrated Abu al-Zinba Ruh ibn al-Faraj from Yahya ibn Bukayr who said: Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) was made caliph in Rajab in the year thirteen, and was killed at the end of Dhu al-Hijja in the year twenty-three; he remained three days after the stabbing, then died at the end of Dhu al-Hijja. Suhayb prayed over him, his son Abdullah ibn Umar washed him, he was shrouded in five garments, and buried with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He was stabbed on Wednesday with nine nights left of Dhu al-Hijja. Some people said: He died the same day. His age when he died, as I heard Malik ibn Anas mention, reached the age of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): sixty-three; some say fifty-nine, some say fifty-three and fifty-five, some say fifty-four; his caliphate was ten years, four months and days."
"74","Narrated Abu al-Zinba from Yahya ibn Bukayr from al-Layth ibn Sad who said: The Commander of the Believers Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) was killed at the return of the pilgrims, in the year twenty-three."
"75","Narrated Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Sadaqa from Abu Hafs Amr ibn Ali who said: It is said Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) was killed aged sixty-three, and the established view is that he was fifty-eight."
"76","Narrated Ubayd ibn Ghannam from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shayba who said: Umar died in the year twenty-three, and his caliphate was ten years."
"77","Narrated Yahya ibn Uthman ibn Salih from Amr ibn al-Rabi ibn Tariq from Yahya ibn Ayyub from Yunus from al-Zuhri from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah ibn Utba from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) that Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) was stabbed at dawn; Abu Lulua, the slave of al-Mughira ibn Shuba, stabbed him, and he was a Magian."
"78","Narrated Abd al-Rahman ibn Jabir ibn al-Buhturi al-Himsi from Bishr ibn Shuayb ibn Abi Hamza from his father from al-Zuhri from Salim from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: When Umar was stabbed they sent for a physician; an Ansari man came and gave him milk to drink, and the milk came out white from the stab below the navel. The physician said to him: Make your bequest, for I do not see you lasting till evening. He said: He told me the truth."
"79","Narrated al-Qasim ibn Zakariya al-Mutarriz from Yusuf ibn Musa al-Qattan from Salama ibn al-Fadl from Muhammad ibn Ishaq from my uncle Abd al-Rahman ibn Yasar who said: I witnessed the death of Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him), and the sun eclipsed that day."
"80","And what Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz from Mualla ibn Asad al-Ammi from Wuhayb from Ayyub from Nafi from Ibn Umar from Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) that he asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): May one of us sleep while junub? He said: Yes, and let him perform his ablution for prayer."
"81","Narrated Muadh ibn al-Muthanna from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Mubarak al-Ayshi from Wuhayb from Ayyub from Nafi from Ibn Umar from Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) heard me saying: By my father; so he said: Allah (mighty and majestic) forbids you to swear by your fathers."
"82","Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz from Yunus ibn Ubaydullah al-Umayri from Mubarak ibn Fadala from Ubaydullah ibn Umar from Nafi from Ibn Umar from Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) that he said: O people, suspect opinion against religion; I saw myself rejecting the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) command by my opinion in exertion, yet by Allah I did not fall short of the truth; that was on the day of Abu Jandal, when the writ was between the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and the people of Mecca. He said: Write: In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate. They said: Do you see we have believed you in what you say? Rather you write: In Your name, O Allah. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) accepted, and I refused until the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: Do you see me accept while you refuse? He said: So I accepted."
"83","Narrated Abu Muslim al-Kashshi from Sulayman ibn Harb from Hammad ibn Zayd from Muhammad ibn Ishaq from Ubaydullah from Nafi from Ibn Umar who said: I said to my father: Who is the man who rescued you from the polytheists on the day they beat you? He said: That was al-As ibn Wail al-Sahmi."
"84","Narrated Abu Muslim al-Kashshi from Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Ansari from his father from Thumama ibn Abdullah ibn Anas from Anas that Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) went out to pray for rain, taking al-Abbas with him, saying: O Allah, when we suffered drought in the time of our Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) we sought nearness to You through our Prophet (peace be upon him), and now we seek nearness to You through the uncle of our Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and be pleased with him."
"85","Narrated al-Miqdam ibn Dawud from al-Nadr ibn Abd al-Jabbar from Ibn Lahia from Abi al-Nadr from Abdullah ibn Dinar who said: A man of Iraq said to Abdullah ibn Umar: Ibn Abbas, while our governor, said: Whoever is given a hundred dirhams for a dirham, let him take it. Ibn Umar said: I heard Umar ibn al-Khattab say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to say on the pulpit: Gold for gold is usury, except like for like, no increase; whatever exceeds is usury. Ibn Umar said: If you are in doubt, ask Abu Said al-Khudri about it. He went and asked Abu Said al-Khudri, who told him he heard that from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Ibn Abbas was told what Ibn Umar and Abu Said said, so Ibn Abbas sought forgiveness and said: This was an opinion I held."
"86","Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz from Abu Nuaym from Yunus ibn Abi Ishaq from Abi Ishaq from Abi Salama ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf who said: Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) said: I traveled with Sad; he urinated, performed ablution, wiped over his khuffs, then led the people. I objected to that, so he said: Are you content with your father? I said: Yes. We gathered before the Commander of the Believers, and Sad said to him: I urinated, then performed ablution and wiped over my khuffs, then prayed. He said: You did well and hit the Sunna. He said: Your son Abdullah objected to that. He said: O Sad, you were older than him and more knowing."
"87","Narrated Muhammad ibn al-Fadl al-Saqati from Abd al-Aziz ibn Abdullah al-Uwaysi from Yazid ibn Abd al-Malik al-Nawfali from Yazid ibn Khusayfa from al-Saib ibn Yazid from Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The price of a singing slave-girl is ill-gotten, her singing is unlawful, looking at her is unlawful, her price is like the price of a dog, the price of a dog is ill-gotten, and whoever's flesh grows on ill-gotten, the Fire is more deserving of him."
"88","Narrated Muhammad ibn al-Fadl al-Saqati from Abd al-Aziz ibn Abdullah from Yazid ibn Abd al-Malik from Yazid ibn Khusayfa from al-Saib ibn Yazid from Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) that when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wished to marry a woman among his women, he would come to her from behind the veil and say to her: Daughter, so-and-so has proposed to you; if you dislike him say No, for none is shy to say No, and if you like, your silence is consent."
"89","Narrated Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal from his father from Sufyan ibn Uyayna from al-Saab ibn Hakim ibn Sharik ibn Namla from his father from his grandfather who said: I was Umar ibn al-Khattab's (may Allah be pleased with him) guest one night; he fed me pieces of a cold camel's head, and fed us oil, saying: This is the blessed oil about which Allah (mighty and majestic) spoke to His Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"90","The lineage of Uthman ibn Affan (may Allah be pleased with him). Narrated Ahmad ibn Shahin from Musab ibn Abdullah al-Zubayri who said: Uthman ibn Affan ibn Abi al-As ibn Umayya ibn Abd Shams ibn Abd Manaf ibn Qusayy ibn Kilab ibn Murra ibn Kab ibn Luayy ibn Ghalib ibn Fihr, kunya Abu Amr, and it is said Abu Abdullah. Uthman's mother was Arwa bint Kurayz ibn Rabia ibn Habib ibn Abd Shams; Arwa's mother was Umm Hakim al-Bayda bint Abd al-Muttalib, paternal aunt of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); Umm Hakim's mother was Fatima bint Amr ibn Aidh ibn Imran ibn Makhzum, grandmother of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on his father's side."
"91","Narrated Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr al-Muqaddami the judge from Abdullah ibn Shabib al-Madani from Ibrahim ibn Yahya al-Shajari from his father from Khazim ibn Husayn from Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr from al-Zuhri from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah ibn Utba from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: The mother of Uthman (may Allah be pleased with them both), Arwa bint Kurayz, embraced Islam."
"92","Description and age of Uthman ibn Affan (may Allah be pleased with him). Narrated Abu Yazid Yusuf ibn Yazid al-Qaratis i from Asad ibn Musa from Ibn Lahia from Abu al-Aswad from Abdullah ibn Shaddad ibn al-Had who said: I saw Uthman ibn Affan (may Allah be pleased with him) on Friday on the pulpit, wearing a coarse Adani waist-wrapper worth four or five dirhams, and a striped Kufi wrap, stocky-fleshed, long-bearded, handsome-faced."
"93","Narrated al-Miqdam ibn Dawud from Khalid ibn Nizar from Ishaq ibn Yahya ibn Talha from his uncle Musa ibn Talha who said: Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him) on Friday would lean on a staff, and was the most beautiful of people, wearing two yellow garments, a waist-wrapper and cloak, until he came to the pulpit and sat upon it."
"94","Narrated Abu Yazid al-Qaratis i from Asad ibn Musa from al-Rabi ibn Badr from al-Jurayri from Abdullah ibn Hazm al-Mazini who said: I saw Uthman ibn Affan (may Allah be pleased with him); I never saw any male or female with a handsomer face than his."
"95","Narrated Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Abd al-Aziz al-Baghawi from Dawud ibn Rushayd from Salama ibn Bashir from Hujr ibn al-Harith al-Ghassani from Abdullah ibn Awf al-Qari who said: I saw Uthman ibn Affan (may Allah be pleased with him), white-bearded."
"96","Narrated al-Miqdam ibn Dawud from Asad ibn Musa from Ibn Abi Dhib from Abd al-Rahman ibn Sad who said: I saw Uthman ibn Affan (may Allah be pleased with him), yellow-bearded."
"97","Narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari from Abu al-Rabi al-Zahrani from Hammad ibn Zayd from a freedman of Uthman from Usama ibn Zayd who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent me to Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him) with a dish containing meat; I entered upon him while Ruqayya was sitting, and I never saw two more handsome than they (may Allah be pleased with them both). I kept looking at Ruqayya one moment and at Uthman another. When I returned, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: Did you enter upon them? I said: Yes. He said: Did you see a couple handsomer than they? I said: No, Messenger of Allah; I kept looking at Ruqayya one moment and at Uthman another. Abu al-Qasim, may Allah have mercy on him, said: This was before the revelation of the verse of the veil."
"98","Narrated Muhammad ibn Uthman ibn Abi Shayba from Ahmad ibn Yunus from Abd al-Malik ibn Abdullah, descendant of Qays ibn Makhrama ibn al-Muttalib, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Uthman al-Qurashi that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered upon his daughter while she was washing Uthman's head (may Allah be pleased with them both), and said: Daughter, treat Abu Abdullah well, for he is the most like my companions to me in character."
"99","Narrated Ali ibn Said al-Askari al-Razi from al-Khalil ibn Amr from Muhammad ibn Salama from Abi Abd al-Rahim from Zayd ibn Abi Unaysa from Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Abd al-Muttalib ibn Abdullah from Abu Hurayra (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: I entered upon Ruqayya bint the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), wife of Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him), with a comb in her hand. She said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) just left me; I had combed his head, and he said: How do you find Abu Abdullah? I said: Well. He said: Honor him, for he is among the most like my companions to me in character."
"100","The age and death of Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him). Narrated Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari from Abd al-Razzaq from Ibn al-Taymi, and Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz from Arim Abu al-Numan from Mutamir ibn Sulayman from his father from Abu Uthman al-Nahdi who said: Uthman ibn Affan (may Allah be pleased with him) was killed in the middle of the days of Tashriq."
"101","Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz: I heard al-Zubayr ibn Bakkar say: Uthman ibn Affan (may Allah be pleased with him) was killed on Friday, with eighteen nights past of Dhu al-Hijja, in the year thirty-six, after Asr, aged eighty-two; and he was fasting that day."
"102","Narrated al-Miqdam ibn Dawud al-Misri from Ali ibn Said al-Raqqi from Abdullah ibn Amr from Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Aqil who said: Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him) was killed in the year thirty-five."
"103","Narrated Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Irq al-Himsi from Muhammad ibn Musaffa from Baqiyya ibn al-Walid from Ubaydullah ibn Amr from Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Aqil who said: Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him) was killed in the year thirty-five."
"104","Narrated Ubayd ibn Ghannam from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shayba from al-Hasan ibn Musa from Abu Hilal from Qatada who said: Uthman was killed aged ninety or eighty-eight."
"105","Narrated Ubayd ibn Ghannam from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shayba who said: Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him) was killed in the year thirty-five."
"106","Narrated Muttalib ibn Shuayb al-Azdi from Abdullah ibn Salih from al-Layth from Abdullah ibn Umar from Ibn Shihab from al-Miswar ibn Makhrama who said: The caliphate of Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him) was twelve years."
"107","Narrated Abu al-Zinba Ruh ibn al-Faraj from Yahya ibn Bukayr who said: The consultation took place and the people agreed upon Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him) with three nights left of Dhu al-Hijja in the year twenty-three; then Uthman was killed on Friday with eighteen nights past of Dhu al-Hijja at the end of the year thirty-five; his age was eighty-eight, and he used to yellow his beard; Uthman's rule (may Allah be pleased with him) was twelve years."
"108","Narrated Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal from his father from al-Hasan ibn Musa from Abu Hilal from Qatada that Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him) was killed aged ninety or eighty-eight."
"109","Narrated Amr ibn Abi al-Tahir ibn al-Sarh al-Misri from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abdullah ibn Abd al-Hakam from Abd al-Malik al-Majishun who said: I heard Malik say: Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him) was killed and lay cast upon the rubbish heap of Banu so-and-so for three days. Twelve men came to him, among them my grandfather Malik ibn Abi Amir, Huwaytib ibn Abd al-Uzza, Hakim ibn Hizam, Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr, and Aisha bint Uthman, with a lamp in a bag; they carried him upon a door, his head knocking upon the door, knock knock, until they brought him to al-Baqi. They differed over praying over him; Hakim ibn Hizam or Huwaytib ibn Abd al-Uzza prayed over him, Abd al-Rahman doubted. Then they wished to bury him, and a man of Banu Mazin stood and said: By Allah, if you bury him with the Muslims I shall tell the people. So they carried him until they brought him to Hashsh Kawkab. When they lowered him into his grave, Aisha bint Uthman cried out, and Ibn al-Zubayr said to her: Be silent; by Allah, if you repeat I shall strike that wherein are your eyes. When they buried him and leveled the earth over him, Ibn al-Zubayr said to her: Cry as you wish to cry. Malik said: Before that, Uthman ibn Affan (may Allah be pleased with him) used to pass by Hashsh Kawkab and say: A righteous man shall be buried here. Abu al-Qasim said: Al-hashsh is the garden."
"110","Narrated Ahmad ibn Abd al-Wahhab ibn Najda al-Hawti from Abd al-Wahhab ibn al-Dahhak from Ismail ibn Ayyash from Muhammad ibn Yazid al-Rahabi from Sahm ibn Hubaysh, who was among those who witnessed Uthman's killing, who said: When evening came I said: If you leave your companion till morning they will mutilate him; so take him to Baqi al-Gharqad, and we made ready for him in the depth of night. Then we carried him, and a black mass came upon us from behind; we feared them till we almost scattered from him. A caller called: No fright upon you, stand firm, for we have come to attend him with you. Ibn Hubaysh used to say: By Allah, they were the angels."
"111","Narrated Amr ibn Abi al-Tahir ibn al-Sarh al-Misri from Abd al-Munim ibn Bashir al-Ansari from Ali ibn Ghurab al-Muharibi from Abdullah ibn Said from his father who said: We were sitting with Ali ibn Abi Talib (may Allah be pleased with him), with Ammar ibn Yasir on his right and Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr on his left, when Ghurab ibn so-and-so al-Saydani came and said: Commander of the Believers, what do you say concerning Uthman? The two men hastened to him and said: About what do you ask? About a man who disbelieved in Allah after his faith and was a hypocrite? The man said to them: It is not you I ask, nor to you I came. Ali said to him: I do not say what they said. They both said to him together: Then why did we kill him? He said: He was put in charge of you and misruled at the end of his days, and you panicked and panicked badly. By Allah, I hope I and Uthman shall be as Allah (mighty and majestic) said: And We removed what was in their breasts of rancor, brothers upon couches facing one another."
"112","Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz from Arim Abu al-Numan from Hammad ibn Zayd from Mujalid ibn Said from Umayr ibn Zawdi who said: Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) addressed the people and said: O people, by Allah, if none enters the Fire but Uthman's killer, I shall not enter it; and if none enters Paradise but Uthman's killer, I shall not enter it. When he came down he was told: You spoke a word that scattered your companions from you. So he addressed them and said: O people, Allah (mighty and majestic) killed Uthman and I am with him. Hammad said, and Habib ibn al-Shahid narrated to us from Muhammad ibn Sirin who said: A Qurashi phrase with two faces. Abu al-Qasim said: As if he means that Allah Most High killed him and I am killed along with him (may Allah be pleased with them both)."
"113","Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz from Arim from Hammad ibn Zayd from Mujalid ibn Said from Umayr ibn Zawdi who said: Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) addressed them, and they cut off his sermon. He said: I was weakened only on the day Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him) was killed. And he struck for them a parable, the parable of three bulls and a lion gathered in a thicket, black, red, and white. When the lion wanted one of them, they joined against him and resisted him. The lion said to the black and red: It is only this white one that disgraces us in our thicket and exposes us; leave me to eat him, for your color is upon my color and my color upon yours. The lion pounced upon him and soon killed him. Then he said to the black: It is only this red one that disgraces and exposes us in our thicket; leave me to eat him, for my color is upon yours and yours upon mine. He pounced upon him and killed him. Then he said to the black: I shall eat you. He said: Let me cry three cries. He said: Verily I was eaten only on the day the white was eaten; verily I was eaten only on the day the white was eaten; verily I was eaten only on the day the white was eaten; verily I was weakened only on the day Uthman was killed."
"114","Narrated Abu Khalifa from Abu Umar Hafs ibn Umar al-Hawdi from al-Hasan ibn Abi Jafar from Mujalid from al-Shaabi who said: Masruq met al-Ashtar, and Masruq said to al-Ashtar: Did you kill Uthman? He said: Yes. He said: By Allah, you killed him fasting, praying at night. Al-Ashtar went and told Ammar (may Allah be pleased with him); Ammar came to Masruq and said: By Allah, Ammar shall be flogged, Abu Dharr shall be exiled, the pasture shall be reserved, yet you say: You killed him fasting, praying at night. Masruq said to him: By Allah, you did neither of two: you neither requited with the like of what you were afflicted with, nor were patient, and it is better for the patient. He said: It was as if he fed him a stone. Al-Shaabi said: No Hamdani woman bore the like of Masruq."
"115","Narrated Abu Khalifa from Ahmad ibn Yahya ibn Humayd al-Tawil from Hammad ibn Salama from Muhammad ibn Amr from his father from his grandfather Alqama ibn Waqqas who said: We gathered in Makhrama's house after Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him) was killed, seeking the pledge. Abu Jahm ibn Hudhayfa said: Whoever of you pledges to us, we shall not bar requital. Ammar ibn Yasir said: But not for Uthman's blood. Abu Jahm said: O son of Sumayya, by Allah you shall be requited for lashes you were lashed, but there shall be no requital for Uthman's blood (may Allah be pleased with him). So they left that day without a pledge."
"116","Narrated Sulayman ibn al-Hasan al-Attar al-Basri from Abu Kamil al-Jahdari from Ismail ibn Ibrahim from Ibn Awn from al-Hasan who said: Waththab, who was among those freed by Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him) and used to stand before Uthman, told me: Uthman sent me and I called al-Ashtar for him. Ibn Awn said: I think he said: I laid a cushion for the Commander of the Believers and one for him. He said: Ashtar, what do the people want of me? He said: Three, there is no escape from one of them. He said: What are they? He said: They give you choice between stripping for them their affair, saying: This is your affair, choose for it whom you will; and between requiting yourself; if you refuse both, the people will kill you. He said: No escape from one of them? He said: No escape from one. He said: As for stripping for them their affair, I shall never strip a shirt I was clothed with. Al-Hasan said: He said: By Allah, for me to be brought forward and have my neck struck is dearer to me than to strip the affair of Muhammad's nation (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), one part over another. Ibn Awn said: And this is liker Uthman's speech. As for requiting myself, by Allah I knew my two companions before me were punished, and my body cannot bear requital. As for killing me, by Allah if you kill me you will never love one another after me, nor fight an enemy together after me ever. Al-Ashtar stood and left. We stayed and said: Perhaps the people, when a man like a wolf peered from a door then withdrew. Then Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr came with thirteen men until they reached Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him); he seized his beard and shook it till I heard the clatter of his molars, saying: What availed you Muawiya, what availed you Ibn Amir, what availed you your letters. He said: Release my beard, nephew, release my beard, nephew. He said: I saw him beckon a man of the people by his eye, who rose to him with an arrowhead and struck him with it in his head. I said: Then what? He said: Then they rushed upon him, by Allah, till they killed him."
"117","Narrated Sulayman ibn al-Hasan al-Attar from Abu Kamil al-Jahdari from Ismail ibn Ibrahim from Ibn Awn from Muhammad ibn Sirin that Muhammad ibn Abi Hudhayfa ibn Utba ibn Rabia and Kab rode a ship at sea. Muhammad said: Kab, do you not find this ship of ours in the Torah, how it sails? He said: No, but I find therein a most wretched youth of Quraysh who leaps in turmoil as the donkey leaps; be not thou he. Ibn Sirin said: They claimed he was he."
"118","Narrated Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Sadaqa al-Baghdadi and Ishaq ibn Dawud al-Sawwaf al-Tustari from Muhammad ibn Khalid ibn Khidash from Salm ibn Qutayba from Mubarak from al-Hasan from Uthman's swordsman (may Allah be pleased with him) that an Ansari man entered upon Uthman and he said: Go back, nephew, thou art not my killer. He said: How didst thou know that? He said: Because thou wast brought to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on thy seventh day, and he tahniked thee and prayed for blessing for thee. Then another Ansari man entered upon him and he said: Go back, nephew, thou art not my killer. He said: How knowest thou that? He said: Because thou wast brought to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on thy seventh day, and he tahniked thee and prayed for blessing for thee. Then Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr entered upon him and he said: Thou art my killer. He said: What makes thee know, Nathal? He said: Because thou wast brought to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on thy seventh day to be tahniked and prayed for, and thou defecated upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: He leapt upon his chest and seized his beard, saying: If thou doest, thy father would be grieved to displease him. He said: He stabbed him in his throat with arrowheads in his hand."
"119","Narrated Yahya ibn Abd al-Baqi al-Adhani from al-Musayyib ibn Wadih from Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak from Yunus ibn Yazid from Ibn Shihab from Abi Salama ibn Abd al-Rahman who said: When the man struck Uthman's hand, he said: It is the first hand to write al-Mufassal."
"120","Narrated Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Irq al-Himsi from Amr ibn Uthman from his father from Muhammad ibn al-Muhajir from al-Abbas ibn Salim that Umayr ibn Rabia told him that Umar ibn al-Khattab sent to Kab al-Ahbar and said: Kab, how findest thou my description? He said: I find thy description an iron horn. He said: What is an iron horn? He said: A right-guiding commander whom no blamer's blame seizes concerning Allah. He said: Then what? He said: Then after thee shall be a caliph killed by an unjust faction. He said: Then what? He said: Then shall be tribulation."
"121","Narrated Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi from Muawiya ibn Amr from Zaida from Ismail ibn Abi Khalid from Qays who said: I heard Said ibn Zayd say: By Allah, if Uhud crumbled for what you did to Ibn Affan, it would be rightful to crumble."
"122","Narrated Abu Muslim al-Kashshi, al-Hasan ibn al-Muthanna al-Anbari, and Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz from Arim Abu al-Numan from al-Saq ibn Hazn from Qatada from Zahdam al-Jarmi who said: Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) addressed us and said: If the people did not seek Uthman's blood, they would be stoned with stones from heaven."
"123","Narrated Zakariya ibn Yahya al-Saji from Muhammad ibn Abi Safwan al-Thaqafi from Umayya ibn Khalid from Qurra ibn Khalid who said: I heard al-Hasan say: The transgressor Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr was seized in a ravine of Egypt's ravines and put inside a donkey's belly and burned."
"124","Narrated al-Miqdam ibn Dawud al-Misri from Abu al-Aswad al-Nadr ibn Abd al-Jabbar from Ibn Lahia from Yazid ibn Amr al-Maafiri who said: I heard Abu Thawr al-Nahmi narrate from Uthman ibn Affan (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: I have stored with my Lord ten: I am the fourth of four in Islam; I never sought fornication nor wished it, nor placed my right hand upon my private part since I pledged to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); no Friday passed upon me since I embraced Islam but I freed therein a slave, unless I had none, then I freed afterward; and I never committed adultery in ignorance nor Islam."
"125","Narrated Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi from Muawiya ibn Amr from Zaida from Kulayb ibn Wail from Habib ibn Abi Mulayka, kunya Abu Thawr, who said: I was sitting with Ibn Umar when a man came and asked him: What thinkest thou of Uthman? Did he attend Badr? He said: No; as for the day of Badr, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Allah, Uthman is in Thy need and Thy Messenger's need; so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) struck for him with his arrow."
"126","Narrated Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Khalid al-Harrani from his father from Ibn Lahia from Abu al-Aswad from Urwa who said: Uthman ibn Affan stayed behind in Medina over his wife, the daughter of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who was ill and feverish; so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) struck for him with his arrow. He said: And my reward, Messenger of Allah? He said: And thy reward."
"127","Narrated Ahmad ibn Yazid ibn Harun al-Makki al-Qazzaz from Ibrahim ibn al-Mundhir al-Hizami from Abbas ibn Abi Shamla from Musa ibn Yaqub al-Zamai from his brother Muhammad ibn Yaqub from Abdullah ibn Rafi from his mother who said: Al-Saba bint al-Hadrami went out, and we heard her say to her son Talha ibn Ubaydullah: Uthman's siege has intensified; wouldst thou speak concerning him that he be eased. Talha (may Allah be pleased with him) was washing one side of his head and did not answer her; she put her hands into her armored sleeves and brought out her breasts, saying: I ask thee by what I carried thee and suckled thee but thou do it. He stood and twisted the washed side of his head's hair till he knotted it, then went out till he came to Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) sitting at the side of his house. Talha (may Allah be pleased with him), with his mother and Umm Abdullah ibn Rafi present, said: Wouldst thou ease this one; his siege has intensified. He tapped with a cup in his hand three times, then raised his head and said: By Allah, I love nothing of this that thou dislikest. Abu Khalifa recited to us from al-Abbas ibn al-Faraj al-Riyashi poetry of Layla al-Akhyaliyya: O caliph of the night-praying. Abu Khalifa recited to us from Abu Muhammad al-Tawzi; Abu Khalifa said: I asked al-Riyashi about it and he said: It is Hassan ibn Thabit's."
"128","Narrated Abu Yazid al-Qaratis i from Asad ibn Musa from Abu Hilal al-Rasibi from Qatada that Uthman ibn Affan (may Allah be pleased with him), when one announcing prayer came to him, would say: Welcome to those who speak justly, and to prayer welcome and kin."
"129","Narrated Abu Yazid al-Qaratis i from Asad ibn Musa from Sallam ibn Miskin from Muhammad ibn Sirin who said: Uthman's wife (may Allah be pleased with him) said when they surrounded him seeking to kill him: Whether you kill him or leave him, he would enliven the whole night in one raka gathering therein the Quran."
"130","Narrated Abu Yazid al-Qaratis i from Asad ibn Musa from al-Mubarak ibn Fadala who said: I heard al-Hasan say: I reached Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him) when I had just neared puberty; I heard him sermonize, and witnessed him say: O people, what do you hold against me? No day passed but they shared good therein, saying: O people, go early to your stipends. They would go early and take them in full. Then it would be said: O Muslims, go early to your clothing. Mantles would be brought and divided among them. Al-Hasan said: The enemy was banished, stipends flowing, relations good, good abundant; no believer on earth feared a believer; whomever one met, of whatever tribe, he was his brother, his affection and support; turmoil is to draw a sword against him."
"131","Narrated Abu Khalifa from Abdullah ibn Abd al-Wahhab al-Hajabi from Hazm ibn Abi Hazm from Abi al-Aswad who said: I heard Abu Bakra say: For me to fall from heaven and be torn is dearer to me than to have shared in Uthman's blood (may Allah be pleased with him)."
"132","Narrated al-Fadl ibn al-Hubab Abu Khalifa from Abdullah ibn Abd al-Wahhab al-Hajabi from Hazm from Abi al-Aswad who said: I heard Taliq ibn Khashshaf say: We came as a delegation to Medina to see for what Uthman was killed. When we arrived, some of us went to Ali, some to al-Husayn ibn Ali (may Allah be pleased with them both), some to the Mothers of the Believers (may Allah be pleased with them). I went till I came to Aisha and greeted her, and she returned greeting. She said: Who is the man? I said: Of Basra's people. She said: Of which Basrans? I said: Of Bakr ibn Wail. She said: Of which Bakr ibn Wail? I said: Of Banu Qays ibn Thalaba. She said: Of such-and-such folk? I said to her: Mother of the Believers, for what was Uthman, Commander of the Believers (may Allah be pleased with him), killed? She said: By Allah, he was killed wronged; may Allah curse his killers; may Allah requite Ibn Abi Bakr with him, bring humiliation to the eyes of Banu Tamim in his house, shed the blood of Banu Budayl upon error, and drive to al-Ashtar an arrow of His arrows. By Allah, not a man of the people but her call struck him."
"133","Narrated Muhammad ibn Ali al-Saigh al-Makki from al-Fadl ibn Ziyad from Ibn al-Mubarak from Ibn Lahia from Yazid ibn Abi Habib that most of the riders who marched against Uthman went mad."
"134","Narrated Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi from Muawiya ibn Amr from Zaida from Asim ibn Abi al-Nujud from Shaqiq ibn Salama who said: Al-Walid ibn Uqba met Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf and said: Why do I see thee estranged from the Commander of the Believers Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him)? Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf (may Allah be pleased with him) said: Convey to him that I did not stay back from Badr. Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him) was told of that and said: As for his saying: I did not stay back from Badr, I was nursing Ruqayya bint the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) till she died; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) struck for me with an arrow, and whomever the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) strikes for with an arrow has attended."
"135","Narrated Ubayd ibn Ghannam from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shayba from Yazid ibn Harun from al-Awwam ibn Hawshab from Said ibn Jumhan from Safina who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The caliphate after me in my nation is thirty years. He said: So we counted and found: Abu Bakr two, Umar ten, Uthman twelve, Ali six (may Allah be pleased with them)."
"136","Narrated Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Abi Musa al-Antaki from Yaqub ibn Kab al-Halabi from al-Walid ibn Muslim from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid ibn Jabir and Abd al-Ghaffar ibn Ismail ibn Ubaydullah from Ismail ibn Ubaydullah from Abi Abdullah al-Ashari from Abi al-Darda (may Allah be pleased with him) from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: I said: Messenger of Allah, they claim thou saidst: A people shall disbelieve after their faith. He said: Yes, and thou art not of them. Abu al-Darda died before Uthman's killing (may Allah be pleased with them both)."
"137","Narrated Abbad ibn Ali al-Sirini from Bakkar ibn Muhammad al-Sirini from Ibn Awn from Muhammad ibn Sirin from Uqba ibn Aws from Abdullah ibn Amr who said: Uthman Dhu al-Nurayn was killed wronged."
"138","Narrated Ubayd ibn Ghannam from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shayba from Abu Usama from Hisham ibn Hassan from Muhammad ibn Sirin from Uqba ibn Aws from Abdullah ibn Amr (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, you hit his name; Umar, an iron horn; Uthman Dhu al-Nurayn, you hit his name; he was killed wronged, given two shares of reward."
"139","Narrated Ubayd ibn Ghannam from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shayba from Abu Muawiya from al-Amash from Abdullah ibn Sinan who said: I heard Abdullah ibn Masud say, when allegiance was given to Uthman: We did our utmost to choose the highest with an arrowhead."
"140","Narrated Ubayd ibn Ghannam from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shayba from Abu Muawiya from Muhammad ibn Bishr from Ismail ibn Abi Khalid from Hakim ibn Jabir who said: I heard Abdullah say, when allegiance was given to Uthman: We did our utmost to choose the highest with an arrowhead."
"141","Narrated Muttalib ibn Shuayb al-Azdi from Abdullah ibn Salih from al-Layth ibn Sad from Khalid ibn Yazid from Said ibn Abi Hilal from Rabia ibn Sayf from Abdullah ibn Amr who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: There shall be twelve caliphs after me: Abu Bakr al-Siddiq shall not remain but a little; and the owner of the millstone of the Arabs' abode shall live praised and be killed martyred. A man said: Who is he, Messenger of Allah? He said: Umar ibn al-Khattab. Then he turned to Uthman and said: People shall ask thee to remove a shirt Allah clothed thee with; by Him in whose hand is my soul, if thou removest it thou shalt not enter Paradise till the camel passes through the eye of the needle."
"142","Narrated Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal from Bashshar ibn Musa al-Khaffaf from al-Hasan ibn Ziyad al-Burjumi, imam of Muhammad ibn Wasi's mosque, from Qatada from al-Nadr ibn Anas from Anas ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him) went out emigrating to Abyssinia, with Ruqayya bint the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); news of them was withheld from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who would go out awaiting news of them. A woman came and told him, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Uthman is the first to emigrate to Allah with his household after Lut."
"143","Narrated Ubayd ibn Ghannam from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shayba from Ubaydullah ibn Musa from Musa ibn Ubayda from Iyas ibn Salama ibn al-Akwa from his father that when the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent Uthman to the people of Mecca, he pledged his companions the pledge of Ridwan, pledging for Uthman with one of his hands upon the other. The people said: Glad tidings to Abu Abdullah circumambulating the House secure. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Were he to stay such and such, he would not circumambulate till I circumambulate."
"144","And what Uthman ibn Affan (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated. Narrated Bishr ibn Musa from Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Muqri from Kahmas ibn al-Hasan from Musab ibn Thabit from Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr who said: Uthman ibn Affan (may Allah be pleased with him) said while preaching on the pulpit: I shall narrate to you a hadith I heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); nothing kept me from narrating to you but miserliness toward you. I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Guarding a night in Allah's path is better than a thousand nights whose nights are stood and whose days are fasted."
"145","Narrated Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari from Abd al-Razzaq from Mamar from Abi Hazim from Sahl ibn Sad who said: Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him) one day adjured the people, saying: Do you know that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) climbed Uhud, with Abu Bakr, Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both), and I, and Uhud shook, with upon it Muhammad the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), Abu Bakr, Umar, and Uthman (may Allah be pleased with them); so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Be firm, Uhud; upon thee is but a prophet, a siddiq, and two martyrs."
"146","147 - Narrated to us Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz, narrated to us Muslim ibn Ibrahim, narrated to us Hurayth ibn al-Sa'ib, narrated to us al-Hasan, from Humran ibn Aban, from Uthman ibn Affan (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Everything in excess of the shade of a house, the crust of bread and a garment covering a man's private parts - or he said: the private parts of the son of Adam - and everything in excess of that, the son of Adam has no right in it."
"147","148 - Narrated to us al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari, narrated to us Ali ibn Bahr, narrated to us Qatadah ibn al-Fudayl ibn Qatadah al-Ruhawi, who said: I heard Abdullah ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Amrah al-Ansari narrating, from his father, from Uthman ibn Affan who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever prays the last Isha prayer in congregation, it is as if he prayed the whole night, and whoever prays the morning prayer in congregation, it is as if he prayed the whole day."
"148","149 - Narrated to us Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal and Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Hadrami, both said: narrated to us Zakariyya ibn Yahya Zahmawayh, narrated to us Ziyad ibn Abdullah, narrated to us Muhammad ibn Suqah, from Amr ibn Maymun, who said: I heard Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him) - and he narrated little from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever performs ablution as he was commanded and prays as he was commanded, he comes out of his sins as his mother bore him."
"149","4 - The lineage of Ali ibn Abi Talib (may Allah be pleased with him) - he was present at Badr. Son of Abd al-Muttalib ibn Hashim ibn Abd Manaf ibn Qusayy ibn Kilab ibn Murrah ibn Ka'b ibn Lu'ayy ibn Ghalib ibn Fihr ibn Malik, known as Abu al-Hasan. 150 - Narrated to us Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal, narrated to me my father, who said: It reached me from Banu Hashim: Abu Talib's name is Abd Manaf ibn Abd al-Muttalib, and Abd al-Muttalib's name is Shaybah ibn Hashim, and Hashim's name is Amr ibn Abd Manaf ibn Qusayy, and Qusayy's name is Zayd."
"150","151 - Narrated to us Ishaq ibn Ahmad al-Khuza'i al-Makki, narrated to us al-Zubayr ibn Bakkar, who said: The mother of Ali ibn Abi Talib (may Allah be pleased with him) is Fatimah bint Asad ibn Hashim ibn Abd Manaf ibn Qusayy, and it is said: she was the first Hashimi woman to bear a child for a Hashimi man. She embraced Islam, emigrated to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in Madinah, died, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) buried her. Her mother is Fatimah bint Hirm ibn Rawahah ibn Hujr ibn Abd Mu'rid ibn Amir ibn Lu'ayy."
"151","The description of Ali ibn Abi Talib (may Allah be pleased with him). 152 - Narrated to us Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz, narrated to us Abu Nu'aym, narrated to us Yunus ibn Abi Ishaq, from Abu Ishaq, who said: I saw Ali (may Allah be pleased with him). My father said to me: O Amr, look at the Commander of the Believers. I did not see him dye his beard; large-headed."
"152","153 - Narrated to us Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Ma'mar, from Abu Ishaq, who said: I saw Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) with white head and beard, wearing a waist-wrapper and a cloak."
"153","154 - Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Hadrami, narrated to us Yusuf ibn Ya'qub al-Saffar, narrated to us Hamad ibn Khalid al-Khayyat, from Ibn Abi Dhi'b, from Sufyan al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, who said: I saw Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) on the pulpit with white head and beard."
"154","155 - Narrated to us Ishaq ibn Ibrahim, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Isra'il, who said: Abu Ishaq informed me, saying: I went out with my father to Friday prayer when I was a boy. When Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) came out and ascended the pulpit, my father said to me: Stand, O Amr, and look at the Commander of the Believers. He said: So I stood, and there he was standing on the pulpit, white of beard and head, wearing a waist-wrapper and a cloak with no shirt on. He said: I never saw him sit on the pulpit until he came down from it. We said to Abu Ishaq: Did he recite Qunut? He said: No."
"155","156 - Narrated to us Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Waki' ibn al-Jarrah, who said: Sharik informed me, from Abu Ishaq, that when Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) married Fatimah (may Allah be pleased with her), she said to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): You married me to a dim-sighted man with a large belly. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I have married you to him, and he is indeed the first of my companions in entering Islam, the most knowledgeable of them, and the greatest of them in forbearance."
"156","157 - Narrated to us Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna, narrated to us Musaddad, narrated to us Yahya ibn Sa'id, (H) and narrated to us Amr ibn Abi al-Tahir ibn al-Sarh al-Misri, both said: narrated to us Abu Salih al-Harrani, who said: Waki' said: both from Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid, from al-Sha'bi, who said: I saw Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) on the pulpit with a white beard that filled what is between his shoulders. Yahya ibn Sa'id added in his narration: On his head were small tufts of hair."
"157","158 - Narrated to us Abdullah ibn al-Saqr al-Sukkari, narrated to us Ibrahim ibn al-Mundhir al-Hizami, from al-Waqidi, who said: It is said: Ali ibn Abi Talib was dark-complexioned, of medium height, fleshy, broad-shouldered, long-bearded, bald, big-bellied, thick-eyed, white of head and beard."
"158","159 - Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Ali al-Sa'igh al-Makki, narrated to us Yahya ibn Ma'in, narrated to us Hajjaj ibn Muhammad, narrated to us Shu'bah, who said: I asked Abu Ishaq: Are you older than al-Sha'bi? He said to me: Al-Sha'bi is older than me by a year or two. He said: Abu Ishaq saw Ali (may Allah be pleased with him), and he used to describe him to us as big-bellied and bald at the front of the head. Shu'bah said: Abu Ishaq was older than Abu al-Bakhtari, and Abu al-Bakhtari did not meet Ali nor see him."
"159","160 - Narrated to us Zakariyya ibn Yahya al-Saji, narrated to us Abu Bakr ibn Nafi', narrated to us Umayyah ibn Khalid, narrated to us Shu'bah, from Amr ibn Murrah, from Abi al-Tufayl, who said: I mentioned to Ibn Mas'ud the saying of Ali (may Allah be pleased with him), and he said: Have you not seen his head like a bowl, with what is around it like a rim."
"160","161 - Narrated to us Abdan ibn Ahmad, narrated to us Yusuf ibn Hammad al-Ma'ni, narrated to us Wuhayb ibn Jarir, narrated to us my father, from Abi Raja' al-Utaridi, who said: I saw Ali (may Allah be pleased with him), fleshy, bald of hair, as if at his side were a sheep's skin."
"161","The age of Ali ibn Abi Talib and his death (may Allah be pleased with him). 162 - Narrated to us Abu al-Zinba' Rawh ibn al-Faraj al-Misri, narrated to us Yahya ibn Bukayr, narrated to us Ibn Lahi'ah and al-Layth ibn Sa'd, from Abi al-Aswad, from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, who said: Ali ibn Abi Talib embraced Islam when he was eight years old."
"162","163 - Narrated to us Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Ma'mar, Qatadah informed me, from al-Hasan and others, who said: The first to believe was Ali ibn Abi Talib, when he was fifteen or sixteen years old."
"163","164 - Narrated to us Abu al-Zinba' Rawh ibn al-Faraj, narrated to us Yahya ibn Bukayr, who said: Ali ibn Abi Talib was killed on Friday, the seventeenth day of Ramadan, in the year forty."
"164","165 - Narrated to us Ahmad ibn Zayd ibn Harun al-Qazzaz al-Makki, narrated to us Ibrahim ibn al-Mundhir al-Hizami, narrated to us Husayn ibn Zayd ibn Ali, from Ja'far ibn Muhammad, from his father, who said: Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) died when he was sixty-three years old."
"165","166 - Narrated to us Ahmad ibn Zayd, narrated to us Ibrahim ibn al-Mundhir, narrated to us Sufyan, from Ja'far ibn Muhammad, from his father, who said: Ali died when he was fifty-eight years old."
"166","167 - Narrated to us al-Qasim ibn Abbad al-Khattabi al-Basri, narrated to us Sa'id ibn Subayh, who said: Hisham ibn al-Kalbi said, from Awanah ibn al-Hakam, who said: When Abd al-Rahman ibn Muljam struck Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) and he was carried to his house, visitors came to him. He praised Allah (Mighty and Majestic), extolled Him, and invoked blessings upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then said: Every man shall meet what he flees from in his flight, and the appointed term drives the soul, and fleeing from its calamities - how often I drove the days, searching them for the hidden secret of this matter, but Allah (Mighty and Majestic) refused but to conceal it. Alas, a stored knowledge. As for my bequest to you: Allah (Mighty and Majestic) - associate nothing with Him, and Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - do not neglect his Sunnah. Uphold these two pillars, and blame is lifted from you so long as you are not dispersed. Each man shall bear his effort, and it is lightened for the ignorant by a Merciful Lord, a straight religion, and a knowledgeable Imam. We were in winds and scattered branches, under the shade of a cloud whose centre vanished, so a captive lowers it. You neighboured, drawing near days in succession, then it fell, and after it you shall be left with a hollow lifeless corpse, still after movement, silent after speech. It is more admonishing to those who take heed than the speech of the eloquent. Your caller is a caller lying in wait for the meeting. Tomorrow you shall see my days, and my secrets shall be uncovered. Allah (Mighty and Majestic) will not favour me unless I draw near to Him with piety, so He forgives a promised lapse. Peace be upon you until the Day of Reckoning. If I remain, I am the guardian of my blood, and if I perish, perishing is my appointment. Pardon is nearness for me and goodness for you, so pardon - may Allah pardon us and you: Do you not love that Allah should forgive you, and Allah is Forgiving, Merciful. Then he said: Departing."
"167","168 - Narrated to us Ahmad ibn Ali al-Abbar, narrated to us Abu Umayyah Amr ibn Hisham al-Harrani, narrated to us Uthman ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Tara'ifi, narrated to us Isma'il ibn Rashid, who said: Part of the account of Ibn Muljam - may Allah curse him and his companions - is that Abd al-Rahman ibn Muljam, al-Bark ibn Abdullah, and Amr ibn Bakr al-Tamimi gathered in Mecca. They discussed the affairs of the people, criticised the conduct of their rulers, then mentioned the people of al-Nahr and invoked mercy upon them. They said: By Allah, we gain nothing by remaining after them; our brothers who called people to the worship of their Lord and feared not the blame of any blamer for Allah's sake. If only we sold ourselves and went to the imams of error seeking to kill them, relieving the land of them and avenging our brothers through them. Ibn Muljam - who was from the people of Egypt - said: I will suffice you against Ali ibn Abi Talib. Al-Bark ibn Abdullah said: I will suffice you against Mu'awiyah ibn Abi Sufyan. Amr ibn Bakr al-Tamimi said: I will suffice you against Amr ibn al-As. They pledged and covenanted by Allah that none of them would turn back from his target until he killed him or died short of it. They took their swords and poisoned them, and appointed the seventeenth of Ramadan for each man to fall upon his target. Each man set out for the city containing his quarry. As for Ibn al-Muljam al-Muradi, he came to his companions in Kufa, concealing his affair from them lest they reveal anything of it. He met companions of his from Taym al-Ribab, among whom Ali ibn Abi Talib (may Allah be pleased with him) had killed a number on the Day of al-Nahr, and they mentioned their slain and invoked mercy upon them. He met that day a woman from Taym al-Ribab called Qatam bint al-Shihnah, whose father and brother Ali ibn Abi Talib (may Allah be pleased with him) had killed on the Day of al-Nahr. She was of surpassing beauty. When he saw her, she confounded his mind and he forgot the need for which he had come, and he proposed to her. She said: I will not marry until you heal me. He said: What do you desire? She said: Three thousand, a slave, a singing girl, and the killing of Ali ibn Abi Talib (may Allah be pleased with him). He said: It is a dowry for you; as for the killing of Ali, I do not think you mentioned him to me while meaning it. She said: Nay, rather seek his unguarded moment; if you strike him you will heal yourself and me, and life with me will benefit you, and if you are killed, what is with Allah is better than this world and its splendour. He said: Nothing brought me to this city but the killing of Ali. She said: When you intend that, inform me so I may seek for you one to strengthen your back and aid you in your affair. She sent for a man of her people from Taym al-Ribab called Wardan, spoke to him, and he agreed. Ibn Muljam went to a man from Ashja' called Shabib ibn Najdah and said to him: Will you have the honour of this world and the Hereafter? He said: What is it? He said: The killing of Ali (may Allah be pleased with him). He said: May your mother be bereaved of you, you have come with a grave matter; how can you manage to kill him? He said: I shall lie in wait for him at dawn; when he comes out for the morning prayer we shall rush upon him and kill him. If we escape, we heal ourselves and attain our revenge, and if we are killed, what is with Allah is better than this world and its splendour. He said: Woe to you, if it were other than Ali it would be easier for me; I know his tribulation in Islam and his precedence with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and I do not find myself inclined to kill him. He said: Do you not know that he killed the people of al-Nahr, the worshippers who prayed? He said: Yes. He said: Then kill him for what he killed of our brothers. So he agreed, and they came to Qatam while she was in the Grand Mosque in retreat, and said to her: Our resolve is settled upon killing Ali. She said: When you intend that, come to me. He came and said: This is the night in which we agreed. They lay in wait in the mosque. Ibn Muljam's sword missed and Shabib's struck, then the people rushed. Wardan fled to his house; a man of his maternal kin entered upon him while he was removing silk and the sword from his chest and said: What are this sword and silk? He told him what had happened, so he went to his house, brought his sword, and struck him until he killed him. Shabib went out toward the gates of Kindah; the people pressed upon him, but a Hadrami man called Uwaymir struck his leg with a sword and felled him, and the Hadrami knelt upon him. When he saw the people coming in pursuit of him, with Shabib's sword in his hand, he feared for himself and left him, saving himself, and Shabib escaped among the crowd. An eyewitness account states: I spent that night in the mosque with people of the city, all standing, bowing, and prostrating, never wearying from the first of the night to its end, when Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) came out for the morning prayer, calling: O people, the prayer, the prayer. I know not whether I uttered these words or looked at the gleam of swords, and I heard: Judgment belongs to Allah, not to you O Ali, nor to your companions. I saw a sword, then I saw people, and I heard Ali saying: Let not the man escape you. The people rushed upon him from every side, and I did not move until Ibn Muljam was seized and brought in to Ali (may Allah be pleased with him), and I entered among those who entered. Ali said: Feed him, give him drink, treat him well; if I live I shall see my judgment concerning him, and if I die from this blow of mine, strike him one blow and do not mutilate him, for I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Beware of mutilation even to a rabid dog. They said to Ibn Muljam: Do you see but that you shall be killed by it, and I see you but among the worst of Allah's creation. Ibn Muljam was bound before al-Hasan when Umm Kulthum bint Ali called to him weeping: O enemy of Allah, my father is in no harm, and Allah shall disgrace you. He said: Why do you weep? By Allah, I bought it for a thousand and poisoned it for a thousand; had this blow fallen upon all the people of the city, not one of them would remain an hour. Ali said to al-Hasan (may Allah be pleased with them both): If I remain I shall see my judgment concerning him, and if I perish from this blow of mine, strike him one blow and do not mutilate him, for I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbidding mutilation. Then Ibn Muljam said: Will you grant me a favour? By Allah, I never gave Allah a covenant but I fulfilled it; I covenanted with Allah to kill Ali and Mu'awiyah or die short of them both. If you wish, leave me to face him, and Allah is my bond to you that if I am not killed I shall come to you and place my hand in yours. Al-Hasan (may Allah be pleased with him) said to him: No, by Allah, until you behold the Fire. He brought him forward and killed him, then the people took him, wrapped him in mats, and burned him with fire. Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) had said: O Banu Abd al-Muttalib, let me not find you plunging into the blood of the Muslims, saying: The Commander of the Believers is killed. As for al-Bark, he lay in wait for Mu'awiyah; Mu'awiyah came out with none guarding him, so he struck him but could not kill him. Mu'awiyah (may Allah be pleased with him) ordered him killed. He sent for al-Sa'idi, who was a physician, and he looked at him and said: Your wound is poisoned; choose from me one of two traits: either I heat an iron and place it where the sword struck, or I give you a draught that cuts off offspring from you, and you shall recover from it, for your wound is poisoned. Mu'awiyah said to him: As for fire, I have no patience for it, and as for the cutting off of offspring, in Yazid and Abdullah and their children is that which delights my eye. So he gave him that draught that night. Then Ali counselled al-Hasan and al-Husayn with piety, kinship, forbearance toward ignorance, understanding in religion, deliberation in affairs, devotion to the Qur'an, good neighbourliness, commanding right, forbidding wrong, and avoiding indecencies. Then he looked at Muhammad ibn al-Hanafiyyah and said: Have you memorised what I counselled your two brothers with? He said: Yes. He said: I counsel you with the like, and I counsel you to revere your two brothers for the greatness of their right over you, to adorn their affair, and not to decide a matter without them. Then he said to them both: I counsel you concerning him, for he is your brother and your father's son, and you know your father loved him. Then his bequest was: I counsel you, O Hasan, O Husayn, and all my family and children and whoever my letter reaches, with piety toward Allah your Lord: Die not except as Muslims, and hold fast all together to the rope of Allah and be not divided. I heard Abu al-Qasim (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Setting right relations is greater than general prayer and fasting. Look to your kin and join them, Allah will lighten the reckoning for you. Allah, Allah concerning orphans, let them not be neglected in your presence. Allah, Allah concerning prayer, for it is the pillar of your religion. Allah, Allah concerning zakat, for it extinguishes the wrath of the Lord (Mighty and Majestic). Allah, Allah concerning your neighbours, for they are the bequest of your Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: Gabriel kept commending them to me until I thought he would bequeath them inheritance. Allah, Allah concerning the Companions of your Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), for he commended them. Allah, Allah concerning the two weak ones: your women and what your right hands possess, for the last that he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) uttered was: I counsel you concerning the two weak ones: women and what your right hands possess. Prayer, prayer. Fear not in Allah the blame of any blamer; He suffices you against whoever intends you and transgresses against you. Speak to people good as Allah commanded you, and do not abandon commanding right and forbidding wrong, lest the worst of you rule you and then you supplicate and are not answered. Then he said: I commend you to Allah and convey peace to you. Then he uttered nothing but: There is no god but Allah, until he passed away in the month of Ramadan in the year forty. Al-Hasan, al-Husayn, and Abdullah ibn Ja'far washed him, he was shrouded in three garments with no shirt among them, al-Hasan pronounced nine takbirs over him, and al-Hasan (may Allah be pleased with him) assumed his office for six months. Before Ibn Muljam struck Ali, he was sitting among Banu Bakr ibn Wa'il when the bier of Abjar ibn Jabir al-Ijli Abu Hajjar, a Christian, passed by him with Christians around it. As for the third, Amr ibn Bakr, he went to Amr ibn al-As in Egypt; he found a man called Kharijah ibn Abi Habib, his chief of police from Banu Amir ibn Lu'ayy, going out to lead the people in prayer, and he rushed upon him thinking him Amr ibn al-As and struck him with the sword, killing him. He was seized and brought in to Amr. When he saw them greeting him as commander, he said: Who is this? They said: Amr ibn al-As. He said: Whom did I kill? They said: Kharijah. He said: By Allah, O wicked one, I aimed at none but you. Amr said: You intended me, and Allah intended Kharijah. He brought him forward and killed him. That reached Mu'awiyah (may Allah be pleased with him), and he wrote to him. The bearer of his death notice was Sufyan ibn Abd Shams ibn Abi Waqqas. A youth said to Sa'd: Will you have wealth and honour? He said: What is it? He said: Bind al-Hasan and seek safety with him to Mu'awiyah. Sa'd said to him: Allah's curse upon you; shall I fall upon the son of the daughter of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and bind him? Evil is the man you are. When al-Hasan (may Allah be pleased with him) saw the people dispersing from him, he sent to Mu'awiyah seeking peace. Mu'awiyah sent to him Abdullah ibn Amir and Abdullah ibn Samurah ibn Habib ibn Abd Shams, and they came to al-Hasan at al-Mada'in, granted him what he asked, and made peace with him. Then al-Hasan (may Allah be pleased with him) stood among the people and said: O people of Iraq, three things make my soul disinclined from you: your killing my father, your stabbing me, and your plundering my goods. He entered into obedience to Mu'awiyah, may Allah have mercy on them both, and entered Kufa and the people pledged allegiance to him."
"168","169 - Narrated to us Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam, narrated to us Muhammad ibn Yusuf al-Firyabi, narrated to us Fitr ibn Khalifah, from Abi al-Tufayl, who said: Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) called them to allegiance, and among them came Abd al-Rahman ibn Muljam, whom he had seen twice before that. Then he said: What restrains the most wretched? By the One in Whose Hand is my soul, this shall surely be dyed from this. And he recited these two verses beginning: Your sides."
"169","170 - Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Idris ibn Asim al-Jammal al-Baghdadi, narrated to us Ishaq ibn Rahawayh, Ishaq: Ya'la ibn Ubayd informed us, from al-A'mash, from Abi Rashid, who said: Men from Basra came to Ubayd ibn Umayr and said: Your brothers from Basra ask you about Ali and Uthman (may Allah be pleased with them both). He said: Has nothing but this brought you? They said: Yes. He said: That is a nation that has passed; it shall have what it earned and you shall have what you earn, and you shall not be asked about what they used to do."
"170","171 - Narrated to us al-Miqdam ibn Dawud, narrated to us Ali ibn Ma'bad, narrated to us Ubaydullah ibn Amr, from Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Aqil, who said: Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) was killed in the year forty."
"171","172 - Narrated to us Ubayd ibn Ghannam, narrated to us Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah, who said: Ali was killed in the year forty, and his caliphate was five years and six months."
"172","173 - Narrated to us Yahya ibn Uthman ibn Salih and Muttalib ibn Shu'ayb al-Azdi, narrated to us Abdullah ibn Salih, al-Layth ibn Sa'd narrated to me, Khalid ibn Yazid narrated to me, from Sa'id ibn Abi Hilal, from Zayd ibn Aslam, that Abu Sinan al-Du'ali narrated to him that he visited Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) during an illness he complained of, and I said to him: We feared for you, O Abu al-Hasan, in this illness of yours. He said: But by Allah, I did not fear for myself from it, for I heard the truthful one believed (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: You shall surely be struck a blow here and a blow here - and he pointed to his temples - and its blood shall flow until it dyes your beard. Its author shall be the most wretched, as the hamstringer of the she-camel was the most wretched of Thamud."
"173","174 - Narrated to us Fudayl ibn Muhammad al-Malati, narrated to us Musa ibn Dawud, narrated to us Qays ibn al-Rabi', from al-Hajjaj, from al-Hakam, from Miqsam, from Ibn Abbas who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) handed the banner to Ali ibn Abi Talib (may Allah be pleased with him) when he was twenty years old."
"174","And what Ali ibn Abi Talib (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated. 175 - Narrated to us al-Abbas ibn al-Fadl al-Asfati, narrated to us Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi, narrated to us Hammad ibn Salamah, from Ayyub, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both), from Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: I married Fatimah, and I said: O Messenger of Allah, house me with her? He said: Have you anything to give her? I said: No. He said: Where is your Hutami armour? I said: It is with me. He said: Give it to her."
"175","176 - Narrated to us Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz, narrated to us Amr ibn Hammad ibn Talhah al-Qannad, narrated to us Asbat ibn Nasr, from Simak ibn Harb, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, that Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) used to say in the lifetime of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Allah (Mighty and Majestic) says: If he dies or is killed, will you turn back on your heels. By Allah, we shall never turn back on our heels after Allah has guided us. By Allah, if he dies or is killed, I shall surely fight for what he fought for until I die. By Allah, I am his brother, his guardian, his cousin, and his heir; who is more entitled to him than me."
"176","177 - Narrated to us al-Abbas ibn al-Fadl al-Asfati, narrated to us Musa ibn Isma'il, narrated to us Hammad ibn Salamah, from Ali ibn al-Hakam, from Abi Uthman, from Abi Musa, that Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) said: Shall I not inform you of the best of this nation after its Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? They said: Yes. He said: Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him). Then he said: Shall I not inform you of the best of this nation after Abu Bakr? They said: Yes. He said: Umar, and if I wished I could inform you of the third."
"177","178 - Narrated to us Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz, narrated to us Abu Nu'aym, narrated to us Harun ibn Salman al-Farra, freedman of Amr ibn Hurayth, Amr ibn Hurayth narrated to me, from Ali (may Allah be pleased with him), that he was sitting on the pulpit and mentioned Abu Bakr and Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both), and said: The best of this nation after its Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is Abu Bakr, and the second is Umar, and if I wished to mention the third I would mention him."
"178","179 - Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah, narrated to us Minjab ibn al-Harith, narrated to us Abu Hudhayfah al-Tha'labi, from Ziyad ibn Ilaqah, from Jabir ibn Samurah al-Suwa'i, from Ali (may Allah be pleased with him): that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I asked my Lord (Mighty and Majestic) for three traits for my nation; He granted me two and withheld one from me. I said: O Lord, do not destroy my nation with hunger. He said: This is yours. I said: O Lord, do not impose upon them an enemy from other than them - meaning the people of polytheism - who would exterminate them. He said: That is yours. I said: O Lord, do not place their might among themselves. He withheld this from me."
"179","180 - Narrated to us Abu Muslim al-Kashshi, narrated to us Abd al-Hamid ibn Bahr al-Zahrani, narrated to us Khalid ibn Abdullah, from Bayan, from al-Sha'bi, from Abi Juhayfah, from Ali (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: When the Day of Resurrection comes it shall be said: O people of the gathering, lower your gazes until Fatimah bint Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passes. She shall pass wearing two green garments - may Allah be pleased with her."
"180","181 - Narrated to us Hafs ibn Umar al-Raqqi, narrated to us Abu Hudhayfah, narrated to us Sufyan, from Jabir, from Abi al-Tufayl, from Ali ibn Abi Talib (may Allah be pleased with him), that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: May Allah curse Suhayl three times, for he used to take a tenth from people in the land, so Allah transformed him into a shooting star."
"181","182 - Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Hadrami, narrated to us Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Salim al-Qazzaz, narrated to us Husayn ibn Zayd ibn Ali, from Ali ibn Umar ibn Ali, from Ja'far ibn Muhammad, from his father, from Ali ibn al-Husayn, from al-Husayn ibn Ali (may Allah be pleased with him), from Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Fatimah (may Allah be pleased with her): Allah is angered by your anger and pleased by your pleasure."
"182","183 - Narrated to us Ja'far ibn Muhammad al-Firyabi, narrated to us Abu Ja'far al-Nufayli, narrated to us Yunus ibn Rashid, from Awn ibn Muhammad ibn al-Hanafiyyah, from his father, from his grandfather Ali ibn Abi Talib (may Allah be pleased with him), that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Upon you is ithmid, for it grows hair, removes impurity, and clarifies vision."
"183","184 - Narrated to us al-Husayn ibn Mansur al-Rummani, narrated to us Abu Ja'far al-Nufayli, narrated to us Yunus ibn Rashid, from Awn ibn Muhammad ibn al-Hanafiyyah, from his father, from his grandfather Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: I know only that we set out as pilgrims raising the voice for Hajj, and neither the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) nor Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) dissolved pilgrimage until they circumambulated the House on the Day of Sacrifice, and al-Safa and al-Marwah."
"184","185 - Narrated to us Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Isra'il, from Abd al-A'la, from Muhammad ibn al-Hanafiyyah, from Ali (may Allah be pleased with him), that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would join fasting from dawn to dawn."
"185","186 - Narrated to us Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz, narrated to us Abu Nu'aym, narrated to us al-Hakam ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Nu'aym al-Bajali, who said: Fatimah bint Ali ibn Abi Talib (may Allah be pleased with them both) narrated to me, saying: My father said, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Whoever frees a Muslim or believing soul, Allah shall shield every limb of his with a limb from the Fire."
"186","5 - The lineage of Talhah ibn Ubaydullah (may Allah be pleased with him). 187 - Narrated to us Ahmad ibn Abdullah ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Barqi, narrated to us Abd al-Malik ibn Hisham, from Abu Ubaydah Ma'mar ibn al-Muthanna, who said: Talhah ibn Ubaydullah ibn Uthman ibn Amir ibn Ka'b ibn Sa'd ibn Taym ibn Murrah ibn Ka'b ibn Lu'ayy ibn Ghalib ibn Fihr ibn Malik. His mother is al-Sa'bah bint al-Hadrami. He was called al-Hadrami because he was in the land of Hadramawt, where he killed Amr ibn Nahid al-Himyari then fled to Mecca and allied with Harb ibn Umayyah. The name of al-Hadrami is Abdullah ibn Ammar ibn Rabi'ah ibn Akbar ibn Awf ibn Malik ibn Uwayf ibn Khazraj ibn Iyad ibn al-Sadaf ibn Hadramawt ibn Qahtan ibn Kindah. Al-Sa'bah is the sister of al-Ala' ibn al-Hadrami, and her mother is Atikah bint Wahb ibn Abd ibn Qusayy ibn Kilab ibn Murrah ibn Ka'b."
"187","188 - Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr al-Muqaddami, narrated to us Abdullah ibn Shabib, narrated to us Ibrahim ibn Yahya al-Shajari, narrated to us my father, from Khazim ibn Husayn, from Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr, from al-Zuhri, from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah ibn Utbah, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: The mother of Talhah ibn Ubaydullah embraced Islam."
"188","189 - Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Khalid al-Harrani, narrated to us my father, narrated to us Ibn Lahi'ah, from Abi al-Aswad, from Urwah, who said: Talhah ibn Ubaydullah ibn Uthman ibn Amr ibn Ka'b ibn Sa'd ibn Taym ibn Murrah was in Syria; he came and spoke to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about his share, and he struck for him his share. He said: And my reward, O Messenger of Allah? He said: And your reward - meaning on the Day of Badr."
"189","190 - Narrated to us Abu al-Zinba' Rawh ibn al-Faraj al-Misri, narrated to us Yahya ibn Bukayr, who said: Talhah ibn Ubaydullah was known as Abu Muhammad (may Allah be pleased with him)."
"190","The description of Talhah ibn Ubaydullah (may Allah be pleased with him). 191 - Narrated to us Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz, narrated to us al-Zubayr ibn Bakkar, Ishaq ibn Yahya ibn Talhah narrated to me, from his uncle Musa ibn Talhah, who said: Talhah ibn Ubaydullah (may Allah be pleased with him) was fair inclining to redness, of medium height closer to short, broad-chested, wide-shouldered; when he turned he turned altogether, large-footed. Al-Zubayr said: He narrated to me - meaning Ibrahim ibn al-Mundhir - from al-Waqidi, who said: Talhah ibn Ubaydullah was dark, abundantly haired, neither curly nor lank, handsome-faced, thin-nosed; when he walked he hastened. He would not dye his grey hair. He was killed on the Day of the Camel in Jumada in the year thirty-six."
"191","192 - Narrated to us Ubayd ibn Ghannam, narrated to us Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah, narrated to us Waki', from Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid, from Qays ibn Abi Hazim, who said: I saw the hand of Talhah ibn Ubaydullah paralysed, with which he shielded the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the Day of Uhud."
"192","193 - Narrated to us Ahmad ibn al-Mu'alla al-Dimashqi, narrated to us Hisham ibn Ammar, narrated to us Muhammad ibn Isa ibn Sumay', from Ibn Abi Dhi'b, from al-Zuhri, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, who said: The finger of Talhah ibn Ubaydullah was paralysed."
"193","Among his virtues (may Allah be pleased with him). 194 - Narrated to us Abu Yazid al-Qaratisli, narrated to us Asad ibn Musa, narrated to us Sufyan ibn Uyaynah, from Mujalid, from al-Sha'bi, from Qabisah ibn Jabir, who said: I never saw any man who gave generously of wealth without being asked more than Talhah ibn Ubaydullah. Sufyan said: His family used to say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) named him al-Fayyad."
"194","195 - Narrated to us Abu Yazid al-Qaratisli, narrated to us Asad ibn Musa, narrated to us Sufyan ibn Uyaynah, from Talhah ibn Yahya, from his grandmother - his wife Su'da - who said: Talhah came to me one day, and I saw heaviness from him. I said: What is with you; perhaps something from us has alarmed you, so we shall make amends to you? He said: No, and excellent is the wife of a Muslim man you are, but wealth has gathered with me and I know not what to do with it. She said: What grieves you of it? Call your people and divide it among them. He said: O boy, bring my people to me. I asked the treasurer: How much did he distribute? He said: Four hundred thousand."
"195","196 - Narrated to us Abu Yazid al-Qaratisli, narrated to us Asad ibn Musa, narrated to us Sufyan, from Amr ibn Dinar, who said: The income of Talhah ibn Ubaydullah was each day a full thousand."
"196","197 - Narrated to us Yahya ibn Uthman ibn Salih, narrated to us Sulayman ibn Ayyub ibn Sulayman ibn Isa ibn Musa ibn Talhah ibn Ubaydullah, my father narrated to me, from my grandfather, from Musa ibn Talhah, from his father Talhah ibn Ubaydullah (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) named me on the Day of Uhud: Talhah al-Khayr, and in the expedition of Dhu al-Ushayrah: Talhah al-Fayyad, and on the Day of Hunayn: Talhah al-Jud. Abu al-Qasim said: With sin and shin both; with sin from al-Usrah, and with shin a place."
"197","198 - Narrated to us Mas'adah ibn Sa'd al-Attar, narrated to us Ibrahim ibn al-Mundhir, narrated to us Muhammad ibn Talhah al-Taymi, from Ishaq ibn Yahya ibn Talhah, from his uncle Musa ibn Talhah, that Talhah slaughtered a camel and dug a well on the Day of Dhu Qarad, feeding them and giving them drink. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Talhah al-Fayyad. So he was named Talhah al-Fayyad."
"198","The age of Talhah ibn Ubaydullah and his death (may Allah be pleased with him). 199 - Narrated to us Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz, narrated to us al-Zubayr ibn Bakkar, who said: Ibrahim ibn al-Mundhir narrated to me, from al-Waqidi, who said: Ishaq ibn Yahya ibn Talhah narrated to me, from his uncle Isa ibn Talhah, who said: Talhah was on the day he was killed sixty-two years old. Al-Waqidi said: He was killed on the Day of the Camel in Jumada in the year thirty-six. Al-Waqidi said: Muhammad ibn Isma'il ibn Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Talhah ibn Ubaydullah narrated to me, from Muhammad ibn Zayd ibn al-Muhajir ibn Qunfudh al-Taymi, who said: Talhah was killed when he was sixty-four, and was buried in Basra in the quarter of Thaqif."
"199","200 - Narrated to us Abu al-Zinba' Rawh ibn al-Faraj, narrated to us Yahya ibn Bukayr, who said: Talhah ibn Ubaydullah (may Allah be pleased with him) was killed on the Day of the Camel in Jumada in the year thirty-six, and his age was fifty-two or fifty-four years. Al-Zubayr was older than him, and his kunya is Abu Muhammad."
"200","201 - Narrated to us Ahmad ibn Yahya ibn Hayyan ibn Khalid al-Raqqi, narrated to us Yahya ibn Sulayman al-Ju'fi, narrated to us Waki', from Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid, from Qays ibn Abi Hazim, who said: I saw Marwan ibn al-Hakam when Talhah was that day struck with an arrow that lodged in the eye of his knee; he kept glorifying Allah until he died."
"201","202 - Narrated to us Ahmad ibn Yahya ibn Hayyan al-Raqqi, narrated to us Yahya ibn Sulayman al-Ju'fi, narrated to us Abdullah ibn Idris, from Layth, from Talhah ibn Musarrif, that Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) came upon Talhah ibn Ubaydullah after he had died, dismounted from his animal and sat him up, wiping dust from his face and beard, invoking mercy upon him and saying: Would that I had died twenty years before this day."
"202","203 - Narrated to us Abu Khalifah, narrated to us Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Mubarak, narrated to us Hammad ibn Zayd, narrated to us Sa'id ibn Abi Arubah, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, from Qays ibn Abbad, who said: I heard Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) on the Day of the Camel saying to his son Hasan: O Hasan, I wish I had died twenty years ago."
"203","And what Talhah ibn Ubaydullah (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated. 204 - Narrated to us Yahya ibn Uthman ibn Salih, narrated to us Sulayman ibn Ayyub ibn Sulayman ibn Isa ibn Musa ibn Talhah ibn Ubaydullah, my father narrated to me, from my grandfather, from Musa ibn Talhah, from his father Talhah ibn Ubaydullah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever deliberately lies about me, let him take his seat in the Fire."
"204","205 - Narrated to us Yahya ibn Uthman ibn Salih, narrated to us Sulayman ibn Ayyub, my father narrated to me, from my grandfather, from Musa ibn Talhah, from Talhah, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Part of humility toward Allah is contentment with the lowest of the honour of sittings."
"205","206 - Narrated to us Yahya ibn Uthman ibn Salih, narrated to us Sulayman ibn Ayyub, my father narrated to me, from my grandfather, from Musa ibn Talhah, from Talhah, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah say: The one who marries among his people is like one who pastures in his own abode."
"206","207 - Narrated to us Yahya ibn Uthman ibn Salih, narrated to us Sulayman ibn Ayyub, my father narrated to me, from my grandfather, from Musa ibn Talhah, from Talhah, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: There was never a prophethood but after it came killing and crucifixion."
"207","208 - Narrated to us Yahya ibn Uthman ibn Salih, narrated to us Sulayman ibn Ayyub, my father narrated to me, from my grandfather, from Musa ibn Talhah, from Talhah, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Amr ibn al-As is among the righteous of Quraysh."
"208","209 - Narrated to us Yahya ibn Uthman ibn Salih, narrated to us Sulayman, narrated to us my father, from my grandfather, from Musa ibn Talhah, from Talhah, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: O Amr, you are of sound judgment in Islam."
"209","210 - Narrated to us Yahya ibn Uthman ibn Salih, narrated to us Sulayman ibn Ayyub, who said: My father narrated to me, from my grandfather, from Musa ibn Talhah, from Talhah, that he led a people in prayer; when he finished, he said: I forgot to seek your permission before leading you; are you pleased with my prayer? They said: Yes, and who would dislike that, O disciple of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Any man who leads a people while they dislike him, his prayer does not pass beyond his ear."
"210","211 - Narrated to us Yahya ibn Uthman ibn Salih, narrated to us Sulayman ibn Ayyub, my father narrated to me, from my grandfather, from Musa ibn Talhah, from Talhah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever is granted a favour, let him mention it; whoever mentions it has thanked for it, and whoever conceals it has been ungrateful for it."
"211","212 - Narrated to us Yahya ibn Uthman ibn Salih, narrated to us Sulayman ibn Ayyub, my father narrated to me, from my grandfather, from Musa ibn Talhah, from his father, who said: The riding camel of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his perfume were entrusted to me. A man came to him asking for one of them, and he said: That is with Talhah ibn Ubaydullah. He came to me and informed me, but I refused him. He returned to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and asked him, and he gave him the like reply. He came to me and informed me, but I refused him. He returned to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said the like, then returned to me. I said to myself: He sends him to me only because he wishes to fulfil his need, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was hardly asked anything but he did it. I said: That I handle the skin of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is more beloved to me than that I handle his riding camel. So I handed it to him. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) intended a journey and ordered that his camel be saddled, and he came to me and said: Which of the two saddles was more beloved to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? I said: The Ta'ifi one. So he saddled it for him, then brought it near to him. When it rose with him it overturned, and he said: Who saddled this? They said: So-and-so. He said: Return it to Talhah. So it was returned to me. Talhah said: By Allah, I never deceived anyone in Islam but him, so that the riding camel of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would return to me."
"212","213 - Narrated to us Yahya ibn Uthman ibn Salih, narrated to us Sulayman ibn Ayyub, my father narrated to me, from my grandfather, from Musa ibn Talhah, from his father, who said: On the Day of Uhud I placed the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) upon my back until he stood firm and reached the rock, sheltered from the polytheists. He said: Like this - and gestured with his hand behind my back: This is Gabriel (peace be upon him) who informed me that he shall never see you on the Day of Resurrection in a terror but he shall deliver you from it."
"213","214 - Narrated to us Yahya ibn Uthman ibn Salih, narrated to us Sulayman ibn Ayyub, my father narrated to me, from my grandfather, from Musa ibn Talhah, from his father, who said: On the Day of Uhud an arrow struck me, and I said: Hass. He said: Had you said: In the name of Allah, the angels would have flown with you while people looked at you."
"214","215 - Narrated to us Yahya ibn Uthman ibn Salih, narrated to us Sulayman ibn Ayyub, my father narrated to me, from my grandfather, from Musa ibn Talhah, from his father, who said: When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw me he would say: Whoever loves to look at a martyr walking upon the earth, let him look at Talhah ibn Ubaydullah."
"215","216 - Narrated to us Yahya ibn Uthman ibn Salih, narrated to us Sulayman ibn Ayyub, my father narrated to me, from my grandfather, from Musa ibn Talhah, from his father, who said: When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw me he would say: My predecessor in this world and my predecessor in the Hereafter."
"216","217 - Narrated to us Yahya ibn Uthman ibn Salih, narrated to us Sulayman ibn Ayyub, my father narrated to me, from my grandfather, from Musa ibn Talhah, from his father, who said: When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) returned from Uhud, he ascended the pulpit, praised Allah (Mighty and Majestic) and extolled Him, then recited this verse: Men who were true to what they covenanted with Allah - the whole verse. A man stood to him and said: O Messenger of Allah, who are they? I came forward wearing two green garments, and he said: O questioner, this is among them."
"217","218 - Narrated to us Yahya ibn Uthman ibn Salih, narrated to us Sulayman ibn Ayyub, my father narrated to me, from my grandfather, from Musa ibn Talhah, from his father, who said: On the Day of Uhud the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) named me: Talhah al-Khayr, and on the day of the expedition of Dhat al-Ushayrah: Talhah al-Fayyad, and on the Day of Hunayn: Talhah al-Jud."
"218","219 - Narrated to us Yahya ibn Uthman ibn Salih, narrated to us Sulayman ibn Ayyub, my father narrated to us, from my grandfather, from Musa ibn Talhah, from his father, who said: I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was among a group of his companions, with a quince in his hand turning it. When I sat with him, he tossed it toward me, then said: Take it, O Abu Muhammad, for it strengthens the heart, sweetens the soul, and removes the heaviness of the chest."
"219","6 - The lineage of al-Zubayr ibn al-Awwam (may Allah be pleased with him). Al-Zubayr ibn al-Awwam ibn Khuwaylid ibn Asad ibn Abd al-Uzza ibn Qusayy ibn Kilab ibn Murrah ibn Ka'b ibn Lu'ayy ibn Ghalib ibn Fihr ibn Malik, known as Abu Abdullah. His mother is Safiyyah bint Abd al-Muttalib, the paternal aunt of the Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). 220 - Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Khalid al-Harrani, my father narrated to me, narrated to us Ibn Lahi'ah, from Abi al-Aswad, from Urwah: Among those of Banu Asad ibn Abd al-Uzza who were present at Badr with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): al-Zubayr ibn al-Awwam ibn Khuwaylid ibn Asad."
"220","221 - Narrated to us Abu al-Zinba', narrated to us Yahya ibn Bukayr, who said: Al-Zubayr was known as Abu Abdullah."
"221","222 - Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Hadrami, narrated to us Musa ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Masruqi, narrated to us Abu Usamah, from Abi Ishaq al-Fazari, from Hisham ibn Urwah, who said: On the Day of Yarmuk, they said to al-Zubayr: O Abu Abdullah."
"222","The description of al-Zubayr ibn al-Awwam (may Allah be pleased with him). 223 - Narrated to us Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz, narrated to us al-Zubayr ibn Bakkar, who said: I heard Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Urwah ibn al-Zubayr say: Al-Zubayr ibn al-Awwam (may Allah be pleased with him) was fair, tall, thin of hair on the sides, light of the cheeks."
"223","224 - Narrated to us Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz, narrated to us al-Zubayr ibn Bakkar, narrated to us Abu Ghazziyyah, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi al-Zinad, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, who said: Al-Zubayr ibn al-Awwam (may Allah be pleased with him) was tall; his feet would drag on the ground when he rode an animal; hairy; sometimes I would grasp the hair of his shoulders."
"224","225 - Narrated to us Ubayd ibn Ghannam, narrated to us Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah, narrated to us Yazid ibn Harun, narrated to us Sa'id ibn Abi Arubah, from Ayyub, from Nafi', who said: Ibn Umar heard a man say: I am the son of the disciple of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Ibn Umar said: If you are of the family of al-Zubayr, otherwise not."
"225","226 - Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Hadrami, narrated to us Amr ibn Abdullah al-Awdi, narrated to us Abu Usamah, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, who said: The first to draw a sword in the path of Allah was al-Zubayr ibn al-Awwam."
"226","227 - Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Hadrami, narrated to us Abd al-Malik ibn al-Walid al-Bajali, Ahmad ibn Yazid al-Wartanisi informed us, narrated to us Fulayh ibn Sulayman, from Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Aqil, from Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said on the Day of the Trench: Who will bring us news of Banu Qurayzah? Then he called them a second time, then a third, and al-Zubayr volunteered. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Every Prophet has a disciple, and my disciple is al-Zubayr."
"227","228 - Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Hadrami, narrated to us Hamzah ibn Awn al-Mas'udi, narrated to us Muhammad ibn al-Qasim al-Asadi, narrated to us Sufyan al-Thawri, Sharik, and Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash, from Asim, from Zirr, from Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Every Prophet has a disciple, and my disciple is al-Zubayr."
"228","229 - Narrated to us Abu Yazid al-Qaratisli, narrated to us Asad ibn Musa, Sukayn ibn Abd al-Aziz al-Basri narrated to us, Hafs ibn Khalid narrated to us, who said: A shaykh who came to us from Mosul narrated to me, saying: I accompanied al-Zubayr ibn al-Awwam on one of his journeys, and major impurity befell him in a barren land. He said: Screen me. So I screened him, then I happened to glance at him and saw him gashed with swords. I said: By Allah, I have seen upon you marks I never saw upon anyone. He said: Have you seen that? I said: Yes. He said: By Allah, there is not a wound among them but with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and in the path of Allah (Mighty and Majestic)."
"229","230 - Narrated to us Abu Yazid al-Qaratisli, narrated to us Asad ibn Musa, narrated to us Hammad ibn Salamah, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from Urwah, who said: Gabriel (peace be upon him) descended on the Day of Badr in the likeness of al-Zubayr ibn al-Awwam, wearing a yellow turban wound round the head."
"230","231 - Narrated to us Abu Yazid al-Qaratisli, narrated to us Asad ibn Musa, narrated to us Jami' Abu Salamah, from Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid, from al-Bahiy, who said: On the Day of Badr there were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) two horsemen: al-Zubayr ibn al-Awwam on a horse on the right flank, and al-Miqdad ibn al-Aswad on a horse on the left flank."
"231","232 - Narrated to us Ahmad ibn Zayd ibn Harun al-Qazzaz al-Makki, narrated to us Ibrahim ibn al-Mundhir al-Hizami, narrated to us Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Urwah, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, that Muti' ibn al-Aswad said: I heard Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) say: By Allah, if I made a covenant or left a bequest, none would be more beloved to me to entrust it to than al-Zubayr ibn al-Awwam, for he is a pillar of the pillars of religion."
"232","233 - Narrated to us Ahmad ibn Zayd ibn Harun al-Makki, narrated to us Ibrahim ibn al-Mundhir, narrated to us Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Urwah, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, that Ali ibn Abi Talib (may Allah be pleased with him) met al-Zubayr (may Allah be pleased with him) in the market, and they reproached one another over something of Uthman's affair (may Allah be pleased with him). Then Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr spoke harshly to him, and Ali said: Do you not hear what he says to me? So al-Zubayr struck him until he fell."
"233","234 - Narrated to us Ahmad ibn Zayd ibn Harun, narrated to us Ibrahim ibn al-Mundhir al-Hizami, narrated to us Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Urwah, from Hisham ibn Urwah, who said: Al-Zubayr struck Asma' bint Abi Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him), and she cried out to Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr. He came, and when he saw him he said: Your mother is divorced if you enter. Abdullah said to him: Will you make my mother subject to your oath? He rushed upon him and rescued her from him, and she separated from him. He said: I was a boy; I would sometimes grasp the hair of al-Zubayr's shoulders."
"234","235 - Narrated to us Ahmad ibn Zayd ibn Harun, narrated to us Ibrahim ibn al-Mundhir, narrated to us Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, that Ali ibn Abi Talib (may Allah be pleased with him) said: O my sons, marry your daughters only to equals. They said: O our father, who are the equals? He said: The sons of al-Zubayr ibn al-Awwam."
"235","236 - Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Hadrami informed us, saying: I heard Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Numayr say: Al-Zubayr was killed when he was sixty-four, and was killed in the year thirty-six."
"236","The age of al-Zubayr ibn al-Awwam, his death and reports about him (may Allah be pleased with him). 237 - Narrated to us Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal, narrated to us Yahya ibn Ma'in, narrated to us Abu Usamah, from Hisham ibn Urwah, who said: Al-Zubayr embraced Islam when he was sixteen, and was killed when he was in his sixties."
"237","238 - Narrated to us Abu al-Zinba' Rawh ibn al-Faraj al-Misri, narrated to us Yahya ibn Bukayr, who said: Al-Zubayr ibn al-Awwam (may Allah be pleased with him) was killed on the Day of the Camel in Jumada - I know not whether the first or the last - in the year thirty-six. Yahya said: Al-Layth informed me, from Abi al-Aswad, that Urwah informed him that al-Zubayr (may Allah be pleased with him) embraced Islam when he was eight years old, and was known as Abu Abdullah. If the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stayed in Mecca thirteen years, then on the day he was killed he was fifty-seven, and if he stayed ten years, then al-Zubayr was fifty-four years old."
"238","239 - Narrated to us Abu Yazid al-Qaratisli, narrated to us Asad ibn Musa, narrated to us Abdullah ibn Wahb, narrated to us al-Layth ibn Sa'd, from Abi al-Aswad, who said: Al-Zubayr ibn al-Awwam embraced Islam when he was eight years old, and emigrated when he was eighteen. The uncle of al-Zubayr would hang al-Zubayr in a mat and smoke him with fire, saying: Return to disbelief. Al-Zubayr would say: I shall never disbelieve."
"239","240 - Narrated to us Abu Yazid al-Qaratisli, narrated to us Asad ibn Musa, narrated to us Anas ibn Iyad, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, who said: When Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) was killed, al-Zubayr erased his name from the register."
"240","241 - Narrated to us Abu Muslim al-Kashshi, narrated to us Abd al-Malik ibn Qurayb al-Asma'i, who said: I heard Ibn Awn say: These excellent ones were killed slain. Then he wept and said: The killer of al-Zubayr advanced upon al-Zubayr, and al-Zubayr advanced upon him, and he said: I remind you of Allah. So al-Zubayr held back from him, until he did that repeatedly. Al-Zubayr said: May Allah destroy him; he reminds by Allah (Mighty and Majestic) yet forgets Him."
"241","242 - Narrated to us Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz, narrated to us Abu Rabi'ah Fahd ibn Awf, narrated to us Mubarak ibn Fudalah, from al-Hasan, from Abi Bakrah, who said: On the Day of the Camel, Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) saw heads falling, took the hand of al-Hasan (may Allah be pleased with him) and placed it upon his belly, then said: O my son, what good can be hoped for after this."
"242","243 - Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Hadrami, narrated to us Hamzah ibn Awn al-Mas'udi, who said: Muhammad ibn al-Qasim al-Asadi narrated to us, Sufyan al-Thawri and Sharik narrated to us, from Asim ibn Abi al-Najud, from Zirr ibn Hubaysh, who said: I was sitting with Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) when the head of al-Zubayr (may Allah be pleased with him) was brought. Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) said: Give glad tidings to the killer of Ibn Safiyyah of the Fire. I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Every Prophet has a disciple, and my disciple is al-Zubayr."
"243","244 - Narrated to us Ubayd ibn Ghannam, narrated to us Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah, narrated to us Abu Usamah, from Hisham ibn Urwah, who said: Al-Zubayr embraced Islam when he was sixteen years old, never stayed back from any expedition the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) undertook, and was killed when he was in his sixties, about a barid from Basra."
"244","245 - Narrated to us Ubayd ibn Ghannam, narrated to us Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah, who said: Al-Zubayr was killed in the year thirty-six; Ibn Jurmuz killed him, with al-Nu'man ibn Zimam and Abu al-Madrahiy, two men of Banu Tamim with him. He was killed in Wadi al-Siba' and buried there."
"245","246 - Narrated to us Ubayd ibn Ghannam, narrated to us Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah, narrated to us Abu Usamah, Hisham ibn Urwah narrated to us: that Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, Uthman, al-Miqdam ibn al-Aswad, Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, and Muti' ibn al-Aswad bequeathed to al-Zubayr ibn al-Awwam."
"246","247 - Narrated to us Mas'adah ibn Sa'd al-Attar, narrated to us Ibrahim ibn al-Mundhir al-Hizami, narrated to us Muhammad ibn Talhah, Ishaq ibn Yahya ibn Talhah narrated to us, from his uncle Musa ibn Talhah, who said: Ali, al-Zubayr, and Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas were weaned in the same year."
"247","And among what al-Zubayr ibn al-Awwam (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated. 248 - Narrated to us Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz, narrated to us Muslim ibn Ibrahim, (H) and narrated to us Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Hadrami, narrated to us Rawh ibn Abd al-Mu'min al-Muqri, both said: Muhammad ibn Dinar narrated to us, Hisham ibn Urwah narrated to us, from his father, from Ibn al-Zubayr, from al-Zubayr, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: One suckling and two sucklings do not prohibit marriage."
"248","249 - Narrated to us Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz, narrated to us Abu Nu'aym Dirar ibn Surad, narrated to us Abd al-Aziz, from Muhammad ibn Abd al-Aziz, from Ibn Shihab, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Miswar ibn Makhramah, from his father, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: Al-Zubayr ibn al-Awwam (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated to me, saying: I saw Hind bint Utbah uncovering her leg on the Day of Uhud, as if I look at anklets on her leg, inciting the people."
"249","250 - Narrated to us Ahmad ibn Rashdin al-Misri, narrated to us Muhammad ibn Abi al-Sari al-Asqalani, narrated to us Umar ibn Hafs ibn Thabit ibn As'ad ibn Zurarah al-Ansari, Umar ibn Hafs: Abd al-Malik ibn Yahya ibn Abbad ibn Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr narrated to us, from his father, from his grandfather, from Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr, from al-Zubayr ibn al-Awwam, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: The earth is Allah's earth, and servants are Allah's servants; wherever one of you finds good, let him fear Allah and stay. I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: That one of you take a rope, gather firewood upon his back, sell and eat, is better for him than asking people, whether they give him or withhold. I heard the Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say when he recited this verse: Allah bears witness that there is no god but He - to His saying: the Mighty, the Wise. He said: And I bear witness that You are none but the Mighty, the Wise."
"250","251 - Narrated to us Ahmad ibn Abd al-Wahhab ibn Najdah al-Hawti, my father narrated to us, Baqiyyah ibn al-Walid narrated to us, Numayr ibn Yazid al-Qayni narrated to us, my father narrated to us, Quhafah ibn Rabi'ah narrated to us, who said: Al-Zubayr ibn al-Awwam (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated to me, saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) led us in the dawn prayer in the mosque of Madinah. When he finished, he said: Which of you will follow me to the delegation of the jinn tonight? The people fell silent and none spoke. He said that three times, then passed by me walking, took my hand, and I walked with him until the mountains of Madinah all vanished from us, and we reached a flat land. There were tall men like spears, tucking their garments between their legs. When I saw them, severe trembling overcame me until my legs could not hold me from fear. When we drew near them, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) drew a line in the earth with his big toe and said to me: Sit in its middle. When I sat, everything of doubt I felt left me. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went between me and them, reciting lofty Qur'an until dawn broke. Then he came until he passed by me and said to me: Join me. I walked with him, and we went a short distance. He said to me: Turn and look; do you see any of those where they were? I said: O Messenger of Allah, I see much darkness. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) lowered his head to the earth, arranged a bone with dung, then threw it to them and said: Those rightly guided of a delegation of a people - they are the delegation of Nasibin - asked me for provision, so I made for them every bone and dung. Al-Zubayr said: It is not lawful for anyone ever to cleanse himself with a bone or dung."
"251","7 - The lineage of Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf (may Allah be pleased with him). 252 - Narrated to us Ahmad ibn Abdullah ibn Abd al-Rahim al-Barqi, narrated to us Abd al-Malik ibn Hisham, from Abu Ubaydah Ma'mar ibn al-Muthanna, who said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf ibn Abd Awf ibn Abd al-Harith ibn Zuhrah ibn Kilab."
"252","253 - Narrated to us Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, that Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf (may Allah be pleased with him) - his name in ignorance was Abd al-Ka'bah. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) named him Abd al-Rahman."
"253","254 - Narrated to us Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal, Uqbah ibn Mukram narrated to me, Ya'qub ibn Muhammad al-Zuhri narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Abd al-Aziz ibn Umar ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf narrated to us, my father narrated to me, from his father, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, who said: My name was Abd Amr, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) named me Abd al-Rahman."
"254","255 - Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Ali al-Madini Fustuqah, al-Husayn ibn Ali ibn Yazid al-Suda'i narrated to us, Ya'qub narrated to us, from his father, from Ibn Ishaq, who said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf ibn Abd Awf ibn Abd al-Harith ibn Zuhrah, known as Abu Muhammad, was present at Badr."
"255","256 - Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Khalid al-Harrani, my father narrated to us, Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us, from Abi al-Aswad, from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr: Among those of Banu Zuhrah ibn Kilab ibn Murrah who were present at Badr with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf ibn al-Harith ibn Zuhrah."
"256","257 - Narrated to us Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz, al-Qa'nabi narrated to us, from Malik, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf (may Allah be pleased with him): What did you do, O Abu Muhammad, in touching the Corner - meaning the Black Stone? Abd al-Rahman said: I touched and left. The Messenger of Allah said: You did right."
"257","The description of Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf (may Allah be pleased with him). 258 - Narrated to us Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Ma'mar, from Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr, from Qabisah ibn Jabir al-Asadi, who said: I was in pilgrim sanctity and saw a gazelle; I shot it and hit the base of its horn - meaning the root of its horn - and it mounted its young. Something of that entered my soul, so I came to Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) asking him, and found beside him a fair man of delicate face; it was Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf (may Allah be pleased with him). I asked Umar (may Allah be pleased with him), and he turned to Abd al-Rahman and said: Do you see a sheep sufficing him? He said: Yes. So he ordered me to slaughter a sheep. We rose from him, and a companion of mine said: The Commander of the Believers did not know how to answer you until he asked the man. Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) heard some of his speech, struck him with the stick, then turned to strike me. I said: O Commander of the Believers, I said nothing; it is he who said it. So he left me. He said: Then he said: You intended to kill the inviolable and exceed the ruling. Then he said: In man are ten traits: nine good and one evil that spoils that evil one. Then he said: Beware the company of youths."
"258","259 - Narrated to us Ishaq ibn Ibrahim, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Ibn Uyaynah, from Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr, from Qabisah ibn Jabir, who said: We came to Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) and mentioned the like, and he inclined toward a man - by Allah, as if his face were a heart."
"259","260 - Narrated to us Abu Yazid al-Qaratisli, narrated to us Asad ibn Musa, narrated to us Damrah, from Uthman ibn Ata', from his father, who said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf (may Allah be pleased with him) would wear a shirt of coarse cotton to mid-shin, and his cloak would strike his buttocks."
"260","261 - Narrated to us Ahmad ibn Abd al-Rahim al-Barqi, Abd al-Malik ibn Hisham narrated to us, Ziyad ibn Abdullah narrated to us, from Ibn Ishaq, that Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf had fallen front teeth, was toothless-gapped, left-handed, lame; he was struck on the Day of Uhud, his teeth broken, wounded with twenty wounds or more, some in his leg, so he limped."
"261","The age of Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf and his death (may Allah be pleased with him). 262 - Narrated to us Abu al-Zinba', Yahya ibn Bukayr narrated to us, who said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf (may Allah be pleased with him) was born ten years after the Elephant, died in the year thirty-one or thirty-two, aged seventy-five. Uthman ibn Affan (may Allah be pleased with them both) prayed over him."
"262","263 / 1 - Narrated to us Abu Yazid al-Qaratisli, Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Sa'd narrated to us, from his father, from his grandfather, who said: I heard Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) on the day Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf died say: Go, son of Awf, for you have attained its choicest and preceded its turbid part."
"263","263 / 2 - Narrated to us Abu Yazid al-Qaratisli, Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Sa'd narrated to us, from his father, from his grandfather, who said: I heard Amr ibn al-As (may Allah be pleased with him) on the day Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf died say: Go, Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, for you have gone with your inward portion, diminishing nothing of it."
"264","264 - Narrated to us Abu Yazid al-Qaratisli, Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, Umarah ibn Zadhan narrated to us, from Thabit al-Bunani, from Anas ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: While A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) was in her house, she heard a sound by which Madinah shook. She said: What is this? They said: A caravan of Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf has come from Syria - seven hundred camels. A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) said: Indeed I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: I saw Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf entering Paradise crawling. That reached Abd al-Rahman, so he came to her and asked her about what reached him, and she told him. He said: I call you to witness that it with its loads, saddles, and saddlecloths is in the path of Allah."
"265","265 - Narrated to us Abu Yazid al-Qaratisli, Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, who said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf gave half his wealth in charity in the lifetime of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - four thousand - then gave forty thousand in charity, then gave forty thousand dinars in charity, then carried five hundred horses in the path of Allah, then carried fifteen hundred camels in the path of Allah. Most of his wealth was from trade."
"266","And what Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated, and mention of the disagreement in al-Zuhri's hadith on plague. 266 - Narrated to us Ahmad ibn Abd al-Wahhab ibn Najdah al-Hawti and Abu Zayd Ahmad ibn Yazid al-Hawti, both said: Abu al-Mughirah narrated to us, Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid ibn Tamim narrated to us, al-Zuhri narrated to us, from Salim, from his father, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: When plague falls in a land and you are in it, do not leave fleeing from it, and when it falls in a land and you are not in it, do not enter upon it. Abu al-Qasim said: Thus narrated it Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid ibn Tamim and Sufyan ibn Husayn, while Ibn Abi Dhi'b differed from them."
"267","267 - Narrated to us Umar ibn Hafs al-Sadusi, Asim ibn Ali narrated to us, Ibn Abi Dhi'b narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Salim ibn Abdullah ibn Amir ibn Rabi'ah, that Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf informed Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) while he was on the road to Syria when it reached him that plague was in it, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: This pain - or this illness - is a punishment with which those before you were punished. When it is in a land you are not in, do not descend upon it, and when it is in a land and you are in it, do not leave fleeing from it. He said: So Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) returned with the people that year."
"268","268 - Narrated to us Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Zayd ibn al-Khattab, from Abdullah ibn al-Harith ibn Nawfal, from Abdullah ibn Abbas, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: When you hear of it in a land, do not come to it, and when it falls in a land and you are in it, do not leave fleeing from it."
"269","269 - Narrated to us Abu Yazid al-Qaratisli, Abdullah ibn Abd al-Hakam narrated to us, Malik informed us, (H). And narrated to us Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz, al-Qa'nabi narrated to us, from Malik, from Ibn Shihab, from Abd al-Hamid ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Zayd ibn al-Khattab, from Abdullah ibn al-Harith ibn Nawfal, from Abdullah ibn Abbas, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: When you hear of it in a land, do not come to it, and when it falls in a land and you are in it, do not leave fleeing from it."
"270","270 - Narrated to us Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz, Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Ayyub, author of al-Maghazi, narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Sa'd narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, Ibn Shihab al-Zuhri narrated to me, from Abd al-Hamid ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Zayd ibn al-Khattab, from Abdullah ibn al-Harith ibn Nawfal, that Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: When you hear of this epidemic in a town, do not come to it, and when it falls and you are in it, do not leave fleeing from it."
"271","271 - Narrated to us Harun ibn Kamil al-Misri, Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, al-Layth narrated to me, Yunus narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, Abd al-Hamid ibn Abd al-Rahman informed me, that Abdullah ibn al-Harith narrated to him that Abdullah ibn Abbas narrated, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: When you hear of it in a land, do not come to it, and when it falls in a land and you are in it, let not fleeing from it expel you."
"272","272 - Narrated to us Fudayl ibn Muhammad al-Malti, Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Isma'il ibn Mujammi' narrated to us, from Ibn Shihab, from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah, from Ibn Abbas, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: When you hear of it in a land, do not come to it, and when it falls and you are in it, do not leave fleeing from it."
"273","273 - Narrated to us Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari, Abd al-Razzaq informed us, Ma'mar informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Amir ibn Sa'd, from Usamah ibn Zayd, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: This epidemic is a filth with which Allah destroyed some nations; some of it remains in the earth, coming at times and going at times. When it falls and you are in a land, do not leave it, and when you hear of it in a land, do not come to it."
"274","274 - Narrated to us Harun ibn Kamil al-Misri, Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, al-Layth narrated to me, Yunus narrated to me, (H). And narrated to us Isma'il ibn al-Hasan al-Khaffaf al-Misri, Ahmad ibn Salih narrated to us, Ibn Wahb informed me, Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Amir ibn Sa'd, from Usamah ibn Zayd, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: This pain - or illness - is a filth with which some nations before you were punished, then it remained after in the earth, going one time and coming another. Whoever hears of it in a land, let them not come to it, and whoever is in a land where it falls while he is in it, let not fleeing from it expel him. The wording is that of al-Layth's narration."
"275","275 - Narrated to us Mahmud ibn Muhammad al-Wasiti, Wahb ibn Baqiyyah narrated to us, Khalid informed us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ishaq, from al-Zuhri and Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir, from Amir ibn Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas, from Usamah ibn Zayd, who said: Plague was mentioned before the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: It is a filth that befell those before you - or a punishment that befell those before you. When you hear of it in a town, do not enter upon it, and when it falls and you are in a town, do not leave fleeing from it."
"276","276 - Narrated to us Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna, Musaddad narrated to us, Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, Ma'mar narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Amir ibn Sa'd, from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: This epidemic is something with which the nations before you were punished; some of it remains in the earth, falling at times and departing at times. When it falls in a land and you are in it, do not leave it, and when it falls in a land and you are not in it, do not enter upon it."
"277","277 - Narrated to us Zakariyya ibn Yahya al-Saji, Muhammad ibn Musa al-Harashi narrated to us, Hatim ibn Wardan narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ishaq, from al-Zuhri, from Amir ibn Sa'd, from Zayd ibn Thabit, who said: Plague was mentioned before the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: It is a filth that befell those before you. When you hear of it in a town, do not enter it upon it, and when it falls and you are in a town, do not leave fleeing from it."
"278","278 - Narrated to us Bakr ibn Sahl al-Dimyati, Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, al-Layth narrated to me, Hisham ibn Sa'd narrated to me, (H) and Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to me, Ishaq ibn Isma'il al-Talqani narrated to us, Ja'far narrated to us, Ja'far ibn Awn narrated to us, Hisham ibn Sa'd informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf (may Allah be pleased with him), that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When you hear of it in a land, do not enter upon it, and when it falls and you are in a land, do not leave fleeing from it."
"279","279 - Narrated to us Amr ibn Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn al-Ala' ibn Zibriq al-Himsi, my grandfather Ibrahim ibn al-Ala' narrated to me, my uncle al-Harith ibn al-Dahhak narrated to me, Mansur ibn al-Mu'tamir narrated to me, who said: I heard Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir narrate from Abi Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked: Which part of the night is most heard? He said: The last depth of the night. Then prayer is accepted until you pray Fajr, then no prayer until the sun is a spear's length or two. Then prayer is accepted until the shadow stands the standing of a spear, then no prayer until the sun declines. Then prayer is accepted until the sun is a spear's length or two, then no prayer until the sun sets. He said: Then he said: Any Muslim man who frees a Muslim person, it is his ransom from the Fire; every bone of his is recompensed with a bone of him. Any Muslim woman who frees a Muslim woman, she is her ransom from the Fire; every bone of her is recompensed with a bone of her. Any Muslim man who frees two Muslim women, they are his ransom from the Fire; two bones of them recompense a bone of him."
"280","280 - Narrated to us al-Miqdam ibn Dawud, Dhu'ayb ibn Imamah al-Sahmi narrated to us, Sulayman ibn Salim, freedman of Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, from his father, from his grandfather, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) missed a man of his companions and said: Where were you? I did not see you; did you not attend prayer? He said: Yes, but I came when people were settled, and I disliked stepping over people's necks. He said: You did well."
"281","281 - Narrated to us al-Miqdam ibn Dawud, Dhu'ayb ibn Imamah narrated to us, Sulayman ibn Salim narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Humayd, from his father, from his grandfather, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The worker, when employed, takes the right and gives the right, is like one striving in the path of Allah until he returns to his house."
"282","282 - Narrated to us al-Miqdam ibn Dawud, Dhu'ayb narrated to us, Sulayman ibn Salim narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Humayd, from his father, from his grandfather, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The midday prayer is of the night prayer. I asked Abd al-Rahman ibn Humayd about al-hajir; he said: When the sun declines."
"283","283 - Narrated to us al-Miqdam ibn Dawud, Dhu'ayb ibn Imamah narrated to us, Sulayman ibn Salim narrated to us, who said: I heard Abd al-Rahman ibn Humayd mention from his father, who said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf (may Allah be pleased with him) heard a man of Banu Hashim say: I am the closest of people to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: Other than you is closer to him than you, though you have his kinship."
"284","284 - Narrated to us Ja'far ibn al-Fadl al-Makhzumi al-Mu'addib, Abd al-Rahman ibn Abd al-Malik ibn Shaybah al-Hizami narrated to us, Muhammad ibn al-Ala' ibn Husayn al-Nabaqi al-Muttalibi narrated to me, who said: Al-Walid ibn Ibrahim ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf narrated to me, from his father, from his grandfather, who said: Umamah bint Abi al-As was in the throes of death, so Zaynab, daughter of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), sent to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying to him: My daughter is in her death throes. He sent to his daughter: To Allah belongs what He takes and His is what He leaves. She was in throes a second time, and she sent to him: My daughter is in her death throes. He sent to his daughter: To Allah belongs what He takes and His is what He leaves. Then came the third, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to her, and she brought the girl out to him; her soul rattled in her chest, and with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) were people of his companions. His eyes shed tears until he grasped his beard. He noticed them looking at him and said: What is with you that you look? They said: O Messenger of Allah, we saw you tender. He said: A mercy Allah (Mighty and Majestic) places where He wills; Allah shall tomorrow show mercy only to the merciful among His servants."
"285","285 - Narrated to us Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz, Abu Nu'aym Dirar ibn Surad narrated to us, Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad al-Darawardi narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Abd al-Aziz, from Ibn Shihab, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Miswar, from his father, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf (may Allah be pleased with him), His saying (Mighty and Majestic): Then He sent down upon you after grief security, slumber. He said: Sleep was cast upon us on the Day of Uhud."
"286","286 - Narrated to us Ali ibn Sa'id al-Razi, Ahmad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abdullah ibn Sa'd al-Dashtaki narrated to us, my father narrated to me, from his father, from Kharijah ibn Mus'ab, from Abdullah ibn Ata' ibn Yasar, from Muhammad ibn Zayd, from Abi Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Little understanding is better than much worship, and the best of your deeds are the easiest of them."
"287","287 - Narrated to us Hashim ibn Marthad al-Tabarani, Adam narrated to us, Ibn Abi Fudayk narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Zayd, from Mus'ab ibn Mus'ab, from Ibn Shihab, from Abi Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from his father, who said: Talhah spoke to Amir ibn Fuhayrah about something, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Gently, O Talhah, for he was present at Badr as you were present at it, and the best of you are the best of you to their freedmen."
"288","288 - Narrated to us Abd al-Warith ibn Ibrahim Abu Ubaydah al-Askari and Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna, both said: Isa ibn Ibrahim al-Biraki narrated to us, Afif ibn Salim narrated to us, Layth ibn Sa'd al-Misri narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Abi Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Satan - may Allah curse him - said: The owner of wealth shall never be safe from me from one of three: I come to him with them morning and evening: his taking wealth from unlawful source, his spending it in unlawful right, and I make it beloved to him so he withholds it from its right."
"289","8 - The lineage of Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas (may Allah be pleased with him). The name of Abu Waqqas is Malik ibn Uhayb ibn Abd Manaf ibn Zuhrah, known as Abu Ishaq, present at Badr. 289 - Narrated to us Abu Khalifah, Ibrahim ibn Bashshar al-Ramadi narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Ali ibn Zayd, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, from Sa'd (may Allah be pleased with him), that he came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, who am I? He said: Sa'd ibn Malik ibn Uhayb ibn Abd Manaf ibn Zuhrah; whoever says other than that, upon him is Allah's curse."
"290","290 - Narrated to us Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari, from Abd al-Razzaq, from al-Thawri, from Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr, from Jabir ibn Samurah, that Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) said to Sa'd: Such is the opinion of you, O Abu Ishaq."
"291","291 - Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami narrated to us, Ma'mar ibn Bakkar al-Sa'di narrated to us, Uthman ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, he said: Sa'd said: O Messenger of Allah, who am I? He said: You are Sa'd ibn Malik ibn Uhayb ibn Abd Manaf ibn Zuhrah; whoever says other than that, upon him is the curse of Allah."
"292","292 - Ahmad ibn Shahin al-Baghdadi narrated to us, Mus'ab ibn Abd Allah al-Zubayri narrated to us, he said: The mother of Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas: Hamnah bint Abi Sufyan ibn Umayyah ibn Abd Shams ibn Abd Manaf, and her mother was the daughter of Abu Sarh ibn Habib ibn Jadhimah ibn Nasr ibn Malik ibn Hisl ibn Amir ibn Lu'ayy ibn Ghalib."
"293","Description of Sa'd ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him. 293 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, al-Zubayr ibn Bakkar narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Imran said: Abd Allah ibn Ja'far narrated to me, from Isma'il ibn Muhammad ibn Sa'd, he said: Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas was curly-haired, hairy in body, dark-skinned and tall, flat-nosed."
"294","294 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, al-Zubayr ibn Bakkar narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn al-Mundhir narrated to us, from al-Waqidi, from Bukayr ibn Mismar, from A'ishah bint Sa'd, she said: My father was a short, stocky, stout man with a large head and coarse fingers, and he witnessed Badr."
"295","295 - Ahmad ibn Rushdin al-Misri narrated to us, Nu'aym ibn Hammad narrated to us, Rushdin ibn Sa'd narrated to us, from Yunus, from Ibn Shihab, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, that Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas used to dye with black."
"296","296 - Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Umar ibn Aban narrated to us, Sulaym ibn Muslim narrated to us, from Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Amir ibn Sa'd, that Sa'd used to dye with black."
"297","297 - Abu Yazid al-Qaratisisi narrated to us, Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, Yahya ibn Zakariyya ibn Abi Za'idah narrated to us, my father narrated to me, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu Ubaydah, from Abd Allah ibn Mas'ud, he said: We shared on the day of Badr, I and Ammar and Sa'd, in the voluntary spoils; Sa'd got two captives, while I and Ammar got nothing."
"298","298 - Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami narrated to us, Abu Kurayb narrated to us, Abu Usamah narrated to us, from Hashim ibn Hashim, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, he said: I heard Sa'd saying: No one became Muslim except on the day I became Muslim, and I remained seven days while I was one-third of Islam."
"299","Age and death of Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas, may Allah be pleased with him. 299 - Abd Allah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, my father narrated to us, Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, from his father, he said: I heard al-Nu'man ibn Rashid narrating from al-Zuhri, from Amir ibn Sa'd, he said: Sa'd was the last of the Muhajirun to die, may Allah be pleased with them. 300 - Abd Allah said: My father said: He died at the age of eighty-three, and died ten miles from Madinah, and was carried on men's necks to Madinah; Marwan was then the governor over it, and he became Muslim at the age of nineteen."
"300","301 - Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami narrated to us, he said: I heard Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Numayr saying: Sa'd died while Marwan was the governor of Madinah, and he prayed over him; he died in the year fifty-five."
"301","302 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, al-Zubayr ibn Bakkar narrated to us, he said: Sa'd died at al-Aqiq in his palace, ten miles from Madinah, and was carried on men's necks to Madinah; and it is said: he died at the age of seventy-some."
"302","303 - Abu al-Zinba' narrated to us, Yahya ibn Bukayr narrated to us, he said: Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas, may Allah be pleased with him, died at al-Aqiq and was carried to Madinah; Marwan ibn al-Hakam prayed over him. And he said: I became Muslim at the age of nineteen, and he died in the year fifty-five."
"303","304 - Abd Allah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, my father narrated to us, Nuh ibn Yazid narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Sa'd narrated to us, he said: Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas died in the time of Mu'awiyah, may Allah be pleased with him, after his first pilgrimage, at the age of eighty-three."
"304","305 - Muhammad ibn Ali al-Madini Fustuqah narrated to us, Abu Musa narrated to us, Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, my father narrated to me, he said: I heard al-Nu'man ibn Rashid narrating from al-Zuhri, from Amir ibn Sa'd, he said: Sa'd was the last of the Muhajirun to die, may Allah be pleased with them."
"305","306 - Abu Yazid al-Qaratisisi narrated to us, Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, Yahya ibn Zakariyya narrated to us, Isma'il narrated to us, from Qays, he said: Ibn Mas'ud had wealth owed by Sa'd, may Allah be pleased with them both, so Ibn Mas'ud said: Pay the wealth that is with you. Sa'd said: Woe to you, what have I to do with you? He said: Pay the wealth that is with you. Sa'd said: By Allah, I see you will meet evil from me; are you but Ibn Mas'ud, a slave from Banu Hudhayl? He said: Yes, by Allah I am Ibn Mas'ud, and you are the son of Hamnah. Hashim ibn Utbah said to them both: You are Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), people look at you. Sa'd threw a stick that was in his hand, then raised his hand and said: O Allah, Lord of the heavens. Abd Allah said to him: Say a word and do not curse. He fell silent. Then Sa'd said: By Allah, were it not for fear of Allah, I would supplicate against you a supplication that would not miss you."
"306","307 - Abu Muslim al-Kashshi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Ansari narrated to us, Ibn Awn narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Muhammad ibn al-Aswad informed me, from Amir ibn Sa'd, he said: While Sa'd was walking, he passed by a man who was reviling Ali, Talhah, and al-Zubayr. Sa'd said to him: You revile people for whom precedence from Allah has already been decreed; by Allah you will either stop reviling them or I shall supplicate to Allah Almighty against you. He said: You threaten me as if you were a prophet. Sa'd said: O Allah, this man reviles people for whom precedence from You has already been decreed, so make him an example today. A Bactrian camel came, people made way for it, and it trampled him. I saw people following Sa'd saying: Allah has answered for you, O Abu Ishaq."
"307","308 - Abu Yazid al-Qaratisisi narrated to us, Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr, from Jabir ibn Samurah, that the people of Kufah complained about Sa'd to Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, so he sent men to ask about him in Kufah. They came to no mosque of the people of Kufah but they praised him well and spoke good, until they came to a mosque of Banu Abs, where a man called Abu Sa'dah stood and said: Since you adjure us, he used not to go out with the detachment, nor judge justly in cases, nor distribute equally. Sa'd said: O Allah, if he is lying, blind his sight, prolong his poverty, and expose him to trials. Abd al-Malik said: I saw him accosting slave-girls in the streets; when they asked him: How are you, O Abu Sa'dah? He would say: Old, blind, poor, afflicted; the supplication of Sa'd struck me. Abu al-Qasim said: Abu Sa'dah is the grandfather of Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah."
"308","309 - Yusuf al-Qadi narrated to us, Amr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Shu'bah narrated to us, from Sa'd ibn Ibrahim, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, he said: A slave-girl of Sa'd called Zira went out wearing a new shirt; the wind uncovered her, so Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, struck her hard with the stick. Sa'd came to stop him, and he struck him with the stick. Sa'd went to supplicate against Umar, so Umar handed him the stick and said: Take retaliation. Sa'd pardoned Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both."
"309","310 - Muhammad ibn Abdus ibn Kamil al-Sarraj narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Bakkar narrated to us, Abd al-Hakim ibn Mansur narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr, he said: A Muslim man lampooned Sa'd, saying: Amat. That reached Sa'd, may Allah be pleased with him, so he raised his hand and said: O Allah, cut off his tongue and his hand from me however You will. He was struck on the day of al-Qadisiyyah; his tongue was cut and his hand was cut, and he was killed."
"310","311 - Mahmud ibn Muhammad al-Wasiti narrated to us, Zakariyya ibn Yahya Zahmawayh narrated to us, Ziyad ibn Abd Allah al-Bakka'i narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr, from Qabisah ibn Jabir al-Asadi, he said: A cousin of ours said on the day of al-Qadisiyyah: Amat. When his word reached Sa'd, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: O Allah, cut off from me his tongue and his hand. An arrow came and struck his mouth so he became mute, then his hand was cut in battle. Sa'd said: Carry me upon a door. He was brought out carried, then he uncovered his back, on which were sores, and told the people his excuse, so they excused him. Sa'd was not cowardly, and he said: I only did this because of what reached me of your words."
"311","312 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Ja'far ibn Sulayman, from Ikrimah ibn Khalid, that Sa'd said to his son when death came to him: My son, you will never meet anyone more sincere to you than me. When you want to pray, perfect your ablution, then pray a prayer after which you think you will not pray again. Beware of greed, for it is present poverty; hold to despair of people, for it is wealth; beware of any deed or word for which apology is needed; and do what you wish."
"312","313 - Abu Yazid al-Qaratisisi narrated to us, Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, Yahya ibn Zakariyya ibn Abi Za'idah narrated to us, Hashim ibn Hashim narrated to me, he said: I heard Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib saying: Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas said: No one became Muslim except on the day I became Muslim, and I remained seven days while I was one-third of Islam."
"313","314 - Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi narrated to us, Mu'awiyah ibn Amr narrated to us, Za'idah narrated to us, from Isma'il, from Qays, he said: I heard Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas saying: I am the first Muslim man to shoot an arrow in the path of Allah Almighty."
"314","315 - Abu Yazid al-Qaratisisi narrated to us, Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, Hatim ibn Isma'il narrated to us, from Bukayr ibn Mismar, from Amir ibn Sa'd, from his father, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) joined for him both his parents. He said: A man from the polytheists had scorched the Muslims, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Sa'd: Shoot, may my father and mother be ransom for you. He said: I drew an arrow without a head and hit his side; he fell and his private part was exposed, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) laughed until I saw his molars."
"315","316 - Muttalib ibn Shu'ayb al-Azdi narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated to us, al-Layth narrated to me, Uqayl narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, that when death came to Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas he called for a worn woolen cloak and said: Shroud me in it, for I met the polytheists in it on the day of Badr; I was keeping it only for this."
"316","317 - Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, Musaddad narrated to us, Yahya ibn Sa'id narrated to us, from Musa al-Juhani, Mus'ab ibn Sa'd narrated to us, he said: When my father prayed in the mosque he shortened and perfected bowing and prostration; when he prayed in the house he lengthened bowing, prostration, and prayer. I said: Father, when you pray in the mosque you shorten, and when alone in the house you lengthen. He said: Son, we are leaders who are followed."
"317","318 - Abu Yazid al-Qaratisisi narrated to us, Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, Yahya ibn Zakariyya ibn Abi Za'idah narrated to us, al-Mujalid narrated to me, from Amir, he said: It was said to Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas: When did you attain answered supplication? He said: On the day of Badr I was shooting before the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); I would place the arrow in the middle of the bow and say: O Allah, shake their feet, frighten their hearts, and do to them such and such. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would say: O Allah, answer Sa'd."
"318","319 - al-Hasan ibn Ali al-Tusi narrated to us, al-Zubayr ibn Bakkar narrated to us, Yahya ibn Muhammad ibn al-Dahhak al-Hizami narrated to me, from his father, he said: Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, stood on the pulpit of Kufah when the two arbitrators differed, and said: I had forbidden you this arbitration and you disobeyed me. A dark youth stood to him and said: By Allah, you did not forbid us, but you commanded us and destroyed us; when it brought what you dislike, you absolved yourself and laid your sin on us. Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, said to him: What have you to do with this speech, may Allah disgrace you; the congregation existed and you were obscure in it, and when strife appeared you rose in it like the sprouting of a goat's horn. Then he turned to the people and said: By Allah, what a station Sa'd ibn Malik and Abd Allah ibn Umar took; by Allah, if it was a sin it is small and forgiven, and if it was good it is great and thanked."
"319","320 - Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi narrated to us, Mu'awiyah ibn Amr narrated to us, Za'idah narrated to us, Husayn ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, Amr ibn Maymun narrated to us, he said: When Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, was struck, he began to bequeath regarding the people of the council: If it falls to Sa'd, so be it; otherwise let the one appointed caliph seek his help, for I did not remove him from incapacity nor treachery."
"320","321 - Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, Musaddad narrated to us, Yahya ibn Sa'id narrated to us, from Abu Hayyan al-Taymi, he said: I heard Abayah ibn Rifa'ah ibn Rafi' saying: It reached Umar that Sa'd had taken a door, then said: The faint sound is cut off. Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, sent to him and burned it. Then Muhammad ibn Maslamah took his hand and brought him out, saying: Sit here for the people. Sa'd apologized to him and swore he did not speak the word that reached the Commander of the Believers."
"321","322 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, he said: It was said to Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas: Will you not fight? You are of the people of the council, and you are more entitled to this matter than others. He said: I will not fight until you bring me a sword with two eyes, a tongue, and two lips that knows the believer from the disbeliever; I have already struggled while I knew struggle."
"322","323 - Ahmad ibn al-Qasim ibn Musawir al-Jawhari narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Sulayman al-Wasiti narrated to us, Abu Usamah narrated to us, from Mujalid, from al-Sha'bi, from Jabir, he said: We were with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas came. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: This is my maternal uncle; let a man show me his maternal uncle."
"323","Among what Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas, may Allah be pleased with him, transmitted: Chapter of prohibition and severity regarding reviling Muslims, deserting them, fighting them, and other than that. 324 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Ma'mar, from Abu Ishaq, from Umar ibn Sa'd, he said: Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas narrated to us, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Killing a Muslim is disbelief, reviling him is wickedness, and it is not lawful for a Muslim to desert his brother for more than three days."
"324","325 - Abu Yazid al-Qaratisisi narrated to us, Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, Rawh ibn Musafir narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Muhammad ibn Sa'd, from his father, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Reviling a Muslim is wickedness and fighting him is disbelief."
"325","326 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abi Nu'aym al-Wasiti narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Sa'd narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Amir ibn Sa'd, from his father, he said: A Bedouin came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: My father used to uphold kinship, and used to do such and such; where is he? He said: In the Fire. The Bedouin seemed to feel distress at that. He said: O Messenger of Allah, where is your father? He said: Wherever you pass by the grave of a disbeliever, give him tidings of the Fire. The Bedouin later became Muslim and said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) burdened me with toil; I never pass by the grave of a disbeliever but I give him tidings of the Fire."
"326","Chapter on honoring Quraysh and other than that. 327 - Sa'id ibn Muhammad ibn al-Mughirah al-Misri narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Sulayman al-Wasiti narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Mujabbar narrated to us, from Ibn Shihab, from Amir ibn Sa'd, from his father, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever intends humiliation of Quraysh, Allah will humiliate him."
"327","328 - Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami narrated to us, Ma'mar ibn Bakkar al-Sa'di narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Sa'd narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Amir ibn Sa'd, from his father, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Ali, may Allah be pleased with him: You are to me in the position of Harun to Musa."
"328","329 - Sa'id ibn al-Mughirah al-Misri narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Sulayman al-Wasiti narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Uthman narrated to us, from al-Abbas ibn Dharih, from Muhammad ibn Sa'd, from his father, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Part of happiness is a righteous wife, a good dwelling, and a good mount; and part of misery is a bad wife, a bad dwelling, and a bad mount."
"329","330 - Muhammad ibn al-Hasan ibn Kaysan al-Missisi narrated to us, Habban ibn Hilal narrated to us, (ha) and Abu Muslim al-Kashshi narrated to us, Muslim ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, both said: Salim ibn Hayyan narrated to us, from Ikrimah ibn Khalid, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from his father, he said: The plague was mentioned before the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he said: It is a punishment that struck those before you; if it is in a land while you are in it, do not leave it, and if it is in it, do not enter it."
"330","331 - Ali ibn Sa'id al-Razi narrated to us, Abd al-Rahman ibn Salamah al-Razi, scribe of Salamah, narrated to us, Salamah ibn al-Fadl narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq al-Hamdani, from Mus'ab ibn Sa'd, from Sa'd, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: Three verses of the Book of Allah Almighty were revealed concerning me: the prohibition of wine was revealed; I was drinking with a man, I argued with him and he argued with me, I became quarrelsome toward him and wounded him, so Allah Almighty revealed: O you who believe, wine and gambling are only... to His word: Will you then desist. And revealed concerning me: We enjoined man kindness to his parents, his mother bore him with hardship... to the end of the verse. And revealed: O you who believe, when you confer privately with the Messenger, offer charity before your private talk. I offered a barley grain, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You are ascetic. Then the other was revealed: Are you afraid to offer charities before your private talk... the whole verse."
"331","332/1 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Ishaq ibn Muhammad al-Farwi narrated to us, Ubaydah bint Nabil narrated to us, from A'ishah bint Sa'd, from Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: What is between my house and my prayer place is a garden from the gardens of Paradise."
"332","332/2 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Ishaq ibn Muhammad al-Farwi narrated to us, Ubaydah bint Nabil narrated to us, from A'ishah bint Sa'd, from Sa'd, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to drink standing."
"333","333 - al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari and Ibrahim ibn Hashim al-Baghawi narrated to us, both said: Umayyah ibn Bistam narrated to us, Yazid ibn Zuray' narrated to us, from Isra'il, from Hakim ibn Jubayr, from Ali ibn Husayn, Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib narrated to me, that Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas narrated to him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Ali: You are to me in the position of Harun to Musa."
"334","334 - al-Hasan ibn al-Abbas al-Razi narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Dahir al-Razi narrated to us, my father narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Salim ibn Abi al-Ja'd, from Abu Abd Allah al-Jadali, he said: I heard Sa'd, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Ali: You are to me in the position of Harun to Musa, except that there is no prophet after me."
"335","Lineage of Sa'id ibn Zayd, may Allah be pleased with him. 335 - Musa ibn Zakariyya al-Tustari narrated to us, Shabab al-Usfuri narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Zayd ibn Amr ibn Nufayl ibn Abd al-Uzza ibn Rabah ibn Abd Allah ibn Qurt ibn Razah ibn Adi ibn Ka'b, kunya Abu al-A'war; his mother: Fatimah bint Na'jah ibn Umayyah ibn Khuwaylid of Khuza'ah."
"336","Description of Sa'id ibn Zayd, may Allah be pleased with him. 336 - Muhammad ibn Ali al-Madini Fustuqah narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Sa'id al-Jawhari narrated to us, from al-Waqidi, he said: Sa'id ibn Zayd was tall, dark-skinned, hairy."
"337","337 - Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Sadaqah narrated to us, he said: I heard Abu Hafs Amr ibn Ali saying: Sa'id ibn Zayd was dark, tall, hairy."
"338","338 - Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Khalid al-Harrani narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us, from Abu al-Aswad, from Urwah, he said: Sa'id ibn Zayd ibn Amr ibn Nufayl arrived from Syria after the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had returned from Badr; he spoke to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who struck for him his share. He said: And my reward, O Messenger of Allah, they claim? He said: And your reward."
"339","339 - al-Hasan ibn Harun ibn Sulayman al-Asbahani narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Musayyibi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Fulayh narrated to us, from Musa ibn Uqbah, from Ibn Shihab, he said: Sa'id ibn Zayd arrived from Syria after the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had arrived from Badr; he spoke to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about his share, who said to him: Your share. He said: And my reward, O Messenger of Allah? He said: And your reward."
"340","Age and death of Sa'id ibn Zayd, may Allah be pleased with him. 340 - Abu al-Zinba' Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, Yahya ibn Bukayr narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Zayd ibn Amr ibn Nufayl died in the year fifty-one at the age of seventy-some; he was buried in Madinah, died at al-Aqiq; Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas and Ibn Umar descended into his grave; his kunya was Abu al-A'war."
"341","341 - Ahmad ibn Rushdin al-Misri narrated to us, Nu'aym ibn Hammad narrated to us, Ibn al-Mubarak informed us, Abd Allah ibn Ja'far informed us, from Zayd ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Sa'id ibn Zayd, that Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas washed Sa'id ibn Zayd at al-Sajarah."
"342","342 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Arim Abu al-Nu'man narrated to us, Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, that Arwa bint Uways sought help from Marwan against Sa'id ibn Zayd, saying: He stole my land and added it to his land. Sa'id said: I would not steal from it after hearing the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Whoever steals a handspan of land will be collared with it to seven earths. He said: I will not ask you after this. Sa'id said: O Allah, if she is lying, take her sight and kill her in her land. Her sight went and she fell into a pit in her land and died."
"343","343 - Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami narrated to us, he said: I heard Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Numayr saying: Sa'id ibn Zayd died in the year fifty-one in Madinah."
"344","344 - Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami narrated to us, Musarrif ibn Amr al-Yami narrated to us, Abdah ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Ubayd Allah ibn Umar, from Abd al-Jabbar, from his brother, from A'ishah bint Sa'd, she said: Sa'd washed Sa'id ibn Zayd at al-Aqiq, then they carried him and brought him; Sa'd came walking until when he reached his house he entered and bathed, then came out and said: I did not bathe because of washing Sa'id; I only bathed because of the heat."
"345","345 - Mahmud ibn Muhammad al-Wasiti narrated to us, Wahb ibn Baqiyyah narrated to us, Khalid informed us, from Ata ibn al-Sa'ib, from Muharib ibn Dithar, from Sa'id ibn Zayd ibn Amr ibn Nufayl, he said: Mu'awiyah sent to Marwan ibn al-Hakam in Madinah to pledge allegiance for his son Yazid. A man of the people of Syria said: What detains you? He said: Until Sa'id ibn Zayd comes and pledges, for he is the noblest of the townspeople; when he pledges, the people will pledge."
"346","346 - Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Yusuf al-Firyabi narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Mansur, from Hilal ibn Yasaf, from Abd Allah ibn Zalim, from Sa'id ibn Zayd, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There will be trials after me, in which there will be such and such. We said: If we reach that, are we destroyed? He said: Killing suffices my Companions."
"347","347 - Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami narrated to us, Abu Kurayb narrated to us, Ubayd ibn Sa'id narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Mansur, from Hilal ibn Yasaf, from Fulan ibn Hayyan, from Abd Allah ibn Zalim, from Sa'id ibn Zayd, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"348","348 - Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami narrated to us, Abu Kurayb narrated to us, Firdaws al-Ash'ari narrated to us, from Mas'ud ibn Sulayman, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from Hilal ibn Yasaf, from Ibn Zalim, from Sa'id ibn Zayd, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mentioned trials. We said: O Messenger of Allah, is everyone afflicted in them destroyed? He said: Killing suffices you."
"349","349 - Abd Allah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, my father narrated to us, (ha) and Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, both said: Abu Usamah narrated to us, Mis'ar narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Maysarah, from Hilal ibn Yasaf, from Abd Allah ibn Zalim, from Sa'id ibn Zayd, he said: He mentioned a trial, meaning the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), like pieces of dark night. He said: People will go into it with swift going. It was said: All of them destroyed? He said: Killing suffices them."
"350","Among what Sa'id ibn Zayd, may Allah be pleased with him, transmitted. 350 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Raja narrated to us, al-Mas'udi informed us, from Nufayl ibn Hisham ibn Sa'id ibn Zayd, from his father, from his grandfather, he said: Waraqah ibn Nawfal and Zayd ibn Amr went seeking the religion until they passed through Syria. Waraqah became Christian; as for Zayd, it was said to him: What you seek is before you. He went until he came to Mosul, where there was a monk. He said: From where comes the traveler? He said: From the house of Ibrahim. He said: What do you seek? He said: The religion. He offered him Christianity but he refused to accept, saying: I have no need of it. He said: What you seek will appear in your land. He set out saying: At Your service, truly, truly, in worship and servitude; good I seek, not worldly gain; is a migrant like one who merely speaks? I seek refuge in what Ibrahim sought refuge in while standing; my nose is in dust for You, O Allah, abased; however much You burden me, I shall bear. Then he would fall prostrating toward the Ka'bah. He said: Zayd ibn Amr passed by the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and Zayd ibn Harithah while they were eating from a spread of theirs; they invited him. He said: Nephew, I do not eat of what is slaughtered upon idols. He said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was never seen eating of what is slaughtered upon idols from that day until he was sent. He said: Sa'id ibn Zayd came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, Zayd was as you saw, or as reached you; seek forgiveness for him. He said: Yes; he sought forgiveness for him, for he will be raised on the Day of Resurrection as a nation alone."
"351","351 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, Abd al-Salam ibn Harb narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Yazid ibn Yuhannis, from Sa'id ibn Zayd ibn Nufayl, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) embraced Hasan and said: O Allah, I have loved him, so love him."
"352","352 - al-Hasan ibn Jarir al-Suri narrated to us, Sulayman ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Dimashqi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Masruq al-Kufi narrated to us, from al-Walid ibn Abd Allah ibn Jumay', from Abu al-Tufayl, from Sa'id ibn Zayd, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Whoever is killed defending his wealth is a martyr."
"353","353 - Bakr ibn Sahl al-Dimyati narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated to us, al-Layth narrated to me, Hisham ibn Sa'd narrated to me, from Muhammad ibn Zayd ibn al-Muhajir, that Asim ibn Ubayd Allah ibn Asim informed him, from his grandfather Asim ibn Umar, that he heard Sa'id ibn Zayd ibn Amr ibn Nufayl saying: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Whoever fights defending his wealth until killed is a martyr."
"354","354 - Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Khalid al-Harrani narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Zayd ibn al-Muhajir, that he heard Abu Ghatfan ibn Tarif al-Murri informing from Sa'id ibn Zayd, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Whoever is killed defending his wealth is a martyr."
"355","355 - Yahya ibn Ayyub al-Allaf al-Misri narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ja'far narrated to us, al-Ala ibn Abd al-Rahman informed me, al-Abbas ibn Sahl ibn Sa'd informed me, that he heard Sa'id ibn Zayd ibn Amr ibn Nufayl saying: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Whoever wrongfully takes a handspan of land, Allah will collar him with it on the Day of Resurrection from seven earths."
"356","356 - Ahmad ibn Muhammad al-Khuza'i narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Bukayr al-Hadrami narrated to us, Thabit ibn al-Walid ibn Abd Allah ibn Jumay' al-Qurashi narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Abu al-Tufayl narrated to me, from Sa'id ibn Zayd, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while upon Hira; it shook, so he struck it with his foot, then said: Be still, Hira, for there is none upon you but a prophet, a truthful one, or a martyr. And these people were with him: Abu Bakr, Umar, Uthman, Ali, Talhah, al-Zubayr, Sa'd, Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, and I, meaning himself."
"357","357 - Abu Zayd al-Hawti and Abu Zur'ah narrated to us, both said: Abu al-Yaman narrated to us, Shu'ayb ibn Abi Hamzah informed us, Abd Allah ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Husayn narrated to me, Nawfal ibn Musahiq narrated to us, from Sa'id ibn Zayd, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Among the worst usury is a man's stretching in the honor of a Muslim without right; and this kinship is a branch from the Merciful: whoever cuts it, Allah forbids him Paradise."
"358","Lineage of Abu Ubaydah ibn al-Jarrah, may Allah be pleased with him. 358 - Muhammad ibn Ali al-Madini Fustuqah narrated to us, al-Husayn ibn Ali ibn Yazid al-Suda'i narrated to us, Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim ibn Sa'd narrated to us, he said: I heard my father, from Ibn Ishaq, he said: Abu Ubaydah ibn al-Jarrah is Amir ibn Abd Allah ibn al-Jarrah ibn Hilal ibn Uhayb ibn Dabbah ibn al-Harith ibn Fihr; he left no offspring. The mother of Abu Ubaydah: Umm Ghanm bint Jabir ibn Abd ibn al-Ala ibn Amir ibn Umayrah ibn Wadi'ah ibn al-Harith ibn Fihr."
"359","359 - Ubayd ibn Ghannam narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu Ubayd is Amir ibn Abd Allah ibn al-Jarrah."
"360","Killing by Abu Ubaydah, may Allah be pleased with him, of his father on the day of Badr. 360 - Abu Yazid al-Qaratisisi narrated to us, Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, Damrah narrated to us, from Ibn Shawdhab, he said: The father of Abu Ubaydah kept confronting Abu Ubaydah on the day of Badr, and Abu Ubaydah kept avoiding him; when he persisted, Abu Ubaydah aimed for him and killed him. Allah Almighty revealed concerning him this verse when he killed his father: You will not find a people who believe in Allah and the Last Day... to the end of the verse."
"361","361 - Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Khalid al-Harrani narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us, from Abu al-Aswad, from Urwah, he said: Among Banu al-Harith ibn Fihr who witnessed Badr: Abu Ubaydah ibn al-Jarrah."
"362","362 - Abd Allah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Muhammad ibn Ja'far narrated to us, Shu'bah narrated to us, from Simak ibn Harb, from Iyad al-Ash'ari, he said: Abu Ubaydah said: Who will race me? A youth said: I will, if you do not get angry. He said: He outran him; I saw the two braids of Abu Ubaydah bouncing behind him upon an Arabian horse."
"363","Age and death of Abu Ubaydah, may Allah be pleased with him. 363 - Abu al-Zinba' Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, Yahya ibn Bukayr narrated to us, he said: Abu Ubaydah, may Allah be pleased with him, died in the plague of Amwas in the year eighteen, at the age of fifty-eight; he witnessed Badr at the age of forty-one; and it is said Mu'adh ibn Jabal prayed over him."
"364","364 - Abu Yazid al-Qaratisisi narrated to us, Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, Abd al-Hamid ibn Bahram narrated to us, from Shahr ibn Hawshab, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ghanm al-Ash'ari, from al-Harith ibn Umayrah al-Harithi, that Mu'adh ibn Jabal, may Allah be pleased with him, was struck with plague, and kept sending al-Harith ibn Umayrah to Abu Ubaydah ibn al-Jarrah asking how he was. Abu Ubaydah showed him a plague sore that had come out on his palm; its matter seemed grave in al-Harith's mind, and he feared it when he saw it. Abu Ubaydah swore to him by Allah that he would not love red camels in exchange for it."
"365","365 - Yahya ibn Uthman ibn Salih narrated to us, Asbagh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, Ibn Wahb narrated to us, from Yunus, from Ibn Shihab, he said: Syria was under two commanders: Abu Ubaydah ibn al-Jarrah, and Yazid ibn Abi Sufyan. Abu Ubaydah died, and appointed as successor his maternal uncle Iyad ibn Ghanm, one of Banu al-Harith ibn Fihr; Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, confirmed him. Then Iyad died, so he appointed in his place Sa'id ibn Amir ibn Hidhyam; then Sa'id ibn Amir died, so he appointed in his place Umayr ibn Sa'd."
"366","Among what Abu Ubaydah ibn al-Jarrah, may Allah be pleased with him, transmitted: Chapter on hoping forgiveness for one who prays the dawn prayer in congregation on Friday. 366 - Yahya ibn Ayyub al-Allaf al-Misri and Ahmad ibn Hammad ibn Zughbah narrated to us, both said: Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, Yahya ibn Ayyub narrated to me, from Ubayd Allah ibn Zahr, from Ali ibn Yazid, from al-Qasim, from Abu Umamah, from Abu Ubaydah ibn al-Jarrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No prayer is superior to the dawn prayer on Friday in congregation; I consider none who attends it among you but forgiven."
"367","Chapter on people's corruption when wines appear and silk and private parts are deemed lawful. 367 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Yunus narrated to us, al-Fudayl ibn Iyad narrated to us, from Layth, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Sabit, from Abu Tha'labah al-Khushani, from Mu'adh and Abu Ubaydah, both said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: This matter began as mercy and prophethood, then will be mercy and caliphate, then will be biting kingship, then will be tyranny and despotism and corruption on earth, deeming silk, private parts, and wines lawful; they will be sustained and aided upon that until they meet Allah Almighty."
"368","368 - Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami narrated to us, Abu Kurayb narrated to us, Firdaws al-Ash'ari narrated to us, Mas'ud ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from a man of Quraysh, from Abu Tha'labah, he said: I met the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, entrust me to a man of good teaching. He entrusted me to Abu Ubaydah ibn al-Jarrah, then said: I have entrusted you to a man who will teach you and discipline you well. I came to Abu Ubaydah ibn al-Jarrah while he and Bashir ibn Sa'd, father of al-Nu'man ibn Bashir, were conversing; when they saw me they fell silent. I said: O Abu Ubaydah, by Allah, this is not how the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) told me. He said: You came while we were discussing a hadith we heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); sit until we narrate to you. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Among you is prophethood, then there will be caliphate upon the method of prophethood, then there will be kingship and despotism."
"369","Chapter of alif: those named Usamah. 11 - Usamah ibn Zayd ibn Harithah, beloved of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), kunya Abu Muhammad, also said Abu Zayd. 369 - Khalaf ibn Amr al-Ukbari narrated to us, Mu'alla ibn Mahdi al-Mawsili narrated to us, Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Umar ibn Abi Salamah, from his father, Usamah ibn Zayd narrated to me, he said: I passed by the mosque, and Ali and al-Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, were sitting. They said: O Usamah, seek permission for us from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). I said: O Messenger of Allah, Ali and al-Abbas are at the door, wanting to enter upon you. He said: Do you know what brought them? I said: No, by Allah, O Messenger of Allah, I do not know what brought them. He said: But I know what brought them; admit them. They entered upon him, and Ali said to him: O Messenger of Allah, we came to ask you: Which of your family is dearest to you? He said: Fatimah bint Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Ali said: By Allah, O Messenger of Allah, I am not asking you about your family. He said: The dearest of my family to me is he upon whom Allah bestowed favor and I bestowed favor: Usamah. He said: Then who, O Messenger of Allah? He said: Then you. Al-Abbas said: You made your uncle last of them? He said: Ali preceded you in migration."
"370","370 - Abu Khalifah narrated to us, Muslim ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, Qurrah ibn Khalid narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Sirin narrated to me, he said: A palm tree reached a thousand dirhams in the time of Uthman ibn Affan, may Allah be pleased with him. Usamah set out to a palm tree, pierced it and took out its heart, and fed it to his mother. They said to him: What carried you to this, while you see the palm tree has reached a thousand? He said: My mother asked me for it; she never asks me anything I can do but I give her."
"371","371 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, from Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: Usamah ibn Zayd was called by the title of commander until he died; they say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) appointed him, then did not remove him until he died."
"372","372 - Abd Allah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, Hudbah ibn Khalid narrated to me, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Musa ibn Uqbah, from Salim, from his father, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Usamah is the dearest of people to me."
"373","Lineage, age, and death of Usamah ibn Zayd. 373 - Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Khalid al-Harrani narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us, from Abu al-Aswad, from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, with his lineage: Usamah ibn Zayd ibn Harithah ibn Sharahil ibn Ka'b ibn Abd al-Uzza ibn Yazid ibn Imri' al-Qays al-Kalbi; Allah bestowed favor upon him, and His Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"374","374 - Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Amr narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Shu'ayb ibn al-Habhab narrated to us, he said: I heard our elders saying: The inscription of Usamah ibn Zayd's ring was: Beloved of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"375","375 - Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn al-Hakam ibn Abi Ziyad al-Qatawani narrated to us, Ishaq ibn Sulayman al-Razi narrated to us, from Abu Sinan, from Abu Ishaq, he said: I saw people among the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wearing their lower garments to mid-calf; he mentioned Ibn Umar, Zayd ibn Arqam, Usamah ibn Zayd, and al-Bara ibn Azib, may Allah be pleased with them."
"376","Among what Usamah ibn Zayd, may Allah be pleased with him, transmitted. 376 - Hafs ibn Umar al-Raqqi narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Ja'far al-Raqqi narrated to us, Ubayd Allah ibn Amr narrated to us, from Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Aqil, from Muhammad ibn Usamah ibn Zayd, from his father, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) clothed him with a Coptic garment from what Dihyah al-Kalbi had gifted him; I clothed my wife with it. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Why do you not wear the Coptic garment? I said: I clothed my wife with it. He said: Order her to put a lining under it, for I fear it describes her bones."
"377","377 - Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, Ali ibn al-Madini narrated to us, Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim ibn Sa'd narrated to us, my father narrated to me, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Sa'id ibn Ubayd ibn al-Sabbaq, from Muhammad ibn Usamah ibn Zayd, from his father, he said: When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) became gravely ill, I descended and people descended to Madinah; I entered upon him while he had fallen silent and could not speak; he kept raising his hand to the sky and pouring it upon me, so I knew he was supplicating for me."
"378","378 - Ahmad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Iqal narrated to us, Abu Ja'far al-Nufayli narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Yazid ibn Abd Allah ibn Qusayt, from Muhammad ibn Usamah ibn Zayd, from his father, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Ja'far: Your character resembles mine; you are from me. And you, O Ali, are from me and the father of my child."
"379","379 - Khalaf ibn Amr al-Ukbari narrated to us, Mu'alla ibn Mahdi narrated to us, Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Umar ibn Abi Salamah, from his father, from Usamah ibn Zayd, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"380","380 - Ibrahim ibn Suwayd al-Shibami narrated to us, Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, Ma'mar informed us, from al-Zuhri, Ata, freedman of Siba', informed me, from Usamah ibn Zayd, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he came to Jam', prayed there Maghrib and Isha."
"381","381 - Muhammad ibn Abdus narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Umar ibn Aban narrated to us, Ya'la ibn Ubayd narrated to us, al-A'mash informed us, from Abu Zibyan, from Usamah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent a detachment to al-Huraqat; the hadith."
"382","382 - Bishr ibn Musa narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Yazid Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Muqri narrated to us, Haywah ibn Shurayh narrated to us, Ayyash ibn Abbas narrated to us, that Abu al-Nadr informed him, that Amir ibn Sa'd narrated to him, that Usamah ibn Zayd informed him, he said: A man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: I practice withdrawal; and he mentioned the hadith."
"383","383 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Amir ibn Sa'd, from Usamah ibn Zayd, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: This plague is a punishment; and he mentioned the hadith."
"384","384 - Idris ibn Ja'far al-Attar narrated to us, Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Amr informed us, from Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir, from Amir ibn Sa'd, from Usamah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: This plague is a punishment; and he mentioned the hadith."
"385","385 - Muttalib ibn Shu'ayb al-Azdi narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated to us, al-Layth narrated to me, Uqayl narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, Urwah ibn al-Zubayr informed me, that Usamah ibn Zayd informed him, he said: I rode with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) upon a donkey with a saddle upon a velvet cloth; and he mentioned the hadith in full."
"386","386 - Idris ibn Ja'far al-Attar narrated to us, Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, Yahya ibn Sa'id informed us, from Musa ibn Uqbah, from Kurayb, from Usamah ibn Zayd, that he moved with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the evening of Arafah; he came to the narrow pass, made his camel kneel, relieved himself, then came and began to perform ablution while Usamah poured upon him. He said: O Messenger of Allah, the prayer. He said: The prayer place is ahead of you."
"387","387 - al-Abbas ibn al-Fadl al-Asfati narrated to us, Khalid ibn Yazid al-Umari narrated to us, Ibn Abi Dhi'b narrated to us, from al-Harith ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Kurayb, from Usamah, he said: I entered upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with gloom, and said: What is with you, O Messenger of Allah? He said: Jibril, peace be upon him, promised me he would come to me and has not come to me for three days. He said: And there was a dog. Usamah said: I placed my hands upon my head and shouted. He said: What is with you, O Usamah? I said: A dog. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered it killed. Then Jibril came to him and he said: Why did you not come to me, while when you promised me you never broke it? He said: We do not enter a house in which there is a dog or images."
"388","388 - Bakr ibn Sahl narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Muhajir, from Sulayman ibn Musa, Kurayb narrated to me, that Usamah ibn Zayd informed him, he said: The Messenger of Allah said: Is there none hastening to Paradise? It is, by the Lord of the Ka'bah, a light glittering, a fragrant basil swaying, a flowing river, a beautiful wife in a garden, in lasting bliss."
"389","389 - Abu Zur'ah Abd al-Rahman ibn Amr al-Dimashqi narrated to us, Abu al-Yaman al-Hakam ibn Nafi' narrated to us, Shu'ayb ibn Abi Hamzah informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, from Usamah ibn Zayd, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his Companions used to pardon the polytheists and the People of the Book as Allah Almighty commanded them, and be patient upon harm. Allah Almighty said: You shall surely hear from those given the Book before you and from those who associate much harm, the verse. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to interpret pardon as Allah commanded him, until Allah permitted regarding them. When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) raided Badr and Allah killed whom He killed of the disbelievers among the chiefs of Quraysh, Ibn Ubayy and those with him of the polytheists said: This is a matter that has turned in his favor; they pledged the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) upon Islam, and became Muslim."
"390","390 - Abu Aqil Anas ibn Salm al-Khawlani narrated to us, Abu al-Asbagh Abd al-Aziz ibn Yahya al-Harrani narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from al-Zuhri, from Urwah, from Usamah ibn Zayd, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out visiting Abd Allah ibn Ubayy in his illness in which he died, and I was with him; he recognized death in him and said: I used to forbid you love of the Jews. As'ad ibn Zurarah said: He died, so what? When he died, his son Abd Allah came to him and said: Abd Allah has died; give me your shirt to shroud him in. He removed his shirt and clothed him with it."
"391","391 - Abu Husayn al-Qadi narrated to us, Yahya al-Himmani narrated to us, Hushaym narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Ali ibn al-Husayn, from Amr ibn Uthman, from Usamah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): A disbeliever does not inherit a Muslim, nor a Muslim a disbeliever, nor do people of two religions inherit from one another."
"392","392 - Abu Husayn narrated to us, Yahya al-Himmani narrated to us, Khalid al-Wasiti narrated to us, from Ata ibn al-Sa'ib, from Abu Abd al-Rahman, from Usamah, he said: I thrust a spear into a man while he was saying: There is no god but Allah. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Usamah: What will you do with 'There is no god but Allah' on the Day of Resurrection? He said that repeatedly until I wished I had not become Muslim before that hour."
"393","393 - Abu Husayn al-Qadi narrated to us, Yahya al-Himmani narrated to us, Qays ibn al-Rabi' narrated to us, from Jami' ibn Shaddad, from Kulthum al-Khuza'i, from Usamah ibn Zayd, he said: I sought permission for some Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he permitted them; he had covered his head with a Ma'afiri cloak; he uncovered his head then said: May Allah curse the Jews, they took the graves of their prophets as mosques."
"394","394 - Abu Husayn narrated to us, Yahya narrated to us, Abu Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Abu Zibyan, from Usamah: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent us to al-Huraqat; and he mentioned the hadith."
"395","395 - Abu Husayn narrated to us, Yahya narrated to us, Abu Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Abu Wa'il, from Usamah: I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: A man will be brought; and he mentioned the hadith."
"396","396 - Abu Husayn narrated to us, Yahya narrated to us, Abu Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Umarah ibn Umayr, from Abu al-Sha'tha, from Ibn Umar, from Usamah, that he saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) enter the House; the hadith."
"397","397 - Abu Husayn narrated to us, Yahya narrated to us, Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid narrated to us, from his father, from Ata ibn Yasar, from Usamah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wiped over the two leather socks."
"398","398 - Abu Husayn narrated to us, Yahya narrated to us, Ali ibn Mushir narrated to us, from Asim, from Abu Uthman, from Usamah, he said: The daughters of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent; the hadith."
"399","399 - Abu al-Husayn narrated to us, Yahya narrated to us, Yahya ibn Zakariyya ibn Abi Za'idah narrated to us, from Uthman ibn Hakim, from Ibn Aflah, freedman of Abu Ayyub, from Usamah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Allah Almighty does not love the obscene, foul-mouthed one."
"400","400 - Abu Husayn narrated to us, Yahya narrated to us, Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Salih ibn Abi al-Akhdar, from al-Zuhri, from Urwah, from Usamah: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered me to raid the sons of Sabah and burn."
"401","401 - Mus'ab ibn Ibrahim ibn Hamzah al-Zubayri narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Ibrahim ibn Sa'd narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, Ibn Diri, freedman of Usamah, narrated to me, from Usamah, that a man arrived from some countryside, pain seized him, and he returned. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I hope its mountain pass will not loom upon us, meaning the pass of Madinah."
"402","402 - Muhammad ibn Abdus ibn Kamil narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Umar ibn Aban narrated to us, Ya'la ibn Ubayd narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Abu Wa'il, from Usamah, he said: I shall never say to a man: You are the best of people, after hearing the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: A man will be brought on the Day of Resurrection; the hadith."
"403","403 - Abd Allah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Ya'la ibn Ubayd narrated to us, from al-Ajlah, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from Ibrahim ibn Sa'd, from Usamah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: This plague is a punishment and torment with which those before you were tormented; if it is in a land, do not leave it, and if it falls in a land while you are not in it, do not enter it."
"404","404 - Ahmad ibn Ali al-Barbahari narrated to us, Zakariyya ibn Adi narrated to us, (ha) and al-Miqdam ibn Dawud narrated to us, Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, Yahya ibn Zakariyya ibn Abi Za'idah narrated to us, from Uthman ibn Hakim, from Muhammad ibn Aflah, from Usamah ibn Zayd, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Allah does not love the obscene, foul-mouthed one."
"405","405 - Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, Ali ibn al-Madini narrated to us, Wahb ibn Jarir ibn Hazim narrated to us, my father narrated to me, he said: I heard Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrating, from Salih ibn Kaysan, from Ubayd Allah ibn Abd Allah ibn Utbah, he said: I saw Usamah ibn Zayd at the chamber of A'ishah supplicating; Marwan came and let him hear harsh words. Usamah said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Allah Almighty hates the obscene, foul-tongued one."
"406","406 - Muhammad ibn Abdus ibn Kamil narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Umar ibn Aban narrated to us, Muhadir narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Umarah ibn Umayr, from Abu al-Sha'tha, from Ibn Umar, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): entered the House and walked until he stood in the middle, drew near the wall, and entered between Usamah and Bilal; the hadith."
"407","407 - al-Asfati narrated to us, Khalid ibn Yazid al-Umari narrated to us, Ibn Abi Dhi'b narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Mihran, from Umayr, freedman of Ibn Abbas, from Usamah ibn Zayd, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): entered the House, saw images, called for water, began wiping them and saying: May Allah destroy people who depict what they do not create."
"408","408 - And from Ibn Abi Dhi'b, from Zibraqan, from Zuhrah, from Usamah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed Zuhr in the midday heat."
"409","409 - Ibrahim ibn Duhaym narrated to us, my father narrated to us, Marwan ibn Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from Musa ibn Ubaydah, from Umar ibn al-Hakam, from Usamah ibn Zayd, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Deeds are presented to Allah Almighty on Monday and Thursday; Allah forgives except for two estranged ones, or one who severs kinship."
"410","410 - Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn al-Abbas narrated to us, Abu Mas'ud narrated to us, Sahl ibn Abd Rabbihi al-Razi narrated to us, from Amr ibn Abi Qays, from Ibn Abi Layla, from his brother Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Usamah ibn Zayd: Among them is one who wrongs himself, and among them is moderate, the verse. He said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: All of them are of this nation."
"411","411 - Yahya ibn Uthman ibn Salih narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Ghurab informed us, Qays ibn al-Rabi' narrated to us, from Jami' ibn Shaddad, from Kulthum al-Khuza'i, from Usamah ibn Zayd, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said in his illness in which he died: May Allah curse the Jews, they took the graves of their prophets as mosques."
"412","412 - Bishr ibn Musa narrated to us, al-Humaydi narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, (ha) and Ishaq ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Ma'mar, (ha) and Idris ibn Ja'far al-Attar narrated to us, Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abi Hafsah narrated to us, (ha) and Abd al-Rahman ibn Salm al-Razi narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Imran al-Asbahani narrated to us, Abu Dawud al-Tayalisi narrated to us, Zam'ah ibn Salih and Abd Allah ibn Budayl ibn Warqa al-Khuza'i narrated to us, (ha) and Muttalib ibn Shu'ayb al-Azdi narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated to us, al-Layth narrated to me, Uqayl ibn Khalid narrated to me, (ha) and Harun ibn Kamil narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated to us, al-Layth narrated to me, Yunus ibn Yazid narrated to me, (ha) and Muttalib ibn Shu'ayb al-Azdi narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated to us, al-Layth ibn Sa'd narrated to me, Yazid ibn Abd Allah ibn al-Had narrated to me, (ha) and Ibrahim ibn Hashim al-Baghawi narrated to us, Umar ibn Abd al-Wahhab al-Thaqafi narrated to us, my father narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Sa'id al-Ansari, (ha) and Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Hashim al-Ba'labakki narrated to us, my father narrated to us, Suwayd ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, from Sufyan ibn Husayn, (ha) and Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, Ali ibn al-Madini narrated to us, Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim ibn Sa'd narrated to us, my father narrated to me, from Salih ibn Kaysan, all of them from al-Zuhri, from Ali ibn Husayn, from Amr ibn Uthman, from Usamah ibn Zayd, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A Muslim does not inherit a disbeliever, nor a disbeliever a Muslim. Ma'mar, Sufyan ibn Husayn, and Salih ibn Kaysan added in their hadith: That the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: And has Aqil left us any house?"
"413","413 - Ibrahim ibn Suwayd al-Shibami narrated to us, Abd al-Razzaq informed us, Ma'mar informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Ali ibn Husayn, from Amr ibn Uthman, from Usamah ibn Zayd, he said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, where is our dwelling tomorrow? He said: Has Aqil left us any house or quarters? Our dwelling is at Khayf Bani Kinanah, where Quraysh pledged upon disbelief."
"414","414 - Idris ibn Ja'far al-Attar narrated to us, Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Sulayman informed us, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas, from Usamah ibn Zayd, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) kept moving in his manner when he poured forth from Jam'."
"415","Chapter on the bad woman, that she is a trial and harm upon her husband. 415 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Hawdhah ibn Khalifah narrated to us, Sulayman al-Taymi narrated to us, from Abu Uthman al-Nahdi, from Usamah ibn Zayd, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I have left after me no trial among people more harmful to men than women."
"416","416 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Sulayman al-Taymi, from Abu Uthman, from Usamah ibn Zayd, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I have left after me no trial more harmful to men than women."
"417","417 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, Abd al-Razzaq informed us, Ma'mar narrated to us, from Sulayman al-Taymi, from Abu Uthman al-Nahdi, from Usamah ibn Zayd, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: I have left after me no trial more harmful to men than women."
"418","418 - Yusuf al-Qadi narrated to us, Amr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Shu'bah informed us, from Sulayman, from Abu Uthman, from Usamah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I have left after me no trial more harmful to men than women."
"419","419 - Sulayman ibn al-Mu'afa ibn Sulayman narrated to us, my father narrated to us, al-Qasim ibn Ma'n narrated to us, from Sulayman al-Taymi, from Abu Uthman, from Usamah ibn Zayd, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I have left among people after me no trial more harmful to men than women."
"420","420 - Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Khalid al-Harrani narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Zuhayr narrated to me, Sulayman narrated to us, from Abu Uthman al-Nahdi, from Usamah ibn Zayd, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I have left after me no trial more harmful than women upon men."
"421","421 - Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn al-Abbas al-Asbahani narrated to us, Abu Mas'ud Ahmad ibn al-Furat narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Sa'id ibn Sabiq narrated to us, Abu Ja'far al-Razi narrated to us, from Sulayman, from Abu Uthman, from Usamah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I stood at the gate of Paradise and saw most of its people were the poor; I saw the people of wealth detained, except those of the people of the Fire, for they were ordered to the Fire."
"422","422 - Ahmad ibn Muhammad al-Jammal narrated to us, Abu Mas'ud narrated to us, Hisham ibn Hilal narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Muslim al-Ta'ifi, from Ma'mar, from Jabir, from Abu Uthman, from Usamah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever leaves three Fridays without excuse is written among the hypocrites."
"423","Chapter. 423 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Raqashi narrated to us, Mu'tamir ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: I heard my father, from Abu Uthman, saying: I was informed that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had Umm Salamah with him, when Jibril, peace be upon him, came and conversed as decreed for him; when he stood, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Umm Salamah, who is this? She said: Dihyah. She said: By Allah, I thought him none but Dihyah, until I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) preaching with news of Jibril, peace be upon him. Mu'tamir said: My father said to Abu Uthman: From whom did you hear it? He said: From Usamah."
"424","424 - Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Khalid al-Harrani narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us, from Salih ibn Abi Arib, from Khallad ibn al-Sa'ib, he said: I entered upon Usamah ibn Zayd and he praised me to my face. He said: What carried me to praise you to your face is that I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: When a believer is praised to his face, faith grows in his heart."
"425","425 - al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari narrated to us, al-Azraq ibn Ali narrated to us, Hassan ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, Abd al-Mun'im ibn Nu'aym narrated to us, al-Jurayri narrated to us, from Abu Uthman al-Nahdi, from Usamah ibn Zayd, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The most grateful of people to Allah are the most grateful of them to people."
"426","426 - Muhammad ibn al-Husayn al-Anmati narrated to us, Abd al-Rahman ibn Nafi' Darakht narrated to us, Ali ibn Thabit narrated to us, al-Wazi' ibn Nafi' narrated to us, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Usamah ibn Zayd, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever says upon me what I did not say, let him take his seat in the Fire."
"427","427 - Musa ibn Harun narrated to us, Abu Mus'ab narrated to us, Abd al-Rahman ibn Zayd ibn Aslam narrated to us, from his father, from Ata ibn Yasar, from Abd Allah ibn Rawahah and Usamah ibn Zayd, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered the house of Hamal, he and Bilal; Bilal came out to them and informed them that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): performed ablution and wiped over the leather socks."
"428","Chapter on exchange. 428 - Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, Musaddad narrated to us, Abd al-Warith narrated to us, from Amir al-Ahwal, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas, from Usamah ibn Zayd, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Usury is only in deferment."
"429","429 - Abd Allah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, Zakariyya ibn Yahya Zahmawayh narrated to us, al-Fadl ibn Musa narrated to us, from Mughirah ibn Ziyad, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas, from Usamah ibn Zayd, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: There is no usury except in deferment."
"430","430 - Abd Allah ibn al-Hasan ibn Ahmad ibn Abi Shu'ayb al-Harrani narrated to us, Yahya ibn Abd Allah al-Babalutti narrated to us, al-Awza'i narrated to us, Ata ibn Abi Rabah narrated to me, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him, Usamah ibn Zayd narrated to me, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Usury is only in deferment."
"431","431 - Muhammad ibn Mu'adh al-Sha'iri al-Baghdadi narrated to us, Ubayd Allah ibn Umar al-Qawariri narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Thabit al-Abdi narrated to us, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Qurayr, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas, from Usamah ibn Zayd, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no usury except in deferment."
"432","432 - Ahmad ibn Zuhayr al-Tustari narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ma'mar al-Bahrani narrated to us, Harun ibn Isma'il al-Khazzaz narrated to us, Ali ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas, from Usamah ibn Zayd, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: There is no usury except in deferment."
"433","433 - Ahmad ibn Zuhayr narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Mansur al-Tusi narrated to us, Abu al-Mundhir Isma'il ibn Umar narrated to us, Sulayman al-Qaflani narrated to us, from Mansur ibn Zadham, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas, from Usamah ibn Zayd, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: There is no usury except in deferment."
"434","434 - Ahmad ibn Zuhayr narrated to us, Abu al-Khattab Ziyad ibn Yahya narrated to us, Sahl ibn Hammad Abu Attab al-Dallal narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Abi Arubah narrated to us, Kathir ibn Shinzhir narrated to us, from Ata ibn Abi Rabah, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, from Usamah ibn Zayd, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Usury is only in deferment."
"435","435 - Abd Allah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, my father narrated to me, (ha) and Muhammad ibn Ishaq ibn Rahawayh narrated to us, my father narrated to us, both said: Muhammad ibn Bakr al-Bursani narrated to us, Yahya ibn Qays al-Mazini narrated to us, from Ata ibn Abi Rabah, from Ibn Abbas, from Usamah ibn Zayd, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Usury is only in deferment or delay."
"436","436 - Abu Muslim al-Kashshi narrated to us, Abu 'Asim narrated to us, from Isma'il ibn 'Abd al-Malik, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn 'Abbas, from Usamah ibn Zayd, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""There is no riba except in debt."""
"437","437 - Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi narrated to us, Musa ibn Dawud al-Dabbi narrated to us, (ha) and Muhammad ibn 'Uthman ibn Sa'id Abu 'Amr al-Darir narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Yunus narrated to us, both saying: Abu Isra'il narrated to us, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, who said: I heard Ibn 'Abbas saying: Usamah ibn Zayd narrated to me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""There is no riba except in debt."""
"438","438 - Al-Hasan ibn 'Ali al-Ma'mari narrated to us, al-Qasim ibn 'Isa al-Ta'i narrated to us, Hushaym narrated to us, from Isma'il ibn Salim, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from Abu Salih Dhakwan, that he asked Ibn 'Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) about selling gold for silver, and he said: ""It is lawful with an increase or decrease when it is hand to hand."" Abu Salih said: So I asked Abu Sa'id al-Khudri about that, and he said: ""It is unlawful except like for like."" So I informed Abu Sa'id of what Ibn 'Abbas had said, and I informed Ibn 'Abbas of what Abu Sa'id al-Khudri had said, then they met while I was with them, and Abu Sa'id al-Khudri began and said: O Ibn 'Abbas, what is this fatwa with which you give fatwa to the people regarding selling gold for silver, ordering them to buy it with increase, with decrease or increase, hand to hand? Ibn 'Abbas said: I am not the earliest of you in companionship of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and this is Zayd ibn Arqam and al-Bara' ibn 'Azib both saying: We heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"439","439 - 'Ali ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz narrated to us, Dawud ibn 'Amr al-Dabbi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Muslim narrated to us, from 'Amr ibn Dinar, from Dhakwan Abu Salih, from Ibn 'Abbas, who said: Usamah ibn Zayd narrated to me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Riba is in delay."""
"440","440 - Yusuf ibn Ya'qub al-Qadi narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Bashshar al-Ramadi narrated to us, (ha) and Bishr ibn Musa narrated to us, al-Humaydi narrated to us, both saying: Sufyan narrated to us, 'Amr ibn Dinar narrated to us, from Abu Salih, from Ibn 'Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him): Usamah informed me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Riba is only in delay."""
"441","441 - 'Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Muhammad ibn Ja'far narrated to us, Shu'bah narrated to us, from 'Amr ibn Dinar, from Abu Salih, from Ibn 'Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both), who said: I heard Usamah ibn Zayd saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""There is no riba except in debt"" - or he said: ""in delay."""
"442","442 - Musa ibn Harun narrated to us, Ishaq ibn Rahawayh narrated to us, Yahya ibn Adam narrated to us, Isra'il narrated to us, from 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Rufay', from Abu Salih, from Abu Sa'id, who said: I came to Ibn 'Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him) and said: What is it that has been conveyed from you? Have you attained what we did not attain, or heard what we did not hear? He said: I said: What do you say about dirhams? He said: Usamah ibn Zayd narrated to us, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: ""There is no riba in hand to hand; riba is only in debt."""
"443","443 - Al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari narrated to us, Yahya al-Himmani narrated to us, Sharik narrated to us, from 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Rufay', from Abu Salih, from Abu Sa'id, that he said to Ibn 'Abbas (may Allah Almighty be pleased with them both): Did you accompany the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in a way I did not accompany him? I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: ""Gold for gold, like for like, and silver for silver, like for like."" Ibn 'Abbas said: Usamah narrated to me: ""That was deferred."""
"444","444 - Ja'far ibn Muhammad ibn Harb al-'Abbadani narrated to us, Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us, Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from 'Ubaydullah ibn Abi Yazid, from Ibn 'Abbas, from Usamah ibn Zayd, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""There is no riba except in delay."""
"445","445 - Bishr ibn Musa narrated to us, al-Humaydi narrated to us, (ha) and Yusuf al-Qadi narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Bashshar, Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr al-Muqaddami and Nasr ibn 'Ali narrated to us, they said: Sufyan narrated to us, 'Ubaydullah ibn Yazid narrated to us, from Ibn 'Abbas, who said: Usamah ibn Zayd informed me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Riba is only in delay."""
"446","446 - Ahmad ibn Yahya ibn Khalid ibn Hayyan narrated to us, Abu 'Ubaydah ibn Fudayl ibn 'Iyad narrated to us, Malik ibn Su'ayr narrated to me, (ha) and Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Mandah al-Asbahani narrated to us, Abu Hafs 'Amr ibn 'Ali narrated to us, Abu 'Asim narrated to us, all from 'Uthman ibn al-Aswad, from Ibn Abi Mulaykah, from Ibn 'Abbas, from Usamah ibn Zayd, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: ""There is no riba except in delay."""
"447","447 - Al-Qasim ibn 'Abd al-Warith al-Baghdadi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Bakkar narrated to us, Qays ibn al-Rabi' narrated to us, from 'Asim ibn Kulayb, from his father, from Ibn 'Abbas, from Usamah ibn Zayd (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: ""There is no riba except in delay."""
"448","448 - 'Abdullah ibn al-Husayn al-Missisi narrated to us, Yahya ibn Ishaq al-Saylahini narrated to us, (ha) and al-'Abbas ibn al-Fadl al-Asfati narrated to us, Sahl ibn Bakkar narrated to us, both saying: Wuhayb narrated to us, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, from Ibn 'Abbas, from Usamah ibn Zayd, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""There is no riba in what is hand to hand."""
"449","449 - Al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari narrated to us, Wahb ibn Baqiyyah narrated to us, Khalid informed us, from Khalid al-Hadhdha', from 'Ikrimah, from Ibn 'Abbas, from Usamah ibn Zayd, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Riba is only in delay."""
"450","450 - Musa ibn Harun narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim ibn Sa'd narrated to us, my father narrated to me, from Ibn Ishaq, 'Ubaydullah ibn 'Ali ibn Abi Rafi' narrated to me, from Sa'id al-Musayyib: Usamah ibn Zayd narrated to me that he heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: ""There is no riba except in delay."""
"451","451 - Ahmad ibn Muhammad al-Shafi'i narrated to us, my uncle Ibrahim ibn Muhammad narrated to us, al-Harith ibn 'Umayr narrated to us, from 'Uqbah, from Kurayb, from Usamah ibn Zayd, who said: I was riding behind the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at 'Arafah."
"452","452 - And Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah narrated to us, al-Husayn ibn Yazid al-Tahhan narrated to us, 'A'idh ibn Habib narrated to us, from Hajjaj ibn Artah, from al-Hakam, from Miqsam, from Ibn 'Abbas, from Usamah ibn Zayd, who said: I was riding behind the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at 'Arafah."
"453","Chapter: The clarification of the abrogation of that, and the retraction of Ibn 'Abbas from currency exchange and his forbidding it (may Allah be pleased with him). 453 - Bishr ibn Musa narrated to us, al-Humaydi narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, 'Amr ibn Dinar narrated to us, that he heard Abu al-Minhal saying: A partner of mine in Kufa sold dirhams for dirhams with an excess between them, so I said: I do not think this is sound. He said: I sold it in the market and no one objected to me. So I came to al-Bara' ibn 'Azib and asked him, and he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to Madinah while our trade was like this, and he said: ""Whatever is hand to hand there is no harm in it, and whatever is deferred there is no good in it."" And I came to Zayd ibn Arqam, for he was greater in trade than me, so I came to him and mentioned that to him, and he said: Al-Bara' spoke the truth. Al-Humaydi said: ""This is abrogated; this is not to be acted upon."""
"454","454 - 'Ali ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, from 'Abd al-Salam ibn Harb, from Mughirah meaning Ibn Miqsam, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Nu'm, that Abu Sa'id al-Khudri met Ibn 'Abbas and testified regarding the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: ""Gold for gold and silver for silver, like for like; whoever increases has committed riba."" Ibn 'Abbas said: ""I repent to Allah (Mighty and Majestic) from what I used to give fatwa."" Then he retracted."
"455","455 - 'Ali ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz narrated to us, al-Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, al-Rabi' ibn Subayh narrated to us, from 'Uqbah ibn Abi Thubayt al-Rasibi and Ghalib al-Qattan, from Abu al-Jawza', who said: I asked Ibn 'Abbas about currency exchange, about one dirham for two dirhams hand to hand. He said: ""I see no harm in what is hand to hand."" Then I came to Makkah the following year, and he had ""forbidden it."""
"456","456 - Musa ibn Harun narrated to us, Abu al-Rabi' al-Zahrani narrated to us, Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, al-Muthanna ibn Sa'id narrated to us, Abu al-Sha'tha' narrated to us, who said: I heard Ibn 'Abbas saying: O Allah, I repent to You from currency exchange; this was only from my opinion, and this is Abu Sa'id al-Khudri narrating it from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"457","457 - Muhammad ibn 'Amr ibn Khalid al-Harrani narrated to us, my father narrated to me, 'Isa ibn Yunus narrated to us, Abu Ghifar al-Muthanna ibn Sa'id narrated to us, who said: I heard Abu al-Sha'tha' saying: I heard Ibn 'Abbas saying: I seek Allah's forgiveness and repent to Him from currency exchange."
"458","458 - 'Ali ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, Fudayl ibn Marzuq narrated to us, from 'Atiyyah, who said: Abu Sa'id said to Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah have mercy on him: Repent to Allah (Mighty and Majestic). He said: I seek Allah's forgiveness and repent to Him. He said: Did you not know that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade gold for gold and silver for silver, and said: ""I fear riba for you""? I said to 'Atiyyah: What is riba? He said: ""The increase and excess between them."""
"459","459 - And 'Abdan ibn Ahmad narrated to us, Talut ibn 'Abbad al-Sayrafi narrated to us, both saying: Salim ibn 'Abdullah Abu Ghiyath al-'Ataki narrated to us, who said: I heard Bakr ibn 'Abdullah al-Muzani narrating that Ibn 'Abbas came from Madinah to Makkah, and I came with him. He praised Allah (Mighty and Majestic) and extolled Him, then said: O people, there is no harm in currency exchange as long as it is hand to hand; riba is only in delay. So the word flew among the people of the east and the west, until when the season ended Abu Sa'id al-Khudri came to him and said: O Ibn 'Abbas, have you consumed riba and fed it? He said: Have I done so? He said: Yes. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Gold for gold, weight for weight, like for like, its ingots and its coins; whoever increases or seeks increase has committed riba. Silver for silver, weight for weight, like for like, its ingots and its coins; whoever increases or seeks increase has committed riba. Barley for barley, dates for dates, and salt for salt, like for like; whoever increases or seeks increase has committed riba."" Then when the following year came, Ibn 'Abbas came, and I came with him. He praised Allah and extolled Him, then said: O people, I spoke the first year a word from my opinion, and I seek Allah's forgiveness for it and repent to Him. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Gold for gold, weight for weight, like for like, its ingots and its coins; whoever increases and seeks increase has committed riba."" And he repeated to them these six kinds."
"460","460 - Al-Husayn ibn al-'Abbas al-Razi narrated to us, 'Ali ibn Hashim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, my father narrated to us, 'Amr ibn Abi Qays narrated to us, from Simak ibn Harb, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn 'Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""When zina and riba appear in a town, they have made the Book of Allah (Mighty and Majestic) binding upon themselves."""
"461","Completion of the hadith of Usamah ibn Zayd. 461 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, 'Abd al-Razzaq informed us, Ma'mar informed us, from Ayyub, from Abu al-Malih, from Usamah ibn Zayd, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Allah does not appoint a servant's death in a land except that He gives him some need in it."""
"462","462 - Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Hadrami narrated to us, Hudbah ibn Khalid narrated to us, Hammam narrated to us, from Qatadah, from 'Azrah, from al-Sha'bi, from Usamah ibn Zayd, who narrated to him that he was riding behind the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and his mount did not lift its running leg until he reached Jam' (Muzdalifah)."
"463","12 - And Usamah ibn Sharik al-Tha'labi, from Banu Tha'labah ibn Yarbu', may Allah have mercy on him. Chapter: What has come regarding medicine, abandoning backbiting, and good character. 463 - 'Ali ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz narrated to us, Muslim ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, (ha) and Abu Muslim al-Kashshi narrated to us, Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us, both saying: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Ziyad ibn 'Ilaqah, from Usamah ibn Sharik, who said: I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at 'Arafat and greeted him, and it was as if birds were upon the heads of his Companions. Bedouins came to him from here and there: O Messenger of Allah, is there hardship upon us in such-and-such? Is there hardship upon us in such-and-such? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Servants of Allah, Allah has lifted hardship, except one who wrongfully takes from a Muslim's honor - that is the one who is in hardship and ruined."" They said: O Messenger of Allah, should we take medicine? He said: ""Take medicine, for Allah (Mighty and Majestic) did not send down a disease except that He made for it a cure, except old age."" They said: O Messenger of Allah, what is the best thing people have been given? He said: ""People have not been given anything better than good character."""
"464","464 - Abu Yazid al-Qaratisî narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, (ha) and Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, Musaddad narrated to us, (ha) and Muhammad ibn Muhammad al-Tammar narrated to us, Sahl ibn Bakkar narrated to us, they said: Abu 'Awanah narrated to us, from Ziyad ibn 'Ilaqah, from Usamah ibn Sharik, who said: We were with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when the Bedouins came to him, many people from here and there, and the people were silenced so none spoke but them. They said: O Messenger of Allah, is there hardship upon us in such-and-such? Regarding things of people's affairs in which there is no harm. He said: ""Servants of Allah, Allah has removed hardship, except one who wrongfully takes from a person - that is the one who is in hardship and ruined."" They said: O Messenger of Allah, is there hardship upon us in taking medicine? He said: ""Take medicine, servants of Allah, for Allah (Mighty and Majestic) did not place a disease except that He placed for it a cure, except old age."" They said: O Messenger of Allah, what is the best thing a man is given? He said: ""Good character."""
"465","465 - 'Uthman ibn 'Umar narrated to us, 'Abdullah ibn Raja' narrated to us, (ha) and Ahmad ibn 'Amr al-Qatarani narrated to us, 'Amr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, both saying: Za'idah informed us, from Ziyad ibn 'Ilaqah, from Usamah ibn Sharik, who said: I was with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when the Bedouins came and asked him: What is the best thing people are given? He said: ""Good character."" They said: O Messenger of Allah, should we take medicine? He said: ""Yes, servants of Allah, take medicine, for Allah (Mighty and Majestic) did not place upon the earth a disease except that He placed for it a cure, except one disease."" They said: What is it? He said: ""Old age."""
"466","466 - 'Uthman ibn 'Umar al-Dabbi narrated to us, 'Abdullah ibn Raja' narrated to us, Isra'il informed us, from Ziyad ibn 'Ilaqah, from Usamah ibn Sharik, who said: I witnessed the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when the Bedouins came to him and said: Is there hardship upon us in such-and-such? Is there hardship upon us in such-and-such? Regarding things of their affairs in which there is no harm. He said: ""Servants of Allah, Allah has removed hardship, except from a man who takes from the honor of a Muslim man - that is the one who is in hardship and ruined."" They said: O Messenger of Allah, should we take medicine? He said: ""Yes, take medicine, for Allah (Mighty and Majestic) did not send down a disease except that He made for it a cure, except one disease."" They said: O Messenger of Allah, what is the best thing man is given? He said: ""Good character."""
"467","467 - Muhammad ibn 'Amr ibn Khalid al-Harrani narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Zuhayr narrated to us, Ziyad ibn 'Ilaqah narrated to us, from Usamah ibn Sharik, who said: I was sitting with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when the Bedouins came from every place and said: O Messenger of Allah, is there hardship upon us in such-and-such? - the hadith."
"468","468 - Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, Musaddad narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Ziyad ibn 'Ilaqah, from Usamah ibn Sharik, who said: I attended the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when he was asked: Which deeds are best? He said: ""Good character."""
"469","469 - 'Ubayd ibn Ghannam narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Sufyan ibn 'Uyaynah narrated to us, from Ziyad ibn 'Ilaqah, from Usamah ibn Sharik, who said: I witnessed the Bedouins asking the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Is there hardship in such-and-such? He said to them: ""Servants of Allah, Allah has removed hardship, except one who takes something from his brother's honor - that is the one who is in hardship."" And he said: ""Take medicine, servants of Allah, for Allah did not place a disease except that He placed with it a cure, except old age."" They said: O Messenger of Allah, what is the best thing the servant is given? He said: ""Good character."""
"470","470 - 'Ubayd ibn Ghannam narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Waki' narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Ziyad ibn 'Ilaqah, from Usamah ibn Sharik, who said: They said: O Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), what is the best thing the Muslim is given? He said: ""Good character."""
"471","471 - Muhammad ibn 'Amr ibn Khalid al-Harrani narrated to us, my father narrated to me, 'Isa ibn Yunus narrated to us, from 'Uthman ibn Hakim, from Ziyad ibn 'Ilaqah, from Usamah ibn Sharik, who said: We were sitting with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) as if birds were upon our heads, none of us speaking, when some people came to him and said: O Messenger of Allah, give us fatwa regarding such-and-such, give us fatwa regarding such-and-such. He said: ""O people, Allah has removed hardship, except one who takes a loan from his brother - that is the one who is in hardship and ruined."" They said: Should we take medicine, O Messenger of Allah? He said: ""Yes, Allah (Mighty and Majestic) did not send down a disease except that He sent down for it a cure, except one disease."" They said: O Messenger of Allah, what is it? He said: ""Old age."" They said: Who are the most beloved of Allah's servants to Allah? He said: ""The best of them in character."""
"472","472 - 'Ubayd ibn Ghannam narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Asbat ibn Muhammad narrated to us, (ha) and al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari narrated to us, 'Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Jarir narrated to us, both from al-Shaybani, from Ziyad ibn 'Ilaqah, from Usamah ibn Sharik, who said: I went out with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performing Hajj, and the people would come to him. Someone said: O Messenger of Allah, I performed sa'y before tawaf, or I delayed something. He would say to them: ""No hardship, except upon a man who takes from the honor of a Muslim man while being unjust - that is the one who is in hardship and ruined."""
"473","473 - 'Ubayd ibn Ghannam narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Asbat narrated to us, from al-Shaybani, from Ziyad ibn 'Ilaqah, from Usamah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about a man who shaved before slaughtering. He said: ""No hardship; let him shave."""
"474","474 - Ja'far ibn Muhammad al-Firyabi narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn al-Hajjaj al-Sami narrated to us, 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Muslim narrated to us, (ha) and al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari narrated to us, 'Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Jarir narrated to us, all from al-A'mash, from Ziyad ibn 'Ilaqah, from Usamah ibn Sharik, who said: We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when some Bedouins came to him and asked him: O Messenger of Allah, should we take medicine? He said: ""Yes, Allah (Mighty and Majestic) did not send down a disease except that He sent down for it a cure."""
"475","475 - Al-Husayn ibn Ishaq narrated to us, 'Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, 'Abdullah ibn Idris narrated to us, from Mis'ar and Layth, from Ziyad ibn 'Ilaqah, from a man of his people called Usamah ibn Sharik, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked during his Hajj: What is the best thing people are given? He said: ""Good character."""
"476","476 - Muhammad ibn Salih ibn al-Walid al-Narsi and al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari narrated to us, both saying: Abu Hafs 'Amr ibn 'Ali narrated to us, Abu 'Asim narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Bishr al-Aslami narrated to us, from Ziyad ibn 'Ilaqah, from Usamah ibn Sharik, who said: I witnessed the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while the people were asking him, one saying: I shaved before slaughtering, and another saying: I did such-and-such first. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) kept saying: ""No hardship, no hardship."""
"477","477 - Ahmad ibn 'Amr al-Bazzar narrated to us, 'Umar ibn Yahya al-Ubulli narrated to us, Hafs ibn Jumay' narrated to us, Simak ibn Harb narrated to us, from Ziyad ibn 'Ilaqah, from Usamah ibn Sharik, who said: The Bedouins came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) asking him: Is there hardship upon us in such-and-such? He said: ""Allah has removed hardship, except a man who takes from his brother's honor - that is the one who is in hardship and ruined."" And he said: ""Allah did not send down a disease except that He sent down for it a cure, except old age."""
"478","478 - 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Salm al-Razi narrated to us, Wasil ibn 'Abd al-A'la narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Fudayl narrated to us, from al-Ajlah, from Ziyad ibn 'Ilaqah, from Usamah ibn Sharik, who said: The Bedouins came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, which people are best? He said: ""The best of them in character."" They said: O Messenger of Allah, should we take medicine? He said: ""Yes, take medicine, for Allah (Mighty and Majestic) did not send down a disease except that He sent down for it a cure."""
"479","479 - Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Hadrami and Muhammad ibn al-'Abbas al-Asbahani al-Akhram narrated to us, both saying: Muhammad ibn Harb al-Nasa'i narrated to us, 'Umar ibn Shabib al-Musalli narrated to us, from 'Amr ibn Qays al-Mula'i, from 'Alqamah ibn Marthad, from Ziyad ibn 'Ilaqah, from Usamah ibn Sharik, who said: The Bedouins came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, is there hardship upon us in such-and-such? Is there hardship upon us in such-and-such? He said: ""No hardship; Allah has removed hardship, except a person who takes from his brother's honor - that is the hardship."" They said: O Messenger of Allah, should we take medicine? He said: ""Take medicine, for Allah (Mighty and Majestic) did not send down a disease except that He sent down for it a cure, except old age."" They said: O Messenger of Allah, what is the best thing man is given? He said: ""Good character."""
"480","480 - 'Abdan ibn Ahmad and Abu Hanifah Muhammad ibn Hanifah al-Wasiti narrated to us, both saying: Ma'mar ibn Sahl al-Ahwazi narrated to us, 'Amir ibn Mudrik narrated to us, Yahya ibn Ayyub narrated to us, from Ziyad ibn 'Ilaqah, from Usamah ibn Sharik, who said: Some Bedouins with swords slung over came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and crowded around him asking him, so we feared for the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and drew near them. He said: And I heard them saying: Is there hardship upon us in such-and-such? Is there hardship upon us in such-and-such? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""O people, Allah has removed hardship, except a man who takes from the honor of his Muslim brother - that is the one who is in hardship and ruined."" They said: O Messenger of Allah, should we take medicine for such-and-such? Should we take medicine for such-and-such? He said: ""Take medicine, for Allah (Mighty and Majestic) did not send down a disease except that He sent down for it a cure, except old age."" They said: O Messenger of Allah, what is the best thing the Muslim servant is given in this world? He said: ""Good character."""
"481","481 - 'Abdan ibn Ahmad narrated to us, Sahl ibn 'Uthman narrated to us, Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, from Ash'ath, from Ziyad ibn 'Ilaqah al-Tha'labi, from Usamah ibn Sharik, who said: A Bedouin said: O Messenger of Allah, tell me what belongs to the Muslim man? He said: ""Good character."""
"482","482 - Ahmad ibn Muhammad al-Jammal al-Asbahani narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn 'Amir ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, my father narrated to me, al-Nu'man ibn 'Abd al-Salam narrated to us, from Malik ibn Mighwal and others, Ziyad ibn 'Ilaqah narrated to us, from Usamah ibn Sharik, who said: I witnessed the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when some Bedouins came to him asking him on his right and on his left: O Messenger of Allah, is there hardship upon us in such-and-such? Twice. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Allah (Mighty and Majestic) has removed hardship, except one who takes from a Muslim person unjustly"" - or he said: ""with injustice - that is in hardship and ruined."" They said: O Messenger of Allah, should we take medicine for such-and-such? Twice. He said: ""Yes, take medicine, for Allah (Mighty and Majestic) did not send down a disease except that He sent down for it a cure, except one disease: old age."" They said: O Messenger of Allah, what is the best thing people are given? He said: ""Good character."""
"483","483 - Ahmad ibn Zuhayr al-Tustari narrated to us, Muhammad ibn 'Uthman ibn Karamah narrated to us, 'Ubaydullah ibn Musa narrated to us, Shayban narrated to us, from Ziyad ibn 'Ilaqah, from Usamah ibn Sharik, who said: I was sitting with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when the Bedouins came from every direction until they crowded upon him, and the people fell silent. They called out: O Messenger of Allah, should we take medicine? He said: ""Yes, for Allah (Mighty and Majestic) did not place a disease except that He placed for it a cure, except one."" They said: What is it, O Messenger of Allah? He said: ""Old age."" They said: O Messenger of Allah, what is the best thing given to man? He said: ""Good character."" Then they called out: O Messenger of Allah, is there hardship upon us in such-and-such? Of what occurs among people. He said: ""O servants of Allah, Allah has removed hardship, except one who takes from a person's honor - that is the one who is in hardship and ruined."""
"484","484 - 'Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, and Zakariyya ibn Yahya al-Saji narrated to us, both saying: Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, 'Amr ibn 'Asim narrated to us, Abu al-'Awwam 'Imran narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Juhadah narrated to us, from Ziyad ibn 'Ilaqah, from Usamah ibn Sharik, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) during the Farewell Hajj saying: ""Your mother and your father, your sister and your brother, then the nearest to you."" He said: Some people came and said: O Messenger of Allah, Banu Yarbu' killed us? He said: ""No soul wrongs another."" He said: Then a man asked him who had forgotten to stone the jamarat. He said: ""Stone, and no hardship."" Then another came to him and said: O Messenger of Allah, I forgot tawaf. He said: ""Perform tawaf, and no hardship."" Then another came to him who had shaved before slaughtering. He said: ""Slaughter, and no hardship."" He said: They did not ask him that day about anything except that he said: ""No hardship, no hardship."" Then he said: ""Allah (Mighty and Majestic) has removed hardship, except a man who takes from a Muslim - that is the one who is in hardship and ruined."" And he said: ""Allah (Mighty and Majestic) did not send down a disease except that He sent down for it a cure, except old age."""
"485","485 - Ibrahim ibn Muhammad al-Sulami narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Isma'il al-Ahmasi narrated to us, Wahb ibn Isma'il al-Asadi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Qays narrated to us, from Ziyad ibn 'Ilaqah, from Qutbah, who said: When the Bedouins came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) we would take advantage of them due to their roughness. They came and said: O Messenger of Allah, is there hardship upon us in such-and-such? He said: ""No hardship; hardship is removed, servants of Allah, except one who takes from his brother's honor unjustly - that is the one who is in hardship and ruined."" They said: O Messenger of Allah, what is the best thing man is given? He said: ""Good character."" Thus Wahb ibn Isma'il narrated it from Muhammad ibn Qays, erring in it; and the correct is from Usamah ibn Sharik."
"486","486 - 'Ubayd ibn Ghannam narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Waki' narrated to us, from al-Mas'udi, from Ziyad ibn 'Ilaqah, from Usamah ibn Sharik, who said: I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and his Companions were as if birds were upon their heads."
"487","Chapter: What has come regarding adhering to the congregation and the prohibition of separating from it, and other matters. 487 - Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Hadrami narrated to us, 'Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Jarir narrated to us, from Zayd ibn 'Ata' ibn al-Sa'ib, from Ziyad ibn 'Ilaqah, from Usamah ibn Sharik, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Whichever man goes out dividing my ummah, strike his neck."" Thus Zayd ibn 'Ata' ibn al-Sa'ib narrated it from Ziyad ibn 'Ilaqah from Usamah; and the correct is from 'Arfajah. Likewise Muhammad ibn Bishr narrated it from Mujalid."
"488","488 - 'Ubayd ibn Ghannam narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Bishr narrated to us, from Mujalid, from Ziyad ibn 'Ilaqah, from Usamah ibn Sharik, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Whoever divides my ummah while they are united, strike his head, whoever he may be."""
"489","489 - Muhammad ibn al-Fadl al-Saqati narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Sulayman narrated to us, 'Abd al-A'la ibn Abi al-Musawir narrated to us, from Ziyad ibn 'Ilaqah, from Usamah ibn Sharik, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""The Hand of Allah is upon the congregation; when one of them strays apart, Satan snatches him as the wolf snatches the sheep from the flock."""
"490","490 - Al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari narrated to us, 'Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, 'Abd al-A'la ibn Abi al-Musawir narrated to us, from Ziyad ibn 'Ilaqah, from Usamah ibn Sharik, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said one day: ""My Companions were weighed tonight: Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) was weighed, then 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) was weighed, then 'Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him) was weighed."" Thus Yazid ibn Harun narrated it; and Sa'dawayh narrated it from 'Abd al-A'la ibn Abi al-Musawir, from Ziyad ibn 'Ilaqah, from Qutbah ibn Malik, from 'Arfajah."
"491","491 - 'Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, Surayj ibn Yunus narrated to us, from al-Muttalib ibn Ziyad, from Ziyad ibn 'Ilaqah, from Usamah ibn Sharik, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""In the black seed there is cure for every disease except death."""
"492","492 - Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Hadrami narrated to us, Sahl ibn Zanjalah al-Razi narrated to us, al-Sabbah ibn Muharib narrated to us, from 'Umar ibn 'Abdullah ibn Ya'la ibn Murrah, from his father, from his grandfather, and from Ziyad ibn 'Ilaqah, from Usamah ibn Sharik, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said regarding wiping over the khuffs: ""For the traveler three days, and for the resident a day and a night."""
"493","493 - Al-Husayn ibn Ishaq narrated to us, 'Ali ibn 'Abdullah ibn Salih al-Dahhan al-Kufi narrated to us, Mufaddal ibn Salih narrated to us, from Ziyad ibn 'Ilaqah, from Usamah ibn Sharik, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: ""No one enters Paradise by his deed."" We said: Not even you, O Messenger of Allah? He said: ""No, not even I, unless Allah (Mighty and Majestic) envelops me with mercy from Him."" And he placed his hand upon his head."
"494","494 - And with its chain he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: ""There is none of you but that a devil is with him."" We said: And you, O Messenger of Allah? He said: ""And I, except that Allah (Mighty and Majestic) helped me against him, so he submitted."""
"495","495 - Ahmad ibn Zuhayr al-Tustari narrated to us, Ma'mar ibn Sahl narrated to us, 'Amir ibn Mudrik narrated to us, Muhammad ibn 'Ubaydullah narrated to us, 'Ali ibn al-Aqmar narrated to us, Usamah ibn Sharik al-Tha'labi narrated to me, who said: I was with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when a funeral was brought near for him to pray over it. He turned and saw a woman approaching, and said: ""Send her back."" So they sent her back repeatedly until she disappeared. When he saw that she had disappeared, he said takbir over it."
"496","13 - Usamah ibn 'Umayr al-Hudhali, son of 'Amir ibn al-Ashtar, from Hudhayl ibn Mudrikah ibn Ilyas ibn Mudar, then from Banu Lihyan. Chapter: The concession to perform prayer in the mounts while traveling on a rainy day. 496 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, from 'Abd al-Razzaq, from al-Thawri, from Khalid al-Hadhdha', from Abu Qilabah, from Abu al-Malih ibn Usamah, who said: We prayed 'Isha' in Basra and it rained upon us, then I came seeking entry, and my father Usamah said to me: You saw us with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when it rained upon us, and the sky did not even wet the soles of our sandals, yet the caller of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called out: ""Pray in your dwellings."""
"497","497 - Al-Miqdam ibn Dawud narrated to us, Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, (ha) and 'Uthman ibn 'Umar al-Dabbi narrated to us, 'Amr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, (ha) and Muhammad ibn 'Abdus ibn Kamil narrated to us, 'Ali ibn al-Ja'd narrated to us, they said: Shu'bah narrated to us, and Muhammad ibn Yahya al-Qazzaz narrated to us, Abu 'Umar al-Hawdi narrated to us, Hammam narrated to us, (ha) and 'Ali ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz and Abu Muslim al-Kashshi narrated to us, both saying: Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, (ha) and Aslam ibn Sahl al-Wasiti narrated to us, al-Qasim ibn 'Isa al-Ta'i narrated to us, Talhah ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, (ha) and al-Miqdam ibn Dawud narrated to us, Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, both saying: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, (ha) and Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, Musaddad narrated to us, Yazid ibn Zuray' narrated to us, from Sa'id ibn Abi 'Arubah, all of them from Qatadah, from Abu al-Malih, from his father, who said: We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on a rainy day, and he ordered a caller who called out: ""Prayer is in the dwellings."""
"498","498 - 'Ali ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz narrated to us, Muslim ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Zarbi narrated to us, from Abu al-Malih al-Hudhali, from his father, who said: I went out in battle with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and we went out to Hunayn with seventeen nights passed of Ramadan. A Friday came upon us, a rainy day, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered Bilal, who called out among the people: ""Prayer is in the dwellings."""
"499","499 - Muhammad ibn 'Abdus ibn Kamil al-Sarraj narrated to us, 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar ibn Aban narrated to us, Abu Usamah narrated to us, from 'Amir ibn 'Ubaydah al-Bahili, from Abu al-Malih, from his father, who said: I witnessed Hunayn with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and a drizzle - meaning rain - befell us. The caller of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called out: ""Whoever wishes to pray in his dwelling, let him pray."""
"500","500 - 'Ubayd ibn Ghannam narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Isma'il ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, from Khalid al-Hadhdha', from Abu Qilabah, from Abu al-Malih, who said: I went out on a rainy night to the mosque, and when I returned I sought entry. My father said to me: You saw us with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day of al-Hudaybiyah when rain befell us that did not wet the soles of our sandals, yet the caller of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called: ""Pray in your dwellings."""
"501","501 - 'Abdan ibn Muhammad al-Marwazi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ghalib al-Antaki narrated to us, al-Husayn ibn al-Sakan narrated to us, 'Imran al-Qattan narrated to us, from Qatadah and Ziyad ibn Abi al-Malih, from Abu al-Malih, from Usamah ibn 'Umayr, who said: I witnessed the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on a rainy day, a Friday, ordering a caller who called out: ""Pray in your dwellings."""
"502","Chapter: Regarding Allah's overlooking His believing servant through the prayer of the Muslims over him, and His pardoning him in what is between Him and him. 502 - Ibrahim ibn 'Umar al-Waki'i narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn al-Hajjaj al-Sami narrated to us, Sawwad ibn Abi al-Aswad narrated to us, Salih ibn Hilal narrated to us, from Abu al-Malih ibn Usamah al-Hudhali: My father (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated to me, from the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: ""When a community of communities, forty or more, testifies, Allah validates their testimony"" - or he said: ""confirms their testimony."""
"503","503 - Al-Hasan ibn 'Ali al-Ma'mari narrated to us, Khalaf ibn Salim narrated to us, Yahya ibn Sa'id narrated to us, Shu'bah narrated to us, Mubashshir ibn Abi al-Malih narrated to us, from his father, from Ibn 'Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: ""No man over whom one hundred pray except that he is forgiven."""
"504","Chapter. 504 - Al-'Abbas ibn al-Fadl al-Asfati narrated to us, 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Mubarak al-'Ayshi narrated to us, (ha) and Ibrahim ibn Hashim al-Baghawi narrated to us, Musa ibn Hayyan al-Basri narrated to us, Abu Qutaybah narrated to us, al-Mufaddal ibn Fudalah narrated to us, from Salim ibn 'Ubaydullah ibn Salim, from Abu al-Malih, from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Fast from crescent to crescent."""
"505","505 - Muhammad ibn 'Abdus ibn Kamil narrated to us, 'Ali ibn al-Ja'd narrated to us, (ha) and Abu Zayd al-Qaratisî narrated to us, Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, (ha) and 'Uthman ibn 'Umar al-Dabbi narrated to us, 'Amr ibn Marzuq narrated to us: Shu'bah informed us, from Qatadah, from Abu al-Malih, from his father, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: ""Allah does not accept prayer without purification, nor charity from misappropriation."""
"506","506 - Abu Muslim al-Kashshi narrated to us, Abu 'Umar al-Darir narrated to us, (ha) and Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi narrated to us, Khalid ibn Khidash narrated to us, (ha) and Ja'far ibn Muhammad al-Firyabi narrated to us, Qutaybah ibn Sa'id narrated to us, they said: Abu 'Awanah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Abu al-Malih, from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Allah does not accept prayer without purification, nor charity from misappropriation."""
"507","507 - Muhammad ibn Yahya al-Qazzaz narrated to us, Abu 'Umar al-Hawdi and Hani ibn Yahya narrated to us, both saying: Hammam narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Abu al-Malih, from his father, that a man freed a share of a slave, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) validated his emancipation and said: ""Allah has no partner."""
"508","Chapter: What has come regarding the prohibition of spreading the skins of beasts of prey. 508 - Yahya ibn 'Uthman ibn Salih narrated to us, Nu'aym ibn Hammad narrated to us, Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Sa'id, (ha) and 'Ubayd ibn Ghannam narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Yazid ibn Harun and 'Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Sa'id, (ha) and Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, Musaddad narrated to us, Yahya ibn Sa'id narrated to us, from Sa'id ibn Abi 'Arubah, from Qatadah, from Abu al-Malih, from his father, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade the skins of beasts of prey. Yazid ibn Harun added: ""to be spread."""
"509","509 - 'Abdan ibn Ahmad narrated to us, Abu Kurayb narrated to us, Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Shu'bah, from Qatadah, from Abu al-Malih, from his father, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade that the skins of beasts of prey be spread."
"510","510 - Ahmad ibn 'Amr al-Khallal al-Makki narrated to us, Ya'qub ibn Humayd narrated to us, 'Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, Ma'mar informed us, from Yazid al-Rishk, from Abu al-Malih, I think from his father, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade that the skins of beasts of prey be spread."
"511","511 - Muhammad ibn Salih ibn al-Walid al-Narsi narrated to us, 'Abdah ibn 'Abdullah al-Saffar narrated to us, Ishaq ibn Idris narrated to us, Aban ibn Yazid narrated to us, from Matar al-Warraq, from Abu al-Malih, from his father, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade that the skins of beasts of prey be spread."
"512","Chapter: Regarding what is said at the time of whispering. 512 - 'Abdan ibn Ahmad narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Abi al-Rabi' al-Samman narrated to us, Sa'id ibn 'Anbasah al-Qattan narrated to us, al-Muhajir ibn al-Munib narrated to us, from Abu al-Malih ibn Usamah, from his father, that a man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, I complain to you of whispering I find in my chest; I enter my prayer and do not know whether I finish on an even number or an odd number. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""When you find that, raise your right forefinger and thrust it into your left thigh, and say: In the Name of Allah, for it is the knife of Satan."""
"513","513 - Al-Miqdam ibn Dawud al-Misri narrated to us, Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, Sufyan ibn 'Uyaynah narrated to us, from Ayyub al-Sakhtiyani, who said: I heard Abu al-Malih, from his father, who had accompanied the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: There were two women among them; one of them struck the other with a tent-pole, killing her and killing what was in her womb. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) judged for the woman with blood-money, and for the fetus with a ghurrah: a male or female slave, or a horse, or two camels of camels, or such-and-such of sheep. A man of the killer's clan said: How do we pay blood-money, O Messenger of Allah, for one who neither ate nor drank nor cried out? Such is wasted. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Are you a rhymester?"" And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) judged that the inheritance of the woman belongs to her husband and her child, and that the blood-money is upon the killer's clan."
"514","514 - 'Ali ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz narrated to us, 'Uthman ibn Sa'id al-Murri narrated to us, al-Minhal ibn Khalifah narrated to us, from Salamah ibn Tammam, from Abu al-Malih al-Hudhali, from his father, who said: Among us was a man called Hamal ibn Malik, who had two wives, one Hudhali and the other 'Amiri. The Hudhali struck the belly of the 'Amiri with a tent-pole - or a pavilion-pole - and she cast a dead fetus. The striker was taken to the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), with a brother of hers called 'Imran ibn 'Uwaymir. When they related the story to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: ""Pay blood-money."" 'Imran said: O Prophet of Allah, do we pay blood-money for one who neither ate nor drank nor cried out? Such is wasted. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Leave aside the rhymes of the Bedouins from me. In it is a ghurrah: a male or female slave, or five hundred, or a horse, or one hundred and twenty sheep."" He said: O Prophet of Allah, she has two sons who are the chiefs of the clan, and they are more entitled to pay on behalf of their mother. He said: ""You are more entitled to pay on behalf of your sister than her children."" He said: I have nothing with which to pay. He said: ""O Hamal ibn Malik - who was then in charge of the charities of Hudhayl, and was the husband of the two women and the father of the slain fetus - take from under your hand, from the charities of Hudhayl, one hundred and twenty sheep."" So he did."
"515","515 - Muhammad ibn Ibrahim ibn Shabib al-'Assal al-Asbahani narrated to us, Isma'il ibn 'Amr al-Bajali narrated to us, Salamah ibn Salih narrated to us, from Abu Bakr ibn 'Abdullah, from Abu al-Malih, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), similar to it."
"516","Chapter: What has come regarding wearing turbans, supplication, and other matters. 516 - 'Abdan ibn Ahmad, Zakariyya ibn Yahya al-Saji, Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Hadrami and 'Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, they said: Ahmad ibn 'Abdah narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Humran narrated to us, Khalid al-Hadhdha' narrated to us, from Abu Tamimah al-Hujaymi, from Abu al-Malih, from his father Usamah, who said: I was riding behind the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when our camel stumbled. I said: Wretched be Satan. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Do not say: Wretched be Satan, for he grows until he becomes like a house, and says: By my strength. Say: In the Name of Allah, for he becomes like a fly."""
"517","517 - 'Ubayd al-'Ijli and 'Abdan ibn Ahmad narrated to us, both saying: al-Hasan ibn al-Sabbah al-Bazzar narrated to us, Abu al-Mundhir Isma'il ibn 'Umar narrated to us, Yunus ibn Abi Ishaq narrated to us, my son 'Isa narrated to me, from 'Ubaydullah ibn Abi Humayd, from Abu al-Malih ibn Usamah, from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Wear turbans; you will increase in forbearance."""
"518","518 - 'Abdan ibn Ahmad narrated to us, Abu Kamil al-Jahdari narrated to us, Yusuf ibn Khalid al-Samti narrated to us, al-Salt ibn Dinar narrated to us, from Abu al-Malih, from his father, who said: The angels descended on the day of Badr wearing turbans, and al-Zubayr was wearing a yellow turban that day."
"519","519 - Al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari and Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Hadrami narrated to us, both saying: Muhammad ibn Abi Saminah narrated to us, 'Abd al-Wahhab ibn 'Isa al-Tammar narrated to us, Yahya ibn Abi Zakariyya narrated to us, 'Abbad ibn Sa'id narrated to us, from Mubashshir ibn Abi al-Malih, from Abu al-Malih, from his father Usamah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: ""He does not thank Allah who does not thank people."""
"520","520 - Ishaq ibn Dawud al-Sawwaf al-Tustari narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn al-Mustamirr al-'Uruqi narrated to us, 'Abd al-Wahhab ibn 'Isa narrated to us, Yahya ibn Abi Zakariyya al-Ghassani narrated to me, 'Abbad ibn Sa'id narrated to me, from Mubashshir ibn Abi al-Malih, from his father, from his grandfather Usamah ibn 'Umayr, that he prayed with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the two rak'ahs of Fajr, praying close to him, and he prayed two light rak'ahs. I heard him saying: ""O Allah, Lord of Jibril, Mika'il, Israfil and Muhammad, I seek refuge in You from the Fire"" three times."
"521","521 - Abu Ghassan Ahmad ibn Sahl al-Sukkari al-Ahwazi narrated to us, Yazid ibn Hakim al-'Askari narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Maslamah narrated to us, from Layth, from Ziyad ibn Abi al-Malih, from his father Abu al-Malih, from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to the owner of the plot that was added to the mosque of Madinah - its owner was a man of the Ansar: ""For you in exchange is a house in Paradise."" A man came, then 'Uthman came and said to him: For you in exchange is ten thousand - so he bought it from him. Then 'Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him) came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, buy from me the plot I bought from the Ansari. He bought it from him for a house in Paradise. 'Uthman said: I bought it for ten thousand dirhams. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) placed a brick, then called Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him), who placed a brick, then called 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with him), who placed a brick, then 'Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him) came and placed a brick, then he said to the people: ""Place."" So they placed."
"522","522 - Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Hadrami narrated to us, he said: I heard Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah ibn Numayr saying: ""The name of Abu al-Malih is 'Amir ibn Usamah."""
"523","14 - Usamah ibn Akhdari. 523 - Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, Musaddad narrated to us, (ha) and Ahmad ibn 'Amr al-Qatarani narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Musa al-Harashi narrated to us, both saying: Bishr ibn al-Mufaddal narrated to us, from Bashir ibn Maymun, from his uncle Usamah ibn Akhdari: That a man of Banu Shuqrah called Asram was among the group who came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: He brought him a black Abyssinian slave boy he had bought in those lands and said: O Messenger of Allah, I bought this and I would like you to name him and supplicate for him for blessing. He said: ""What is your name?"" He said: Asram. He said: ""Rather you are Zur'ah."" He said: ""What do you want him for?"" He said: I want him as a shepherd. He said: ""Then he is 'Asim."" And the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) closed his fist."
"524","Chapter: Of those named Ubayy. 15 - The lineage of Ubayy ibn Ka'b (may Allah be pleased with him). 524 - Muhammad ibn 'Amr ibn Khalid al-Harrani narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us, from Abu al-Aswad, from 'Urwah: ""Among those who witnessed Badr: Ubayy ibn Ka'b ibn Qays ibn 'Ubayd ibn Zayd ibn Mu'awiyah ibn 'Amr ibn Malik ibn al-Najjar."""
"525","The description of Ubayy ibn Ka'b and his kunyah (may Allah be pleased with him). 525 - 'Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, my father narrated to us, Hushaym narrated to us, from Yunus and Mubarak, from al-Hasan: 'Utayy al-Sa'di informed us, who said: I saw Ubayy ibn Ka'b (may Allah be pleased with him) white of head and beard, not dyeing."
"526","526 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, 'Abd al-Razzaq informed us, al-Thawri informed us, from Sa'id al-Jurayri, from Abu al-Salil, from 'Abdullah ibn Rabah, from Ubayy ibn Ka'b, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""May knowledge delight you, Abu al-Mundhir."""
"527","527 - Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Hadrami narrated to us, Muhammad ibn al-Husayn ibn Ishkab narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Kunasa narrated to us, from Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid, from Zirr ibn Hubaysh, who said: There was harshness in Ubayy."
"528","528 - 'Ali ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz narrated to us, Abu Ghassan narrated to us, al-Hasan ibn Salih narrated to us, from Mutarrif, from al-Sha'bi, from Masruq, who said: The judges among the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) were six: 'Umar, 'Ali, 'Abdullah, Ubayy, Zayd and Abu Musa."
"529","The age of Ubayy ibn Ka'b and his death (may Allah be pleased with him). 529 - Abu al-Zinba' Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, Yahya ibn Bukayr narrated to us, who said: ""Ubayy ibn Ka'b (may Allah be pleased with him), kunyah Abu al-Mundhir, died in Madinah in the year twenty-two; and one saying: in the year thirty, during the caliphate of 'Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him)."""
"530","530 - 'Ubayd ibn Ghannam ibn Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah ibn Numayr narrated to us, who said: ""Ubayy ibn Ka'b (may Allah be pleased with him) died during the caliphate of 'Umar in the year twenty-two."" Ibn Numayr said: And some say: ""During the caliphate of 'Uthman (may Allah be pleased with them)."""
"531","And of what Ubayy ibn Ka'b (may Allah be pleased with him) transmitted. 531 - 'Ali ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz narrated to us, Abu Hudhayfah narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Yunus ibn 'Ubayd, from al-Hasan, from 'Utayy, from Ubayy ibn Ka'b (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""The food of the son of Adam has been made a parable for this world; look at what comes out of the son of Adam, even if he spices and salts it - it is known what it becomes."""
"532","532 - Abu Muslim al-Kashshi narrated to us, 'Uthman ibn al-Haytham narrated to us, 'Awf narrated to us, from al-Hasan, from 'Utayy, from Ubayy ibn Ka'b (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""When you see a man boasting with the boasting of Jahiliyyah, make him bite (the father's member) and do not use a euphemism."""
"533","533 - 'Ali ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz narrated to us, Muslim ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, Shu'bah narrated to us, from 'Ali ibn Zayd, from Yusuf ibn Mihran, from Ibn 'Abbas, from Ubayy ibn Ka'b (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The last verse revealed upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was: {There has come to you a Messenger from among yourselves} to the end of the verse."
"534","534 - Abu Muslim al-Kashshi narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn Nusayr narrated to us, Abu Umayyah ibn Ya'la al-Thaqafi narrated to us, from Musa ibn 'Uqbah, from Ishaq ibn Yahya al-Ansari, from 'Ubadah ibn al-Samit, from Ubayy ibn Ka'b, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Whoever is pleased that a building be raised for him, and that his ranks be elevated, let him pardon one who wronged him, give one who deprived him, and join one who cut him off."""
"535","535 - 'Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, Ja'far ibn Mihran al-Sabbak narrated to us, 'Abd al-Warith ibn Sa'id narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Juhadah, from al-Hakam, from Mujahid, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Ubayy ibn Ka'b, that Jibril (peace be upon him) came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was at the pond of Banu Ghifar and said: O Muhammad, Allah (Mighty and Majestic) commands you to teach your ummah the Qur'an upon one letter. Then he said: Upon two letters, until it ended at seven letters; whoever reads a letter of them, it is as He said."
"536","536 - 'Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, al-Hasan ibn Qaza'ah narrated to me, Sufyan ibn Habib narrated to us, from Shu'bah, from Thuwayr ibn Abi Fakhitah, from his father, from al-Tufayl ibn Ubayy, from his father, that he heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: {And He made binding upon them the word of piety}. He said: ""There is no god but Allah."""
"537","537 - 'Amr ibn Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Zibriq al-Himsi narrated to us, my father narrated to me, 'Amr ibn al-Harith narrated to us, from 'Abdullah ibn Salim, from al-Zubaydi, from al-Zuhri: Ishaq, the freed slave of al-Mughirah ibn Nawfal, informed me, from Ubayy ibn Ka'b al-Ansari (may Allah be pleased with him), that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""The Hour will not come until the Euphrates recedes from a mountain of gold, over which nine-tenths of them fight."""
"538","538 - 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Muslim al-Razi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Mihran al-Jammal narrated to us, Mubashshir ibn Isma'il narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Mutarrif Abu Ghassan, from Abu Hazim, from Sahl ibn Sa'd: Ubayy ibn Ka'b (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated to me that the fatwa they used to give - that water is from water (ghusl required only after ejaculation) - was a concession the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had granted at the beginning of time; then afterwards we were commanded to wash."
"539","539 - Ahmad ibn Khalil al-Halabi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn 'Isa al-Tabba' narrated to us, Mu'adh ibn Muhammad ibn Mu'adh ibn Ubayy ibn Ka'b narrated to us, from his father, from his grandfather, from Ubayy ibn Ka'b (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""O Abu al-Mundhir, I have been commanded to present the Qur'an to you."" He said: By Allah I believed, by your hand I submitted, and from you I learned. He said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) repeated the statement. He said: O Messenger of Allah, and was I mentioned there? He said: ""Yes, by your name and lineage in the highest assembly."" He said: Then recite, O Messenger of Allah."
"540","540 - Ahmad ibn Khulayd al-Halabi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn 'Isa al-Tabba' narrated to us, Mu'adh ibn Muhammad ibn Mu'adh ibn Ubayy ibn Ka'b narrated to us, from his father, from his grandfather, from Ubayy ibn Ka'b (may Allah be pleased with him), that he said: O Messenger of Allah, what is the reward of fever? He said: ""Good deeds flow for its owner as long as a foot throbs upon him or a vein beats upon him."" Ubayy said: ""O Allah, I ask You for a fever that does not prevent me from going out in Your path, nor going out to Your House, nor to the mosque of Your Prophet."" He said: Ubayy was never touched except that he had fever."
"541","541 - Al-'Abbas ibn al-Fadl al-Asfati narrated to us, Musa ibn Isma'il narrated to us, Aban ibn Yazid narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from al-Hadrami ibn Lahiq, from Muhammad ibn Ubayy ibn Ka'b, from his father, that he had a date-pile of dates which would decrease. He guarded it one night, and behold a beast like an adolescent boy. He greeted him, and he returned the greeting. He said: What are you, jinn or man? He said: No, rather jinn. He said: Give me your hand. He gave him his hand, and behold his hand was a dog's hand, and his hair a dog's hair. He said: Thus is the creation of the jinn. He said: The jinn knew that among them there is no man stronger than me. He said: What brought you? He said: We heard that you love charity, so we came to take some of your food. He said: What saves us from you? He said: This verse in Surah al-Baqarah: {Allah - there is no god but He, the Ever-Living, the Sustainer}. Whoever says it in the evening is protected from us until morning, and whoever says it in the morning is protected from us until evening. In the morning he came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and mentioned that to him, and he said: ""The wicked one spoke the truth."""
"542","542 - Muhammad ibn 'Ali al-Marwazi narrated to us, Husayn ibn Sa'd ibn 'Ali ibn al-Husayn ibn Waqid narrated to us, my father narrated to me, from my grandfather, from al-Husayn ibn Waqid, from 'Ata' ibn al-Sa'ib, from al-Sha'bi, from Ibn 'Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him), from Ubayy ibn Ka'b (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: ""If man had two valleys of wealth, he would seek a third; nothing fills the belly of man except dust. Then Allah (Mighty and Majestic) turns to whoever repents."""
"543","543 - Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Hadrami narrated to us, Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Rahman al-'Anbari narrated to us, Umayyah ibn Khalid narrated to us, Abu al-Jariyah al-'Abdi narrated to us, from Shu'bah, from Abu Ishaq, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn 'Abbas, from Ubayy ibn Ka'b, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): He read: {You have reached from me} with shaddah (laddunni)."
"544","16 - Ubayy ibn Malik al-Qushayri (may Allah be pleased with him). 544 - 'Umar ibn Hafs al-Sadusi narrated to us, 'Asim ibn 'Ali narrated to us, (ha) and Muhammad ibn 'Abdus ibn Kamil narrated to us, 'Ali ibn al-Ja'd narrated to us, (ha) and 'Uthman ibn 'Umar al-Dabbi narrated to us, 'Amr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, they said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Zurarah ibn Awfa, from Ubayy ibn Malik, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: ""Whoever reaches his parents or one of them, then enters the Fire, Allah has distanced him."""
"545","17 - Ubayy ibn 'Umarah al-Ansari (may Allah be pleased with him). 545 - Bishr ibn Musa narrated to us, Yahya ibn Ishaq al-Saylahini narrated to us, Yahya ibn Ayyub narrated to us, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Razin, from Muhammad ibn Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Ayyub ibn Qatan al-Kindi, from Ibn 'Umarah al-Ansari - and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had prayed in his house facing both qiblahs - who said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, may I wipe over the khuffs? He said: ""Yes, for a day."" I said: O Messenger of Allah, and for two days? He said: ""Yes, and three."" I said: O Messenger of Allah, and three? He said: ""Yes, and whatever you wish."""
"546","546 - Al-Hasan ibn Ghulayb al-Misri narrated to us, Sa'id ibn 'Ufayr narrated to us, Yahya ibn Ayyub narrated to us, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Razin, from Muhammad ibn Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Ayyub ibn Qatan, from 'Ubadah ibn Nusayy, from Ubayy ibn 'Umarah al-Ansari, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed in his house. I said: O Messenger of Allah, may I wipe over the khuffs? He said: ""Yes."" I said: A day? He said: ""Yes, and two days."" He said: I said: And three, O Messenger of Allah? He said: ""Yes, and whatever seems good to you."""
"547","Chapter: Of those named Usayd. 18 - The lineage of Usayd ibn Hudayr, 'Aqabi, Badri, kunyah Abu 'Atik; and it is said: Abu Yahya (may Allah be pleased with him). 547 - Muhammad ibn 'Amr ibn Khalid al-Harrani narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us, from Abu al-Aswad, from 'Urwah: ""Among those who witnessed al-'Aqabah: Usayd ibn Hudayr ibn Simak ibn 'Ubayd ibn Rafi' ibn Imri' al-Qays ibn Zayd ibn 'Abd al-Ashhal, and he is a naqib."""
"548","548 - Abu al-Zinba' Rawh ibn al-Faraj al-Misri narrated to us, Yahya ibn Bukayr narrated to us, who said: Usayd ibn Hudayr, kunyah Abu Yahya, died in the year twenty, and 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) carried him between the two posts of the bier until he placed him in al-Baqi' and prayed over him."
"549","549 - Muhammad ibn 'Ali al-Madini narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Sa'id al-Jawhari narrated to us, from al-Waqidi, who said: ""Usayd ibn Hudayr died in the year twenty, in Sha'ban."""
"550","550 - Al-Hasan ibn Harun ibn Sulayman al-Asbahani narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Musayyibi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Fulayh narrated to us, from Musa ibn 'Uqbah, from Ibn Shihab: ""In the naming of those who witnessed al-'Aqabah of the Ansar, then of Banu 'Abd al-Ashhal: Usayd ibn Hudayr, and he is a naqib."""
"551","And of what Usayd ibn Hudayr (may Allah be pleased with him) transmitted. 551 - Idris ibn Ja'far al-'Attar narrated to us, Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, (ha) and Ahmad ibn al-Qasim ibn Musawir al-Jawhari narrated to us, 'Affan ibn Muslim narrated to us, both saying: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Anas, from Usayd ibn Hudayr, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""You will see after me favoritism."" They said: What do you command us? He said: ""Be patient until you meet me at the Hawd."""
"552","552 - Ahmad ibn Zayd ibn al-Harish al-Ahwazi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ma'mar al-Bahrani narrated to us, Harami ibn 'Umarah narrated to us, Shu'bah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Anas, from Usayd ibn Hudayr (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""The Ansar are my inner garment and my outer garment; people will increase while they decrease, so accept from their doers of good and overlook their doers of evil."""
"553","553 - 'Ali ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz and Abu Muslim al-Kashshi narrated to us, both saying: Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, (ha) and Abu Muslim al-Kashshi narrated to us, Abu 'Umar al-Darir narrated to us, both saying: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn 'Amr, from his father, from his grandfather, from 'A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) returned from a journey, he would alight at Dhu al-Hulayfah, and the children would come out to them and inform them about their families. Usayd ibn Hudayr was informed of his wife's death, so he wept. It was said to him: Do you weep? He said: Why should I not weep, when I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: ""The Throne shook its timbers for the death of Sa'd ibn Mu'adh."""
"554","553 / 2 - 'Ubayd ibn Ghannam narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, Muhammad ibn 'Amr narrated to us, from his father, from his grandfather, from 'A'ishah, from Usayd ibn Hudayr (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""The Throne shook for the death of Sa'd ibn Mu'adh."""
"555","554 - Yahya ibn Ayyub al-'Allaf al-Misri narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, Yahya ibn Ayyub and Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us, both saying: 'Umarah ibn Ghaziyyah narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah ibn 'Amr ibn 'Uthman, from his mother Fatimah bint Husayn (may Allah be pleased with him), from 'A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her), that she said: Usayd ibn Hudayr was among the most excellent of people, and he would say: ""If I were as I am in one of three states, I would be of the people of Paradise, and I have no doubt about that: when I recite the Qur'an, or when I hear it recited, when I hear the sermon of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and when I witness a funeral. I never witnessed a funeral but that I spoke to myself of only what will be done with it and what it will come to."""
"556","555 - 'Ali ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz narrated to us, Hawdhah ibn Khalifah narrated to us, Ibn Jurayj narrated to us, 'Ikrimah ibn Khalid narrated to me that Usayd ibn Hudayr ibn Simak narrated to him, who said: Mu'awiyah (may Allah be pleased with him) wrote to Marwan ibn al-Hakam: When a man is stolen from and finds his stolen property, he is more entitled to it when he finds it. Marwan wrote to me of that, while I was his governor over al-Yamamah. I wrote to Marwan that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) judged that when it is found with an unaccused man, its master may take it for the price if he wishes, and if he wishes he may pursue his thief. Then Abu Bakr, 'Umar and 'Uthman (may Allah be pleased with them) judged likewise after him. Marwan sent my letter to Mu'awiyah, and Mu'awiyah sent to Marwan: Neither you nor Usayd judge over me in what I have been put in charge of; rather I judge over you both, so carry out what I ordered you. Marwan sent Mu'awiyah's letter to me, and I said: By Allah, I will never judge by it."
"557","556 - 'Ali ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz narrated to us, 'Amr ibn 'Awn al-Wasiti narrated to us, Khalid narrated to us, from Husayn ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Usayd ibn Hudayr, who said: While he was with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - and there was jesting in him - entertaining the people to make them laugh, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) poked him in his side. He said: Let me retaliate. He said: ""Take retaliation."" He said: You have a shirt on, and I have no shirt on. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) raised his shirt, and he embraced him and began kissing his side, saying: This is what I wanted, O Messenger of Allah."
"558","557 - Al-Hasan ibn al-'Abbas al-Razi narrated to us, Hafs ibn 'Umar al-Mihraqani narrated to us, 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Abdullah al-Dashtaki narrated to us, Abu Ja'far al-Razi narrated to us, from Husayn, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Usayd ibn Hudayr, that a man would make people laugh in the presence of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said with his finger in his side. He said: You have killed me, O Messenger of Allah. He said: ""Take retaliation."" He said: You have a shirt on, and I had no shirt on. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) raised his shirt until his side appeared, and he embraced him and began kissing his side, saying: By my father and mother, this is what I wanted."
"559","558 - 'Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, Hudbah ibn Khalid narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from al-Hajjaj, from 'Abdullah ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, from his father, from Usayd ibn Hudayr (may Allah be pleased with him), that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Perform wudu from camel meat"" - the hadith."
"560","559 - 'Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, Abu Ma'mar al-Qati'i Isma'il ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, 'Abbad ibn al-'Awwam narrated to us, from al-Hajjaj ibn Artah, from 'Abdullah ibn 'Abdullah al-Razi, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Usayd ibn Hudayr, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Pray in sheepfolds, and do not perform wudu from their milk; do not pray in camel resting-places, and perform wudu from their milk."""
"561","560 - Ishaq ibn Dawud al-Sawwaf al-Tustari narrated to us, 'Abd al-Quddus ibn Muhammad al-'Attar narrated to us, 'Amr ibn 'Asim narrated to us, Abu al-'Awwam narrated to us, al-Hajjaj ibn Artah narrated to us, from 'Abdullah ibn 'Abdullah, judge of Rayy, from Ibn Abi Layla, from Usayd ibn Hudayr, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), similar to it."
"562","561 - Ahmad ibn Hammad ibn Zughbah narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, Yahya ibn Ayyub narrated to us, from Ibn al-Had, from 'Abdullah ibn Khabbab, from Abu Sa'id, from Usayd ibn Hudayr, that while he was reciting Surah al-Baqarah with his horse tied."
"563","562 - Al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari narrated to us, 'Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Bishr narrated to us, Muhammad ibn 'Amr narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ibrahim al-Taymi narrated to us, from Mahmud ibn Labid, that Usayd ibn Hudayr was among the people with the best voices in the Qur'an. He recited one night with his horse tied near him and his son sleeping beside him. The horse turned in its tether, so he recited, and the horse turned in its tether; he recited, and the horse turned in its tether. He left, took his son, fearing the horse would trample him. In the morning he mentioned that to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Recite, Usayd, for the angels never ceased listening to your voice; had you recited, a canopy between heaven and earth would have remained by morning, people seeing the angels in it."""
"564","563 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, from 'Abd al-Razzaq, from Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri and Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Salamah: While Usayd ibn Hudayr (may Allah be pleased with him) was praying at night, he said: A cloud-like thing enveloped me, in it like lamps, with the woman sleeping beside me while pregnant, and the horse tied in the house. I feared the horse would bolt and frighten the woman so she would miscarry. I left my prayer, and mentioned that to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the morning. He said: ""Recite it, O Usayd, for that is an angel listening to the Qur'an."""
"565","564 - Al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari narrated to us, Yahya al-Himmani narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn 'Ayyash narrated to us, from 'Asim, from Zirr, from Usayd ibn Hudayr, that he came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, I was reciting Surah al-Kahf last night when something came until it covered my mouth. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""That is tranquility coming to listen to the Qur'an."""
"566","565 - Muhammad ibn Ruzayq ibn Jami' al-Misri narrated to us, Harun ibn Sa'id al-Ayli narrated to us, Anas ibn 'Iyad narrated to us, from 'Ubaydullah ibn 'Umar, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Usayd ibn Hudayr, who said: I was praying on a moonlit night with my horse secured; it moved about, and I was startled, then it moved about again. I raised my head, and behold a canopy enveloping me, coming between me and the moon. I was startled and entered the house. In the morning I mentioned that to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: ""Those are the angels coming to listen to your Qur'an at the end of the night, Surah al-Baqarah."" And Usayd was of beautiful voice."
"567","566 - 'Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, Hudbah ibn Khalid narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Thabit, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Usayd ibn Hudayr, that he said: O Messenger of Allah, while I was reciting Surah al-Baqarah I heard a sound behind me and thought my horse had been released. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Recite it, O Abu 'Atik."" He said: I turned, and behold lamps suspended between heaven and earth, while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was saying: ""Recite it, O Abu 'Atik."" He said: O Messenger of Allah, I could not continue. He said: ""Those are the angels descending for the recitation of Surah al-Baqarah; had you continued, you would have seen wonders."""
"568","567 - Musa ibn Harun narrated to us, Ishaq ibn Rahawayh narrated to us, Mu'adh ibn Hisham informed us, my father narrated to me, from Qatadah, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Usayd ibn Hudayr, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), similar to it."
"569","568 - Al-Fadl ibn al-'Abbas al-Asbahani narrated to us, Bashshar ibn Musa al-Khaffaf narrated to us, (ha) and al-Hasan ibn 'Ali al-Ma'mari narrated to us, Masruq ibn al-Marzuban narrated to us, both saying: Yahya ibn Zakariyya ibn Abi Za'idah narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from Husayn ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Amr ibn Sa'd ibn Mu'adh, from Mahmud ibn Labid, from Ibn Shafi', who was a physician, who said: I cut the sciatic vein of Usayd ibn Hudayr, and he narrated to me two hadiths. He said: Two households of my people came to me and said: Speak for us to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to apportion to us some of these dates. I came to him and spoke to him. He said: ""Yes, we apportion to each household half; and if Allah returns to us, we shall return to them."" I said: May Allah reward you well on our behalf. He said: ""And you - may Allah reward you well on my behalf, O company of Ansar, for you, as far as I know, are chaste and patient. You will meet after me favoritism, so be patient until you meet me."""
"570","19 - Usayd ibn Zuhayr (may Allah be pleased with him). 569 - Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Qirmiti al-Baghdadi, of the descendants of 'Amir ibn Rabi'ah, narrated to us, 'Uthman ibn Ya'qub al-'Uthmani narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Talhah al-Taymi narrated to us, Bashir ibn Thabit ibn Usayd ibn Zuhayr narrated to us, (ha) and he also narrated to me from his sister Su'da bint Thabit, from their father Thabit, from their grandfather Usayd ibn Zuhayr (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) deemed Rafi' ibn Khadij too young on the day of Uhud. His uncle Zuhayr, may Allah have mercy on him, said to him: O Messenger of Allah, he is a skilled archer. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted him. He was struck by an arrow in his chest. His uncle brought him to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: My nephew has been struck by an arrow. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""If you wish that we remove it, we shall remove it; and if you wish to leave it, then if he dies with it in him, he dies a martyr."""
"571","570 - 'Ubayd ibn Ghannam narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, (ha) and al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari narrated to us, 'Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, both saying: Abu Usamah narrated to us, from 'Abd al-Hamid ibn Ja'far: Abu al-Abrad, freed slave of Banu Khatmah, narrated to us that he heard Usayd ibn Zuhayr al-Ansari narrating from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: ""A prayer in the mosque of Quba' is like an 'umrah."""
"572","571 - Ibrahim ibn Na'ilah al-Asbahani narrated to us, al-Salt ibn Mas'ud al-Jahdari narrated to us, Khalid ibn al-Harith narrated to us, 'Abd al-Hamid ibn Ja'far narrated to us, who said: My father informed me, from Rafi' ibn Usayd ibn Zuhayr, from his father, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade leasing land."
"573","20 - Usayd ibn Yarbu' al-Ansari, martyred on the day of al-Yamamah (may Allah be pleased with him). 572 - Muhammad ibn 'Amr ibn Khalid al-Harrani narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us, from Abu al-Aswad, from 'Urwah: ""In the naming of those killed on the day of al-Yamamah of the Ansar, of Banu Sa'idah: Usayd ibn Yarbu'."""
"574","573 - Al-Hasan ibn Harun ibn Sulayman al-Asbahani narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Musayyibi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Fulayh narrated to us, from Musa ibn 'Uqbah, from Ibn Shihab: ""In the naming of those martyred on the day of al-Yamamah of the Ansar, of Banu Sa'idah: Usayd ibn Yarbu'."""
"575","21 - Usayd ibn Malik Abu 'Amrah al-Ansari; and it is said: Bashir ibn 'Amr ibn Mihsan (may Allah be pleased with him); and it is said: Tha'labah ibn 'Amr ibn Mihsan; and it is said: 'Amr ibn Mihsan, of Banu Mazin ibn al-Najjar; and it is said: Abu 'Amrah gave 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) on the day of Siffin one hundred thousand dirhams aiding him with it on the day of the Camel; and he was killed on the day of Siffin. 574 - Muhammad ibn 'Ali al-Madini Fustuqah narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Sa'id al-Jawhari narrated to us, from al-Waqidi, who said: ""And in it Abu 'Amrah al-Mazini died in the year thirty-seven."""
"576","Chapter: Of the proofs of the prophethood of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). 575 - 'Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Yusuf al-Firyabi narrated to us, al-Awza'i narrated to us, (ha) and Abu 'Abd al-Malik Ahmad ibn Ibrahim al-Qurashi narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn 'Abdullah ibn al-'Ala' ibn Zabr al-Himsi narrated to us, my father 'Abdullah ibn al-'Ala' narrated to me, from al-Zuhri and al-Awza'i, both saying: al-Muttalib ibn 'Abdullah ibn Hantab narrated to us, 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi 'Amrah al-Ansari narrated to me, my father narrated to me, who said: We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on a campaign he fought. The people suffered hunger, so the people asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for permission to slaughter some of their mounts. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was about to permit them that, when 'Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) said: What do you think, O Messenger of Allah - if we slaughter our mounts, then meet our enemy tomorrow while hungry on foot? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""What do you think, O 'Umar?"" He said: Call the people with the remnants of their provisions, then supplicate for us in them for blessing, for Allah (Mighty and Majestic) will suffice us through your supplication, if Allah wills. He said: It was as if a covering was upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then it was removed. He called for a cloth, ordered it, and it was spread. Then he called the people with the remnants of their provisions, and they brought what they had - some brought a bowl of food, or a handful, and some brought the like of an egg. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered it placed upon that cloth. Then he supplicated in it for blessing and spoke what Allah willed him to speak. Then he called out to the army, and they came. Then he ordered them, and they ate, were satisfied, filled their vessels and provision bags. Then he called for a small vessel placed before him, then called for water, poured it in it, then rinsed his mouth in it and spoke what Allah willed him to speak. Then he inserted his little finger in it - I swear by Allah, I saw the fingers of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gushing springs of water. Then he ordered the people, and they drank, watered, filled their skins and vessels. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) laughed until his molars appeared, then said: ""I bear witness that there is no god but Allah alone, without partner, and that Muhammad is His servant and Messenger; no one meets Allah with them on the Day of Resurrection except that he enters Paradise, whatever he was upon."""
"577","576 - Al-Hasan ibn Ghulayb al-Misri narrated to us, Sa'id ibn 'Ufayr narrated to us, Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Bukayr ibn 'Abdullah ibn al-Ashajj, from Bayhas al-Thaqafi, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi 'Amrah al-Ansari, from his father - I know it only from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - that it was said to him: O Messenger of Allah, what of one who believes in you without seeing you, and affirms you without seeing you - what is for them? He said: ""Glad tidings for them, twice; those are of us, those are with us."""
"578","22 - Usayr ibn 'Amr Abu Salit al-Ansari, Badri; and it is said: Usayrah ibn Malik ibn 'Adi ibn 'Amir ibn Ghanm ibn 'Adi ibn al-Najjar (may Allah be pleased with him). 577 - Al-Hasan ibn Harun ibn Sulayman al-Asbahani narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Musayyibi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Fulayh narrated to us, from Musa ibn 'Uqbah, from Ibn Shihab: ""In the naming of those who witnessed Badr of the Ansar: Abu Salit Usayr ibn 'Amr."""
"579","Chapter: Regarding the prohibition of domestic donkeys. 578 - Ahmad ibn 'Abdullah al-Bazzaz al-Tustari narrated to us, al-Hasan ibn Yahya al-Uruzzi narrated to us, 'Abdullah ibn Harun ibn Abi 'Isa narrated to us, my father narrated to me, from Ibn Ishaq: 'Abdullah ibn Hamzah al-Fazari narrated to us, from 'Abdullah ibn Sulayt, from his father Abu Salit, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade eating the meat of tame donkeys while the pots were boiling, so we overturned them upon their faces."
"580","579 - 'Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz narrated to us, Harun ibn 'Abdullah narrated to us, (ha) and Musa ibn Harun narrated to us, my father narrated to us, both saying: Muhammad ibn al-Hasan al-Makhzumi narrated to us, Yahya ibn Muhammad ibn Marwan ibn 'Abdullah ibn Abi Salit al-Ansari ibn 'Amr narrated to us, who said: The people suffered severe hunger in the campaign of Khaybar. They went to their donkeys in the presence of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), slaughtered them, then placed them in the pots. While they were boiling, its prohibition came down upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""The prohibition of the donkeys you are cooking has come down."" So the pots were overturned upon their faces."
"581","580 - Narrated to us Ubayd ibn Ghannam, narrated to us Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah, narrated to us Abdullah ibn Numayr, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Ubaydullah ibn Amr ibn Damrah al-Fazari, from Abdullah ibn Abi Salit, from his father Abu Salit, and he was a Badr veteran, he said: The prohibition of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) against eating the meat of donkeys reached us, while the pots were boiling with it, so we overturned them on their faces."
"582","Chapter of those named Aws; 23 - from what Aws ibn Aws al-Thaqafi (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated; chapter on ghusl on Friday and going early. 581 - Narrated to us Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Mamar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Qilabah, from Abu al-Ashath al-Sanani, from Aws ibn Aws (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever washes and performs ghusl on Friday, and goes early and arrives early, and draws near to the imam and listens attentively, will have for every step he takes the reward of fasting a year and standing it in prayer, and that is easy for Allah."
"583","582 - Narrated to us Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Said ibn Abi Maryam, narrated to us Muhammad ibn Yusuf al-Firyabi, narrated to us Sufyan, from Abdullah ibn Isa, from Yahya ibn al-Harith, from Abu al-Ashath, from Aws ibn Aws al-Thaqafi, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever washes and goes early in the morning, then sits close to the imam and listens and keeps silent, will have for every step he takes the reward of a year, its fasting and its standing in prayer."
"584","583 - Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Hadrami, narrated to us Abdullah ibn Umar ibn Aban, narrated to us Abd al-Rahim ibn Sulayman, from Abu al-Hakam al-Araj, from Abdullah ibn Isa, from Yahya ibn al-Harith, from Abu al-Ashath al-Sanani, from Aws ibn Aws al-Thaqafi, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said regarding Friday: Whoever washes and performs ghusl and goes early, and draws near and listens, will have for every step he takes from his house to the mosque the reward of a year, its fasting and its standing in prayer."
"585","584 - Narrated to us Abu Khalifah, narrated to us Ali ibn al-Madini, narrated to us al-Walid ibn Muslim, from Ibn Jabir, he said: I heard Abu al-Ashath al-Sanani saying: I heard Aws ibn Aws al-Thaqafi reporting from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: Whoever washes and performs ghusl on Friday, then goes in the morning and arrives early, and walks and does not ride, and does not engage in idle talk, recorded for him is the deed of a year. Ibn Jabir said: So I narrated this hadith to Yahya ibn al-Harith al-Dhimari, and he said: I heard Abu al-Ashath narrating it from Aws ibn Aws from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he said: For every footstep is the deed of a year, its fasting and its standing. Ibn Jabir said: So Yahya remembered and I forgot. Al-Walid said: I mentioned that to Abu Amr al-Awzai, and he said: The hadith is established that for every footstep is the deed of a year."
"586","585 - Narrated to us al-Hasan ibn Ali al-Mamari and Ubayd ibn Ghannam, both said: Narrated to us Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah, narrated to us Ibn al-Mubarak, narrated to us al-Awzai, narrated to me Hassan ibn Atiyyah, narrated to me Abu al-Ashath al-Sanani, narrated to me Aws ibn Aws, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Whoever washes and performs ghusl on Friday, then goes early and arrives early, and walks and does not ride, and draws near and listens, and does not engage in idle talk, will have for every step he takes the deed of a year, its fasting and its standing in prayer."
"587","586 - Narrated to us Musa ibn Harun, narrated to us Mansur ibn Abi Muzahim, narrated to us Yazid ibn Yusuf, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid ibn Jabir, from Abu al-Ashath al-Sanani, from Abu Asma al-Rahabi, from Aws ibn Aws (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever washes and performs ghusl and goes in the morning, and arrives early and walks and does not ride, and draws near the imam and does not engage in idle talk, will have the reward of a year, its fasting and its standing in prayer."
"588","587 - Narrated to us Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Ibn Jurayj, from Umar ibn Muhammad, from Said ibn Abi Hilal, from Muhammad ibn Said al-Asadi, from Aws ibn Aws, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: When it is Friday, and one of you washes his head and performs ghusl, then goes in the morning and arrives early, and draws near and listens and keeps silent, he will have for every step he takes the fasting of a year and the standing of a year."
"589","588 - Narrated to us Abu Habib Yahya ibn Nafi al-Misri, narrated to us Said ibn Abi Maryam, (Ha) and narrated to us Ahmad ibn Rishdin, narrated to us Ahmad ibn Salih, both said: Narrated to us Ibn Wahb, Amr ibn al-Harith informed me that Said ibn Abi Hilal narrated to him, from Muhammad ibn Said, from Ubadah ibn Nusayy, from Aws (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Whoever washes and performs ghusl on Friday, then goes in the morning or goes at noon or arrives early, then draws near and keeps silent and listens, will have, to the measure of every step he takes, as the reward of standing a year in prayer and fasting a year."
"590","Chapter on the excellence of Friday. 589 - Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah, narrated to me my father, (Ha) and narrated to me al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari, both said: Narrated to us Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah, narrated to us Husayn ibn Ali al-Jufi, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid ibn Jabir, from Abu al-Ashath al-Sanani, from Aws ibn Aws (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The best of your days is Friday; in it Adam was created, in it is the blowing of the Trumpet, and in it is the swoon. So send abundant salutations upon me in it, for your salutations are presented to me. They said: O Messenger of Allah, how will our salutations be presented to you when you have decayed? He said, meaning: become worn out. He said: Allah the Mighty and Majestic has forbidden the earth to consume the bodies of the Prophets."
"591","Chapter. 590 - Narrated to us Ahmad ibn al-Mualla al-Dimashqi the judge, narrated to us Hisham ibn Ammar, (Ha) and narrated to us Abdan ibn Ahmad, narrated to us Hisham ibn Khalid, both said: Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Shuayb, narrated to us Yazid ibn Ubaydah, from Abu al-Ashath al-Sanani, from Aws ibn Aws, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Isa son of Maryam (peace be upon him) will descend at the white minaret east of Damascus."
"592","591 - Narrated to us Musa ibn Harun, narrated to us Dawud ibn Amr al-Dabbi, narrated to us Ismail ibn Ayyash, narrated to me Abd al-Rahman ibn Abdullah ibn Muhayriz, from his father, from Aws ibn Aws (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever lies about his Prophet, or about his own eyes, or about his parents, will not smell the fragrance of Paradise."
"593","Chapter on magnifying the statement La ilaha illa Allah and that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah. 592 - Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Abdus ibn Kamil and Muhammad ibn Jafar al-Razi, both said: Narrated to us Ali ibn al-Jad, narrated to us Shubah, from al-Numan ibn Salim, he said: I heard Aws ibn Aws al-Thaqafi saying: I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with the delegation of Thaqif, and I was in the lower part of the qiblah area where no one was except the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sleeping, when a man came to him and spoke to him privately, then he said: Go and kill him. Then he said: Does he not testify that there is no god but Allah and that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah? He said: Yes. He said: I have been commanded to fight the people until they say La ilaha illa Allah; when they say it, their blood and wealth become forbidden to me except by its right."
"594","593 - Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Khalid al-Harrani, narrated to me my father, narrated to us Zuhayr, narrated to us Simak ibn Harb, from al-Numan ibn Salim, from Aws, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered upon us while we were in a dome in the mosque of Madinah, and he took hold of part of the dome, then a man came to him and spoke to him privately about something unknown. He said: Go and tell them to kill him. Then he said: Perhaps he testifies that there is no god but Allah? He said: Yes. He said: Go and tell them to release him, for I have been commanded to fight the people until they say La ilaha illa Allah; when they say it, their blood and wealth become forbidden to me except by a due right, and their reckoning is upon Allah the Mighty and Majestic."
"595","594 - Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Hayyan al-Mazini, narrated to us Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi, narrated to us Abu Awanah, from Simak, from al-Numan ibn Salim, from Aws, he said: A man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) speaking to him privately while I was listening, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Go to them and tell them to kill him. Then he called him back after he had gone, and said: Perhaps he says La ilaha illa Allah? He said: Yes. He said: Tell them to release him, for it has been revealed to me that I fight the people until they say La ilaha illa Allah; when they say it, their wealth and blood become forbidden to me except by its right, and their due is upon Allah."
"596","595 - Narrated to us Ubayd ibn Ghannam, narrated to us Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah, narrated to us Abdullah ibn Bakr al-Sahmi, narrated to us Hatim ibn Abi Sagheerah, from Simak ibn Harb, from al-Numan ibn Salim, that Amr ibn Aws informed him that his father Aws informed him, he said: While we were sitting with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he was narrating to us and reminding us, a man came to him and spoke to him privately. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Go and kill him. When the man turned away, he called him back and said: Does he testify that there is no god but Allah? He said: Yes. He said: Take him and let him go free, for I have only been commanded to fight the people until they say La ilaha illa Allah; when they do that, their blood and wealth become forbidden to me by its right, and their reckoning is upon Allah."
"597","Chapter. 596 - Narrated to us Ibrahim ibn Nailah al-Asbahani, narrated to us Ismail ibn Amr al-Bajali, narrated to us Qays ibn al-Rabi, from Umayr ibn Abdullah, from Abd al-Malik ibn al-Mughirah al-Taifi, from Aws ibn Aws al-Thaqafi, he said: We stayed with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for half a month; I saw him turn away to his right, and I saw him turn away to his left, and I saw his sandals, which had two straps."
"598","597 - Narrated to us al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari and Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Hadrami, both said: Yahya al-Himmani informed us, narrated to us Qays ibn al-Rabi, from Umayr ibn Abdullah al-Khathami, from Abd al-Malik ibn al-Mughirah al-Taifi, from Aws ibn Aws, he said: I stayed with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for half a month; I saw him praying wearing two strapped sandals, and I saw him spitting to his right and to his left."
"599","Chapter on what Allah has prepared for Uthman ibn Affan (may Allah be pleased with him) in Paradise. 598 - Narrated to us al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari, narrated to us Ishaq ibn Wahb al-Allaf, narrated to us al-Fadl ibn Sawwar al-Basri, narrated to us Layth ibn Sad, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Marthad ibn Abdullah al-Yazani, from Aws ibn Aws al-Thaqafi, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: While I was sitting, Jibril (peace be upon him) came to me and carried me and admitted me into the Paradise of my Lord the Mighty and Majestic. While I was sitting, an apple was placed in my hand, and the apple split into two halves, and from it emerged a maiden, than whom I never saw any maiden more beautiful or lovelier, glorifying Allah with a glorification the like of which the first and the last never heard. I said: Who are you, O maiden? She said: I am one of the houris; Allah the Mighty and Majestic created me from the light of His Throne. I said: For whom are you? She said: For the wrongfully killed caliph, Uthman ibn Affan (may Allah be pleased with him)."
"600","24 - Aws ibn Hudhayfah al-Thaqafi (may Allah be pleased with him). Chapter on the excellence of reciting the Quran. 599 - Narrated to us Fudayl ibn Muhammad al-Malti, narrated to us Abu Nuaym, narrated to us Abdullah ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Taifi, (Ha) and narrated to us Muadh ibn al-Muthanna, narrated to us Musaddad, narrated to us Qurran ibn Tamam, (Ha) and narrated to us al-Husayn Ishaq al-Tustari, narrated to us Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah, narrated to us Waki, both said: Narrated to us Abdullah ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Yala al-Taifi, from Uthman ibn Abdullah ibn Aws al-Thaqafi, from his grandfather Aws ibn Hudhayfah, he said: We came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) as the delegation of Thaqif, and he lodged us in a dome of his; our confederate brothers stayed with al-Mughirah ibn Shubah. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to come to us after Isha and talk to us, and most of his talk was complaining of Quraysh, saying: We were in Makkah humiliated and weak, and when we came to Madinah, war went back and forth against us and for us. One night he was late coming to us and stayed long, so we said: O Messenger of Allah, you have been late. He said: My portion of the Quran came upon me, and I disliked going out until I completed it. So we asked the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): How did the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) divide the Quran? They said: He divided it into three, five, seven, nine, eleven, thirteen, and the portion of the Mufassal."
